window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"u": "index.html", "t": "RingDotNet", "h": "", "x": "RingDotNet Call any .NET class from Ring with simple syntax. Build Windows Forms and WPF programs, web servers with ASP.NET Core, and databases with Entity Framework \u2014 all in Ring. Get started New to Ring? See the applications Function index 117 Namespaces 4,681 Wrapped classes 32 Forms samples 10 WPF samples 45 Applications 31 Test files"}, {"u": "index.html#a-first-taste", "t": "RingDotNet", "h": "A first taste", "x": "A first taste # load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text\" } oSB = new StringBuilder(\"Hello\") oSB.Append(\", \").Append(\"Ring!\") ? oSB.ToString() # Hello, Ring! ? oSB.Length # 12 ? new Math().Max(3,9) # 9 ? new Math().Sqrt(144) # 12 The classes are real Ring classes with the same method names as the .NET ones \u2014 like RingQt for Qt \u2014 so code completion, inheritance and ordinary Ring syntax just work. There is no eval and no string-based dispatch in your code."}, {"u": "index.html#what-is-inside", "t": "RingDotNet", "h": "What is inside", "x": "What is inside # \ud83d\udd0c Simple syntax oObj.Method(), oObj.Property = x, enums as strings, events with setClickEvent(). \ud83d\udcda The class library Strings, dates, files, regex, networking, crypto, XML, JSON, reflection, LINQ and more. \ud83d\udd01 Callbacks and events Pass Ring code wherever .NET expects a delegate, as RingQt does with events. \ud83e\ude9f Windows Forms All controls wrapped, plus short helpers: uiForm(), uiButton(), UiCanvas for drawing. \ud83c\udfa8 WPF Windows, XAML, data binding, shapes, brushes and animation. \ud83c\udf10 ASP.NET Core A Kestrel web server whose routes are Ring functions. JSON in one call. \ud83d\uddc4\ufe0f Entity Framework Cor"}, {"u": "index.html#see-it-running", "t": "RingDotNet", "h": "See it running", "x": "See it running #"}, {"u": "index.html#how-the-package-is-organised", "t": "RingDotNet", "h": "How the package is organised", "x": "How the package is organised # Folder Contents bin/ ring_dotnet.dll (the C extension), the managed bridge, ASP.NET and EF assemblies libraries/dotnetlib/ The Ring library: functions, the generated wrapper classes, GUI/WPF/web/data helpers extensions/ringdotnet/ C source of the extension and C# source of the bridge, web server and data layer samples/ 32 Windows Forms samples, 10 WPF samples and console samples applications/ Complete programs samples/UsingDotNet/tests/ Automated tests ( run.sh ) tools/ The class generators ( gendotnet.ring , wrapassembly.ring ) and the manifest Tip New here? Rea"}, {"u": "getting-started.html", "t": "Getting started", "h": "", "x": "New to Ring? The appendix has a Quick introduction to Ring (about 15 minutes); read it first if you have never written a Ring program."}, {"u": "getting-started.html#requirements", "t": "Getting started", "h": "Requirements", "x": "Requirements # You need Why Ring 1.27 or newer The language. The package is a normal Ring package. .NET 6 or newer (tested with .NET 9) The runtime that RingDotNet hosts inside the Ring process. Windows Desktop runtime Only for Windows Forms and WPF programs (it is part of the .NET Desktop runtime installer). ASP.NET Core runtime Only for web programs ( \"RingDotNet.Web\" ). Note The package is built and tested on Windows with Ring 1.28 and .NET 9. The C extension and the managed bridge are portable; the Windows Forms and WPF parts are Windows-only by nature."}, {"u": "getting-started.html#install", "t": "Getting started", "h": "Install", "x": "Install # Install the package with RingPM , the Ring package manager, in one step: ringpm install ringdotnet from ringpackages That is all. The package is one zip file ( extensions/ringdotnet.zip ): RingPM copies it and its setup.ring extracts it into your Ring folder, which is much faster than installing thousands of files one by one. It brings the library, the extension ( ring_dotnet.dll ), the managed assemblies, the samples and the applications, so every program can start with: load \"dotnet.ring\" You do not need to set a path or copy files. To test the installation, run a sample: ring samp"}, {"u": "getting-started.html#linux-and-macos", "t": "Getting started", "h": "Linux and macOS", "x": "Linux and macOS # The package works on Linux (tested on Ubuntu 24.04, x64, with .NET 9) and should work on macOS. The extension is a C library, so it is built once for your system; the managed files are the same on every system and are installed in ring/bin by the package. cd ring/extensions/ringdotnet sh buildgcc.sh # Linux (macOS: sh buildclang.sh); writes libring_dotnet.so to ring/lib The build needs the Ring headers and library ( ring/language/include and ring/lib ). Install the .NET runtime (not only the SDK) with the packages of your system or with the dotnet-install script, and for the "}, {"u": "getting-started.html#your-first-program", "t": "Getting started", "h": "Your first program", "x": "Your first program # # The first program: simple syntax, the namespaces are opened with using load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text\" } oSB = new StringBuilder(\"Hello\") oSB.Append(\", \").Append(\"Ring!\") ? oSB.ToString() ? oSB.Length ? new Math().Max(3,9) ? \"Running on .NET \" + new Environment().Version.ToString() Hello, Ring! 12 9 Running on .NET 9.0.15 The using { } block opens the .NET namespaces, like using System; in C#. After it, every class of those namespaces is a Ring class with the same name: new StringBuilder(\"Hello\") , oSB.Append(...) , oSB.Length , new Math().Max(3,9) . Th"}, {"u": "getting-started.html#the-startup-modes", "t": "Getting started", "h": "The startup modes", "x": "The startup modes # The first .NET call starts the runtime, and the runtime can be started in one mode only. You do not have to choose it: the using { } block looks at the names and starts the mode that the program needs. The block names... Mode that is started Use it for only System... namespaces the base runtime Console programs, files, networking, JSON, XML, reflection, LINQ System.Windows* or System.Drawing* Windows Desktop runtime, Ring thread becomes STA ( dnUseDesktop() ) Windows Forms, WPF Microsoft.AspNetCore* or \"RingDotNet.Web\" ASP.NET Core runtime, loads the web server ( dnUseWeb()"}, {"u": "getting-started.html#importing-namespaces", "t": "Getting started", "h": "Importing namespaces", "x": "Importing namespaces # The wrapper classes are loaded on demand , one namespace at a time, so a program only pays for what it uses. Name the namespaces in a using block, like C#. Each item must be a text; anything else is an error: using { \"System.IO\" # File, Directory, Path, StreamReader ... \"System.Text.RegularExpressions\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" # also loads the GUI helpers (uiForm, uiButton ...) } using is the same as calling dnImport(\"Namespace\") for each text; the function form is handy when the name is computed. If a method returns an object whose class belongs to a namespace you did not"}, {"u": "getting-started.html#your-first-window", "t": "Getting started", "h": "Your first window", "x": "Your first window # # A button that counts its clicks load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } nClicks = 0 oForm = uiForm(\"Click counter\",260,130) oCount = uiLabel(oForm,\"0 clicks\",20,15,220,24) uiButton(oForm,\"Click me\",20,50,100,32,\"clicked()\") uiTest(oForm,\"selfTest()\") uiRun(oForm) uiEnd() # -------------------------------------------------------------------------- func clicked nClicks++ oCount.Text = \"\" + nClicks + \" clicks\" func selfTest oForm.Controls.Item(1).PerformClick() oForm.Controls.Item(1).PerformClick() oForm.Controls.Item(1).PerformClick() dnAssert(\""}, {"u": "getting-started.html#run-the-tests", "t": "Getting started", "h": "Run the tests", "x": "Run the tests # Every feature has a test, and every sample and application can test itself: sh samples/UsingDotNet/tests/run.sh B:/ring/bin/ring.exe # runs tests, samples and applications ring samples/UsingDotNet/applications/Calculator/main.ring --selftest # drives the form and prints SELFTEST OK Read Testing for how the self tests work."}, {"u": "getting-started.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "Getting started", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # Core concepts : how .NET names, overloads, properties, enums and events look in Ring. Windows Forms and the applications . ASP.NET Core and Entity Framework Core for servers and databases. Gotchas & limits before you write a long program."}, {"u": "how-it-works.html", "t": "How it works", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#the-layers", "t": "How it works", "h": "The layers", "x": "The layers # Your Ring program oSB = new StringBuilder(\"Hi\") \u00b7 oForm.Text = \"Hello\" \u00b7 oBtn.setClickEvent(\"...\") dotnetlib (Ring library) generated wrapper classes \u00b7 using \u00b7 dnCallback \u00b7 gui.ring \u00b7 wpf.ring \u00b7 web.ring \u00b7 data.ring ring_dotnet.dll (C extension) starts the runtime through hostfxr \u00b7 sends requests \u00b7 runs Ring callbacks RingDotNet.Bridge.dll (C#): reflection, handle table, type conversion, delegates .NET class library WinForms \u00b7 WPF \u00b7 ASP.NET \u00b7 EF Your DLLs and NuGet packages"}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#the-extension-and-the-bridge", "t": "How it works", "h": "The extension and the bridge", "x": "The extension and the bridge # ring_dotnet.dll is a normal Ring extension written in C. When the first .NET call is made it: finds the installed .NET runtime and starts it inside the Ring process with hostfxr (no separate process, no IPC), loads RingDotNet.Bridge.dll , a small C# assembly, keeps one entry point: dotnet_request(operation, data) . The bridge uses reflection. A Ring call such as oSB.Append(\", \") becomes a request call [handle, \"Append\", \", \"] ; the bridge finds the right overload from the argument types, invokes it, and returns the result."}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#handles", "t": "How it works", "h": "Handles", "x": "Handles # .NET objects never leave .NET. Ring holds a handle (a number) inside a DotNetObject , and the bridge keeps the real object in a table. The generated wrapper classes are DotNetObject s with typed methods, so oSB.Append(...) and oSB.callMethod(\"Append\",[...]) do the same thing."}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#the-wire-format", "t": "How it works", "h": "The wire format", "x": "The wire format # Values travel as a compact tagged text: s5:Hello (string), n2:42 (number), l... (list), o3:17 (object handle), c... (callback), e... (error). Numbers that are not whole travel as the 8 bytes of the double, so there is no loss of precision . Binary data ( byte[] ) travels as a Ring string."}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#generated-wrapper-classes", "t": "How it works", "h": "Generated wrapper classes", "x": "Generated wrapper classes # tools/gendotnet.ring reads the manifest ( tools/dotnetmanifest.ring ) and uses reflection to write one Ring class per .NET class into libraries/dotnetlib/generated/ (two files per namespace: a loader and the classes). These files are plain Ring source with real inheritance, properties (as attributes with getX / setX ), events and enums. using { \"System.IO\" } loads one namespace on demand. A class index lets the library import the right namespace automatically when a returned object has a type that is not loaded yet."}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#results-are-wrapped-by-their-real-type", "t": "How it works", "h": "Results are wrapped by their real type", "x": "Results are wrapped by their real type # When a method returns an object, the bridge sends the list of its type and base types. dnWrapObject looks for the most specific generated class (importing its namespace if needed) and creates it. If no class exists the result is a plain DotNetObject \u2014 use callMethod and getProp on it."}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#callbacks-ring-code-called-from-net", "t": "How it works", "h": "Callbacks: Ring code called from .NET", "x": "Callbacks: Ring code called from .NET # dnCallback(\"...\") passes Ring code where .NET expects a delegate. The bridge builds a delegate of the exact type with a compiled expression; when .NET invokes it, the C extension runs the Ring function on the Ring thread and returns its result. Callbacks run synchronously during a call, which is why events from timers on other threads are ignored (see Gotchas )."}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#threads", "t": "How it works", "h": "Threads", "x": "Threads # Thread What runs there The Ring thread All Ring code, all calls into .NET and all callbacks. With dnUseDesktop() it is an STA thread and runs the message loop in Application.Run() . .NET threads Kestrel connections, tasks, timers. They never call Ring directly. The web server queues requests and Ring handles them in serveOne() ."}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#runtime-modes", "t": "How it works", "h": "Runtime modes", "x": "Runtime modes # The runtime is configured with one of four runtimeconfig.json files: base ( RingDotNet.Bridge ), desktop ( RingDotNet.Desktop ), web ( RingDotNet.Web ) and all ( RingDotNet.All ). dnUseDesktop() , dnUseWeb() and dnUseAll() select the file before the runtime starts. dnUseData() only loads the Entity Framework assemblies; an AssemblyResolve handler in the bridge finds them next to it."}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#packages", "t": "How it works", "h": "Packages", "x": "Packages # File Role ring_dotnet.dll C extension RingDotNet.Bridge.dll reflection bridge RingDotNet.Web.dll request queue and Kestrel host RingDotNet.Data.dll RingDb and RingTypedDb (Entity Framework) Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore*.dll , e_sqlite3.dll ... Entity Framework Core and SQLite"}, {"u": "tutorials.html", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tutorials.html#how-to-use-the-tutorials", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "How to use the tutorials", "x": "How to use the tutorials # Every tutorial has a program in the folder docs/tutorials . Read the explanation, run the program, change it, and do the exercises at the end. The programs are real: they are tested together with the rest of the package, so the output you see here is the output you will get. cd docs/tutorials ring t01_first_look.ring # a console tutorial ring g01c_counter.ring # a window ring g01c_counter.ring --selftest # the same program checks itself and closes Tip You do not need to know .NET to start. If you know Ring, you can follow every tutorial. The first two pages explain t"}, {"u": "tutorials.html#start-here", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "Start here", "x": "Start here # \ud83e\udded .NET for Ring programmers What .NET is, and the words you will meet: runtime, class library, assembly, namespace, class, object, static, property, method, event, struct, enum, null. \ud83d\udcd6 Reading the .NET documentation How to find a class on Microsoft Learn and turn what you read into Ring code."}, {"u": "tutorials.html#part-1-the-class-library-console-programs", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "Part 1 \u2014 The class library (console programs)", "x": "Part 1 \u2014 The class library (console programs) # 1 A first look Objects, types, properties, methods, static members, structs, collections and null. 2 Working with text Ring's own text functions and what .NET adds: Join, Format, StringBuilder, regular expressions, Base64, encodings. 3 Numbers, dates and times Math, parsing safely, big numbers, random numbers, DateTime, TimeSpan and cultures. 4 Collections and LINQ Lists, dictionaries, sets, queues, stacks, sorting and asking questions with LINQ. 5 Files and folders Path, Directory, File, readers and writers, file information and zip. 6 JSON and "}, {"u": "tutorials.html#part-2-programs-with-windows-web-and-data", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "Part 2 \u2014 Programs with windows, web and data", "x": "Part 2 \u2014 Programs with windows, web and data # 11 Your first window A form, a label, a button, an event \u2014 with and without the helper functions. 12 A form with many controls Text boxes, number box, combo box, check box, list box and validation. 13 A small text editor Menus, a status bar, open and save, dock layout. 14 Drawing and animation A canvas, a timer and the keyboard: a bouncing ball and a paddle. 15 WPF XAML windows, finding elements, events and data binding. 16 A web service with a database JSON over HTTP, stored with Entity Framework. 17 Databases step by step Tables, rows, searches,"}, {"u": "tutorials.html#part-3-going-further", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "Part 3 \u2014 Going further", "x": "Part 3 \u2014 Going further # 19 Callbacks and events Give Ring code to .NET: function names, code texts, events, errors and the thread rule. 20 Exploring .NET with reflection Ask a class what it can do: properties, methods, parameters, enums. 21 Project: a log analyzer Files, regular expressions, dictionaries, LINQ and a JSON report in one program. 22 Images Draw, read and change pixels, make a gray copy, resize and save PNG files. 23 Layout and richer controls Split bars, tabs, table layout, and a data grid with a filter. 24 Project: a to-do list A complete window application that remembers its t"}, {"u": "tutorials.html#part-4-more-lessons-and-projects", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "Part 4 \u2014 More lessons and projects", "x": "Part 4 \u2014 More lessons and projects # 29 Regular expressions in depth Patterns, groups, replace, split, greedy and lazy, validation. 30 Tabular data CSV, DataTable, DataView, Compute, sorting, filtering and grouping. 31 Project: duplicate files Folders, content hashes and a dictionary find the copies. 32 Settings Defaults, a JSON file, special folders, and a damaged file. 33 Cultures Formatting, parsing, sorting and case in different languages. 34 Timers and progress A stopwatch, a progress bar, and a long job in small steps. 35 Project: Tic-Tac-Toe Rules and state in testable functions, nine b"}, {"u": "tutorials.html#part-5-even-more", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "Part 5 \u2014 Even more", "x": "Part 5 \u2014 Even more # 40 Your own dialog A modal form that asks for data, validates it and returns it; tested with a timer. 41 Menus, toolbars, shortcuts Write each action once and reach it from a menu, a toolbar, a context menu and a key. 42 A folder browser TreeView, ListView, recursion and the Tag property: a small Explorer. 43 Calendars and dates Month tables, ages, business days, birthdays and safe date arithmetic. 44 Statistics and simulation Mean, median, mode, deviation, primes, gcd and a Monte Carlo estimate of pi. 45 Binary files BinaryWriter and BinaryReader, a file format, a hex dum"}, {"u": "tutorials.html#learning-path", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "Learning path", "x": "Learning path # If you want to... Read understand the vocabulary .NET for Ring programmers , Reading the .NET documentation process data and files tutorials 2\u20136 write programs that do not crash tutorial 7, then 8 talk to other computers tutorial 9, then 16 build desktop programs tutorials 11\u201315 store data tutorial 17 use code that others wrote in C# tutorial 18 give Ring code to .NET, explore classes tutorials 19, 20 bigger projects tutorials 21, 24 images and richer windows tutorials 22, 23 network programs and web sites tutorials 25, 26 more databases and testing tutorials 27, 28 text patter"}, {"u": "tut-concepts.html", "t": ".NET for Ring programmers", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-concepts.html#what-is-net", "t": ".NET for Ring programmers", "h": "What is .NET?", "x": "What is .NET? # .NET is a large collection of ready-made code from Microsoft, plus the program that runs it. You do not write that code \u2014 you use it. Instead of writing your own function to read a file, compress a folder, hash a password or talk to a web server, you ask .NET to do it. RingDotNet lets a Ring program ask. Two things make up .NET: Part What it is Analogy The runtime The engine that loads and runs .NET code. RingDotNet starts it inside your Ring program. The Ring virtual machine The class library Thousands of classes: text, numbers, files, networking, windows, databases... Ring's "}, {"u": "tut-concepts.html#classes-and-objects", "t": ".NET for Ring programmers", "h": "Classes and objects", "x": "Classes and objects # Almost everything in .NET is an object . An object has data and abilities ; the class is the description that the objects are made from \u2014 like a recipe and the cakes baked from it. using { \"System.Text\" } oText = new StringBuilder(\"Hello\") # create an object of the class StringBuilder You already know this from Ring: Ring has classes and objects too ( class , new ). The difference is that these classes were written by Microsoft, in C#, and live in .NET."}, {"u": "tut-concepts.html#properties-methods-and-events", "t": ".NET for Ring programmers", "h": "Properties, methods and events", "x": "Properties, methods and events # An object has three kinds of members: Member What it is In Ring Example Property something the object knows (a value you read or change) oObject.Name and oObject.Name = value oForm.Text = \"Hello\" Method something the object can do oObject.Name(arguments) oText.Append(\"!\") Event something that happens , to which you react oObject.setNameEvent(\"code\") oButton.setClickEvent(\"clicked()\")"}, {"u": "tut-concepts.html#static-members-no-object-needed", "t": ".NET for Ring programmers", "h": "Static members: no object needed", "x": "Static members: no object needed # Some classes are just a toolbox of functions. Their members are static : you call them on the class, without creating an object. Math is the classic example: you never create \"a math\". using { \"System\" } ? new Math().Max(3,9) # a static method (Ring creates a harmless helper object to call it) ? new Math().PI # a static property In the class reference a static member is marked static ."}, {"u": "tut-concepts.html#namespaces-and-assemblies", "t": ".NET for Ring programmers", "h": "Namespaces and assemblies", "x": "Namespaces and assemblies # .NET has thousands of classes, so they are organised: A namespace is a folder of classes with a dotted name: System.IO holds the file classes, System.Text holds the text classes, System.Windows.Forms holds the window classes. An assembly is a file (a .dll ) that contains the compiled classes. You rarely need to think about it. In Ring you bring a namespace in with using : using { \"System.IO\" \"System.Text\" } After that the classes of that namespace are available by their short name: File , Path , StringBuilder ."}, {"u": "tut-concepts.html#value-types-structs-and-enums", "t": ".NET for Ring programmers", "h": "Value types: structs and enums", "x": "Value types: structs and enums # Most types are classes. Two kinds of types are special: A struct is a small value : DateTime (a date and time), TimeSpan (a duration), Point (x and y). You use it like an object, but copying it copies the value. An enum is a list of named choices: the day of the week ( Monday , Tuesday ...), a window state ( Normal , Maximized ). In Ring an enum value is a text : oForm.WindowState = \"Maximized\" ."}, {"u": "tut-concepts.html#null", "t": ".NET for Ring programmers", "h": "null", "x": "null # null means \"no object\". A method that finds nothing may return null. In Ring dnNull() is the .NET null, and an object you got from .NET can be asked oObject.isNull() ."}, {"u": "tut-concepts.html#exceptions", "t": ".NET for Ring programmers", "h": "Exceptions", "x": "Exceptions # When something goes wrong \u2014 the file does not exist, the text is not a number \u2014 .NET raises an exception . In Ring an exception is a normal Ring error, and you catch it with try ... catch . Tutorial 7 explains this."}, {"u": "tut-concepts.html#generics-and-collections", "t": ".NET for Ring programmers", "h": "Generics and collections", "x": "Generics and collections # A collection holds many items. .NET has a list ( List ), a dictionary ( Dictionary ), a set, a queue and a stack. They are generic : the same class works for any type of item ( List<string> , List<int> ). RingDotNet gives you ready names for the common ones: StringList , NumberList , StringDictionary ... Tutorial 4 shows them."}, {"u": "tut-concepts.html#the-words-around-net", "t": ".NET for Ring programmers", "h": "The words around .NET", "x": "The words around .NET # Word Meaning C# The main language that .NET code is written in. You do not need to learn it to use .NET from Ring, but the documentation shows C# examples. SDK The software development kit : the compiler and tools for writing .NET programs. You only need it to build C# code ( tutorial 18 ). Runtime What is needed to run .NET programs. RingDotNet needs the runtime (and the desktop or ASP.NET runtime for windows and web). NuGet The package store of .NET. A NuGet package is a set of .dll files that you can wrap and use. BCL The base class library : the part of .NET that ev"}, {"u": "tut-concepts.html#how-ring-and-net-meet", "t": ".NET for Ring programmers", "h": "How Ring and .NET meet", "x": "How Ring and .NET meet # Ring and .NET are two different systems. RingDotNet is the bridge between them: A Ring text becomes a .NET string, a Ring number becomes a .NET number, a Ring list becomes an array. A .NET object comes back to Ring as a Ring object with the same name as the .NET class. A .NET enum is a text, a .NET bool is 1 or 0 . Ring code runs on one thread ; .NET may use many. Code that .NET wants to call back into Ring runs on the Ring thread. Read How it works when you want the details."}, {"u": "tut-concepts.html#next", "t": ".NET for Ring programmers", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Reading the .NET documentation shows how to find a class on Microsoft Learn and use it from Ring."}, {"u": "tut-reading-docs.html", "t": "Reading the .NET documentation", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-reading-docs.html#where-the-documentation-is", "t": "Reading the .NET documentation", "h": "Where the documentation is", "x": "Where the documentation is # The official reference is Microsoft Learn \u2192 .NET API browser (search for \"learn.microsoft.com dotnet api\"). Every class has a page. For example the page of System.Text.StringBuilder lists its constructors, properties, methods and events, with a short description and a C# example for each. RingDotNet also has its own class reference with the same information written for Ring: every member shows how to call it from Ring . Use it first; use Microsoft Learn when you want the long explanation."}, {"u": "tut-reading-docs.html#how-to-find-the-class-you-need", "t": "Reading the .NET documentation", "h": "How to find the class you need", "x": "How to find the class you need # Think about the job : \"read a file\" \u2192 files; \"split a date\" \u2192 dates; \"send a web request\" \u2192 HTTP. Search by words in the search box of this site (press / ) or on Microsoft Learn: read text file C# . The answer almost always names a class: File , StreamReader ... Look at the namespace shown on the page title: System.IO.File means the namespace is System.IO . Check that the class is wrapped : open api/System.IO.html in the class reference , or search the class name."}, {"u": "tut-reading-docs.html#reading-a-class-page", "t": "Reading the .NET documentation", "h": "Reading a class page", "x": "Reading a class page # A Microsoft page for a method looks like this (shortened): File.ReadAllText(String) \u2192 String File.ReadAllText(String, Encoding) \u2192 String and a C# example: string text = File.ReadAllText(@\"C:\\notes.txt\"); Console.WriteLine(text); From C# to Ring # In C# In Ring using System.IO; using { \"System.IO\" } var f = new FileInfo(path); oFile = new FileInfo(cPath) File.ReadAllText(path) new File().ReadAllText(cPath) (a static method is called on new File() ) text.Length (a property) oText.Length list.Add(\"x\") (a method) oList.Add(\"x\") button.Click += handler; (an event) oButton.set"}, {"u": "tut-reading-docs.html#a-complete-example", "t": "Reading the .NET documentation", "h": "A complete example", "x": "A complete example # Task: \"get the file name from a path\". Search \u2192 Path.GetFileName in System.IO . The documentation says: public static string GetFileName(string path) . It is static , so on a new Path() object, and it takes one text: using { \"System.IO\" } oPath = new Path() ? oPath.GetFileName(\"c:/work/report.txt\") # report.txt"}, {"u": "tut-reading-docs.html#when-a-class-is-not-wrapped", "t": "Reading the .NET documentation", "h": "When a class is not wrapped", "x": "When a class is not wrapped # Everything in the base library and in Windows Forms, WPF, ASP.NET and Entity Framework is wrapped. Other packages can be wrapped in one command: Your own assemblies . Without wrapping, dnType(\"Namespace.Type\") and dnNew(...) still reach any .NET type: Core functions ."}, {"u": "tut-reading-docs.html#next", "t": "Reading the .NET documentation", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Start the first lesson: A first look ."}, {"u": "tut-01-first-look.html", "t": "Tutorial 1: A first look", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-01-first-look.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 1: A first look", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # how to start using .NET from Ring, what a type , an object , a property , a method and a static member look like, how a struct and a collection look."}, {"u": "tut-01-first-look.html#before-you-start", "t": "Tutorial 1: A first look", "h": "Before you start", "x": "Before you start # You need Ring and the RingDotNet package. Open a terminal in docs/tutorials . If something does not work, read Getting started . If Ring is new to you, read the Quick introduction to Ring in the appendix first."}, {"u": "tut-01-first-look.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 1: A first look", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # Read the program first; the explanations follow. The line using { ... } brings classes in. func main is where Ring starts. # Tutorial 1: a first look at .NET from Ring - runtime, types, objects, namespaces load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text\" \"System.Collections.Generic\" } func main # 1. The runtime that runs inside your Ring program ? \"Runtime : .NET \" + new Environment().Version.ToString() # 2. Everything in .NET is an object of some type (a class) oText = new StringBuilder(\"Hello\") oType = oText.GetType() ? \"Type of the object: \" + oType.FullName ? \"Namespace : \" "}, {"u": "tut-01-first-look.html#how-it-works-step-by-step", "t": "Tutorial 1: A first look", "h": "How it works, step by step", "x": "How it works, step by step # 1. The runtime # new Environment().Version asks the .NET runtime that runs inside your Ring program for its version ( Environment is a class of the System namespace that the using block opened). Nothing is installed \"next to\" Ring: the runtime is part of your program while it runs. 2. Everything is an object of some type # new StringBuilder(\"Hello\") creates an object. GetType() is a method that every .NET object has; it returns an object that describes the type. Its properties FullName , Namespace and IsClass tell you what you are holding: a class called System.Tex"}, {"u": "tut-01-first-look.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 1: A first look", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Idea Example a runtime inside your program new Environment().Version create an object new StringBuilder(\"Hello\") a property oText.Length a method oText.Append(\", .NET\") a static member new Math().Max(3,9) a struct and an enum DateTime , DayOfWeek a collection StringList no object dnNull()"}, {"u": "tut-01-first-look.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 1: A first look", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Change the program to print the type of a DateTime object. Which namespace is it in? Create a StringBuilder and append your name five times with a loop. What is its Length ? Use new Math() to print the square root of 2 and the larger of 7 and 19 . Find Environment.MachineName in the class reference (namespace System ) and print it."}, {"u": "tut-01-first-look.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 1: A first look", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 2: Working with text ."}, {"u": "tut-02-text.html", "t": "Tutorial 2: Working with text", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-02-text.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 2: Working with text", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # which text jobs Ring does by itself and which need .NET, String helpers ( Join , Format , Compare , IsNullOrWhiteSpace ), StringBuilder for long texts, regular expressions with Regex , Base64 and UTF-8."}, {"u": "tut-02-text.html#texts-between-ring-and-net", "t": "Tutorial 2: Working with text", "h": "Texts between Ring and .NET", "x": "Texts between Ring and .NET # A Ring text is a .NET string as soon as you pass it to a .NET method. A .NET method that returns a string gives you a Ring text. One rule is important: a .NET string is not an object in Ring , it is just a Ring text. That means you cannot call a .NET instance method such as Trim() on it. For those jobs Ring has its own functions: trim() , upper() , lower() , substr() , left() . Use Ring's functions for the basics , and .NET for what Ring does not have. The static methods of the class String ( Join , Format ...) work fine because they take the text as an argument."}, {"u": "tut-02-text.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 2: Working with text", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 2: working with text - Ring text functions, String helpers, StringBuilder, regular expressions, encodings load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text\" \"System.Text.RegularExpressions\" \"System.Globalization\" } func main # Ring already has many text functions: use them for the basics cSentence = \" The quick brown fox \" ? \"Ring trim/upper : [\" + upper(trim(cSentence)) + \"]\" aWords = str2list(substr(trim(cSentence),\" \",nl)) ? \"Ring words : \" + len(aWords) # .NET adds helpers that Ring does not have. String's static methods: oString = new String() ? \"Join : \" + oString"}, {"u": "tut-02-text.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 2: Working with text", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Ring first # upper(trim(cSentence)) is Ring. The words are found by turning spaces into new lines and reading each line as an item: str2list(substr(cSentence,\" \",nl)) . String's static helpers # Call Result oString.Join(\"-\",aWords) all the words with - between them oString.Format([ :format = \"{0} has {1} items\", :arg0 = \"The list\", :arg1 = 3 ]) {0} , {1} are replaced by the arguments oString.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(\" \") true for empty or blank text oString.Compare(\"apple\",\"banana\") -1 if the first sorts before the second, 0 if equal, 1 if after String.Format exists in several forms, "}, {"u": "tut-02-text.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 2: Working with text", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Use Ring's own functions for basic text work; call String 's static helpers when you need Join , Format , Compare . StringBuilder builds long texts efficiently. Regex finds, tests and replaces patterns. Text, bytes and encodings are different things."}, {"u": "tut-02-text.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 2: Working with text", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Use Regex to find every number in \"I have 3 cats and 12 fish\" . (Pattern: [0-9]+ .) Use String.Format with a list of named values to print Ann is 31 years old from the values \"Ann\" and 31 . Write a function isEmail(cText) that returns true when Regex.IsMatch says the text looks like name@site.com . Encode your name to Base64 with Convert.ToBase64String , then decode it with Convert.FromBase64String ."}, {"u": "tut-02-text.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 2: Working with text", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 3: Numbers, dates and times ."}, {"u": "tut-03-numbers-dates.html", "t": "Tutorial 3: Numbers, dates and times", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-03-numbers-dates.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 3: Numbers, dates and times", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # Math for rounding, roots and powers, TryParse : turn text into a number safely, BigInteger : numbers without a limit, Random , DateTime , TimeSpan , parsing dates and formatting in different cultures."}, {"u": "tut-03-numbers-dates.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 3: Numbers, dates and times", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 3: numbers, parsing, dates and times load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Numerics\" \"System.Globalization\" } func main oMath = new Math() # Math: functions on numbers ? \"Round to 2 digits : \" + oMath.Round([ :value = 3.14159, :digits = 2 ]) ? \"Square root of 144: \" + oMath.Sqrt(144) ? \"Power 2^10 : \" + oMath.Pow(2,10) ? \"Absolute, floor : \" + oMath.Abs(-7.5) + \", \" + oMath.Floor(7.9) ? \"Min and Max : \" + oMath.Min(4,9) + \", \" + oMath.Max(4,9) # Converting text to numbers: TryParse never fails, it tells you whether it worked aResult = new Int32().TryParse(\"123\") "}, {"u": "tut-03-numbers-dates.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 3: Numbers, dates and times", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Math # Math is a class of static functions: Round , Sqrt , Pow , Abs , Floor , Min , Max . Round has several versions (round to a whole number, round to some digits), so the program uses named arguments : Round([ :value = 3.14159, :digits = 2 ]) . The names come from the .NET documentation, and the order does not matter. TryParse: no crash # Int32.Parse(\"abc\") raises an error. TryParse never does: it returns a list [worked, value] . A method that has an out parameter in C# returns its results as a list in Ring: first the return value (here true or false), then the out values. aR"}, {"u": "tut-03-numbers-dates.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 3: Numbers, dates and times", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Math functions and named arguments, TryParse for safe conversion, BigInteger for exact big numbers, dates are values that you copy, never change, format patterns and cultures."}, {"u": "tut-03-numbers-dates.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 3: Numbers, dates and times", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Print how many days are left until the end of this year. Parse the text \"15/03/2024\" with ParseExact and a format of dd/MM/yyyy . Compute 100! with BigInteger and print how many digits it has. Throw a die 10 times with Random and count how many sixes you get. Print today's date in three cultures ( en-US , fr-FR , ja-JP )."}, {"u": "tut-03-numbers-dates.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 3: Numbers, dates and times", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 4: Collections and LINQ ."}, {"u": "tut-04-collections.html", "t": "Tutorial 4: Collections and LINQ", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-04-collections.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 4: Collections and LINQ", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the five common collections and when to use each, how to get a collection's items back into a Ring list, LINQ: filter, sort and summarise with a few calls."}, {"u": "tut-04-collections.html#which-collection", "t": "Tutorial 4: Collections and LINQ", "h": "Which collection?", "x": "Which collection? # Collection Use it when... Ring class List you need items in order, and to find them by position StringList , NumberList , ObjectList Dictionary you look items up by a key (a name, an id) StringDictionary , NumberDictionary , ObjectDictionary Set each value may appear only once StringSet Queue first in, first out (a line of customers) StringQueue Stack last in, first out (undo) StringStack Ring lists are flexible and fine for most programs. Use .NET collections when you need their special behaviour (a set, a queue), speed with big data, or when a .NET method asks for one."}, {"u": "tut-04-collections.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 4: Collections and LINQ", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 4: collections and LINQ - lists, dictionaries, sets, queues, sorting and querying load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Collections.Generic\" \"System.Linq\" } func main # A list keeps items in order oFruit = new StringList() oFruit.Add(\"banana\") oFruit.Add(\"apple\") oFruit.Add(\"cherry\") ? \"Count : \" + oFruit.Count ? \"Second item : \" + oFruit.Item(1) oFruit.Sort(NULL) ? \"After Sort : \" + list2str(oFruit.toList()) ? \"Contains apple : \" + oFruit.Contains(\"apple\") ? \"Index of cherry : \" + oFruit.IndexOf(\"cherry\") # A dictionary maps keys to values oAges = new NumberDictionary() "}, {"u": "tut-04-collections.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 4: Collections and LINQ", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # A list # Add adds, Count counts, Item(1) reads by position (the first item is position 0 ), Sort(NULL) sorts in place, Contains and IndexOf search. toList() copies the items into a Ring list, so you can use for ... in . Note Sort(NULL) : Sort has several versions (with a comparer, with a range...). NULL means \"the version without parameters\". A dictionary # SetItem(\"Ann\",31) stores a value under a key; Item(\"Ann\") reads it. TryGetValue(\"Cy\") asks without failing when the key is missing, and returns the list [found, value] . A set # Adding \"red\" twice leaves one \"red\" . Sets also"}, {"u": "tut-04-collections.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 4: Collections and LINQ", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # choose the collection by the question you ask of it, toList() brings a .NET collection to Ring, TryGetValue and NULL for methods with several versions, LINQ answers questions in one chain."}, {"u": "tut-04-collections.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 4: Collections and LINQ", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Put the numbers 5, 3, 8, 3, 1 into a NumberList , sort it, and print it. Count how many times each word appears in a sentence with a NumberDictionary . Use a StringStack to reverse the letters of a word. With LINQ: from [3,8,12,5,20] get the numbers above 4, doubled ( Select ), and their sum."}, {"u": "tut-04-collections.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 4: Collections and LINQ", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 5: Files and folders ."}, {"u": "tut-05-files.html", "t": "Tutorial 5: Files and folders", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-05-files.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 5: Files and folders", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # why you use Path.Combine , reading a whole file, line by line, and writing and appending, information about a file, listing a folder, copying, zipping, cleaning up."}, {"u": "tut-05-files.html#the-classes", "t": "Tutorial 5: Files and folders", "h": "The classes", "x": "The classes # Class Job Path work with names of files and folders (no disk access) File read, write, copy, delete a file in one call Directory create, delete, list folders FileInfo information about one file: size, name, dates StreamReader / StreamWriter read or write a file piece by piece (big files) ZipFile ( System.IO.Compression ) make and open zip files"}, {"u": "tut-05-files.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 5: Files and folders", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 5: files and folders - Path, Directory, File, reading and writing, zip load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.IO.Compression\" } func main oPath = new Path() oFile = new File() oDirectory = new Directory() # Never build paths by joining text: Path.Combine uses the right separator cFolder = oPath.Combine([oPath.GetTempPath(),\"ringdotnet_tutorial5\"]) if oDirectory.Exists(cFolder) oDirectory.Delete([ :path = cFolder, :recursive = True ]) ok oDirectory.CreateDirectory(cFolder) ? \"Folder created : \" + oDirectory.Exists(cFolder) # Write a text file and read i"}, {"u": "tut-05-files.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 5: Files and folders", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Paths # Never build a path by joining texts with / or \\ . Path.Combine([a,b]) uses the right separator for the computer, and Path.GetTempPath() gives a folder where temporary files are allowed. Other Path helpers: GetFileName , GetExtension , GetFileNameWithoutExtension , GetDirectoryName . They only work on the text of a path; the file does not have to exist. Ask before you act # Directory.Exists(cFolder) and File.Exists(cFile) return true or false. Directory.Delete([ :path = cFolder, :recursive = True ]) deletes a folder and everything in it. True for recursive is the named ar"}, {"u": "tut-05-files.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 5: Files and folders", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # build paths with Path , not with + , ask Exists before you read, use a reader for big files and always close it, FileInfo gives the facts about a file."}, {"u": "tut-05-files.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 5: Files and folders", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write a program that counts the lines in every .ring file of a folder ( Directory.GetFiles(folder, \"*.ring\") ). Copy a file to the Documents folder ( Environment.GetFolderPath(\"MyDocuments\") ). Find the biggest file in a folder with FileInfo.Length . Write a log: each run appends a line with the date and time."}, {"u": "tut-05-files.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 5: Files and folders", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 6: JSON and XML ."}, {"u": "tut-06-json-xml.html", "t": "Tutorial 6: JSON and XML", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-06-json-xml.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 6: JSON and XML", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what JSON and XML are, reading a JSON document and its nested values, loading XML, looping over elements, XPath queries, adding an element."}, {"u": "tut-06-json-xml.html#json-and-xml-in-one-minute", "t": "Tutorial 6: JSON and XML", "h": "JSON and XML in one minute", "x": "JSON and XML in one minute # JSON looks like Ring lists and objects: {\"name\":\"Ring\",\"year\":2016,\"tags\":[\"fast\",\"small\"],\"author\":{\"first\":\"Mahmoud\"}} { } an object of properties , [ ] an array, texts in quotes, numbers without. XML is a tree of elements with attributes : <book id=\"1\" year=\"1999\"><title>Learn Ring</title></book> JSON is lighter and used by web services; XML is older and used in files and configuration."}, {"u": "tut-06-json-xml.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 6: JSON and XML", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 6: JSON and XML - reading, writing and searching structured data load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Text.Json\" \"System.Xml\" } func main # JSON: turn Ring data into text oJson = new JsonSerializer() ? \"A Ring list as JSON : \" + oJson.Serialize([1,\"two\",[3,4]]) # JSON: read a document you received cJson = '{\"name\":\"Ring\",\"year\":2016,\"tags\":[\"fast\",\"small\"],\"author\":{\"first\":\"Mahmoud\",\"country\":\"Egypt\"}}' oDocument = new JsonDocument().Parse(cJson) oRoot = oDocument.RootElement ? \"The name : \" + oRoot.GetProperty(\"name\").GetString() ? \"The year : \" + oRoot.GetProperty(\"ye"}, {"u": "tut-06-json-xml.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 6: JSON and XML", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # JSON: write # JsonSerializer.Serialize(aList) turns a Ring list into JSON text: a list of values becomes an array , nested lists become nested arrays. (To make a JSON object from [name, value] pairs, the web library has webJson(...) : see tutorial 16 .) JSON: read # JsonDocument.Parse(text) reads a JSON text. RootElement is the top value. From an element you: Want Call a property of an object GetProperty(\"name\") an item of an array Item(0) how many items GetArrayLength() the value as text / number GetString() / GetInt32() Chain them to go deep: oRoot.GetProperty(\"author\").GetPro"}, {"u": "tut-06-json-xml.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 6: JSON and XML", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # JSON for web data, XML for documents, read JSON by following properties, XPath asks questions of XML."}, {"u": "tut-06-json-xml.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 6: JSON and XML", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Read a JSON text with an array of three numbers and print their sum. Find the cheapest book in the XML of the program with XPath and a loop. Save the changed XML to a file with oDoc.Save(cPath) and read it back. Turn the Ring list [1,[2,3],\"four\"] into JSON and read it back with JsonDocument ."}, {"u": "tut-06-json-xml.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 6: JSON and XML", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 7: When things go wrong ."}, {"u": "tut-07-errors.html", "t": "Tutorial 7: When things go wrong", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-07-errors.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 7: When things go wrong", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what an exception is and how Ring shows it, try / catch and reading the error text, asking first ( Exists , TryParse ) instead of catching, raising your own errors, cleaning up even when something fails."}, {"u": "tut-07-errors.html#exceptions", "t": "Tutorial 7: When things go wrong", "h": "Exceptions", "x": "Exceptions # When .NET cannot do what you asked, it throws an exception : it stops the normal flow and reports the problem. In Ring a .NET exception is a Ring error. Without try , it ends your program with a message. With try ... catch you decide what happens. try new Int32().Parse(\"abc\") catch ? cCatchError done cCatchError holds the message. It starts with DotNet Error: followed by the name of the exception ( System.FormatException ) and an explanation. The name tells you the kind of problem."}, {"u": "tut-07-errors.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 7: When things go wrong", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 7: when things go wrong - exceptions, try/catch, checking before you act load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" } func main # A .NET exception becomes a Ring error you can catch try new Int32().Parse(\"abc\") ? \"this line is never reached\" catch ? \"1. Caught the error:\" ? \" \" + cCatchError done # The text of the error names the .NET exception type: you can react to the kind of error try new File().ReadAllText(\"c:\\this\\file\\does\\not\\exist.txt\") catch if substr(cCatchError,\"FileNotFoundException\") > 0 or substr(cCatchError,\"DirectoryNotFoundException\") > 0 ? \"2. T"}, {"u": "tut-07-errors.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 7: When things go wrong", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # 1. Catch the error # The first block shows the message: System.FormatException: The input string 'abc' was not in a correct format. 2. React to the kind of error # substr(cCatchError,\"FileNotFoundException\") > 0 asks if the name appears in the text. Different problems get different answers: a missing file may be told to the user; a full disk may stop the program. 3. Ask first # Catching is for the unexpected. If you can check a condition, do that: File.Exists(cName) returns false instead of raising an error. It is shorter, faster and clearer. 4. TryParse # The same idea for numb"}, {"u": "tut-07-errors.html#good-habits", "t": "Tutorial 7: When things go wrong", "h": "Good habits", "x": "Good habits # Do Do not check with Exists , TryParse , IsNullOrWhiteSpace first wrap the whole program in one try catch where you can do something about it catch and ignore show a friendly message, log the technical one show cCatchError to end users close what you open leave files open"}, {"u": "tut-07-errors.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 7: When things go wrong", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write readNumber(cText) that returns the number, or -1 if the text is not a number, using TryParse . Read a file that may not exist: print missing if it does not, the number of lines if it does. Do it twice: once with Exists , once with try . Write divide(a,b) that raises \"Division by zero\" when b is 0, and catch it. Cause a DirectoryNotFoundException : write a file into a folder that does not exist. Read the message."}, {"u": "tut-07-errors.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 7: When things go wrong", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 8: Hashing and encryption ."}, {"u": "tut-08-security.html", "t": "Tutorial 8: Hashing and encryption", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-08-security.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 8: Hashing and encryption", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the difference between hashing and encryption , SHA-256 hashes, storing passwords correctly with a salt and many rounds (PBKDF2), HMAC and random tokens, encrypting and decrypting text with AES. Warning This is a first look at the ideas, not a security course. For real systems use the standard libraries as shown here, never invent your own scheme, and keep keys out of your source code."}, {"u": "tut-08-security.html#hashing-and-encryption", "t": "Tutorial 8: Hashing and encryption", "h": "Hashing and encryption", "x": "Hashing and encryption # Hashing Encryption Direction one way: you cannot get the data back two ways: you can decrypt with the key Result size always the same (SHA-256: 32 bytes) about the size of the data Use check that data is the same; store passwords keep data secret A hash is a fingerprint. The same input always gives the same hash; a tiny change gives a completely different hash."}, {"u": "tut-08-security.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 8: Hashing and encryption", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 8: hashing and encryption basics - checksums, password hashes, random tokens, AES load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Security.Cryptography\" \"System.Text\" } func main oConvert = new Convert() # A hash is a fingerprint of data: the same input always gives the same hash, you can not go back ? \"SHA-256 of 'abc' : \" + lower(oConvert.ToHexString(new SHA256().HashData(\"abc\"))) ? \"MD5 of 'abc' : \" + lower(oConvert.ToHexString(new MD5().HashData(\"abc\"))) + \" (old: do not use it for security)\" ? \"Changing one letter: \" + left(lower(oConvert.ToHexString(new SHA256().Hash"}, {"u": "tut-08-security.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 8: Hashing and encryption", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # SHA-256 and MD5 # new SHA256().HashData(\"abc\") returns the hash bytes; Convert.ToHexString writes them as hexadecimal. SHA-256 is the modern standard. MD5 is broken for security : it is only fine for a quick checksum of a download. Passwords # Never store a password, and never store a plain hash of it either (attackers have tables of hashes of common passwords). Store a salted, slow hash : a salt is random data mixed in, so two people with the same password get different hashes, many rounds ( iterations ) make each guess slow for an attacker. Rfc2898DeriveBytes (PBKDF2) does bot"}, {"u": "tut-08-security.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 8: Hashing and encryption", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # hashing is one way, encryption is two ways, a password hash needs a salt and many rounds, RandomNumberGenerator for secrets, NULL selects a method's version without parameters."}, {"u": "tut-08-security.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 8: Hashing and encryption", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write checksum(cFile) that returns the SHA-256 of a file's bytes ( File.ReadAllBytes ). Make a tiny \"user store\": a dictionary of user \u2192 [salt, hash] with register and login functions. Compute the HMAC of a message with two different keys. Are the results different? Encrypt a file's text with AES and write the cipher as Base64 text."}, {"u": "tut-08-security.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 8: Hashing and encryption", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 9: HTTP ."}, {"u": "tut-09-http.html", "t": "Tutorial 9: HTTP", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-09-http.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 9: HTTP", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what HTTP requests and responses are, a web server whose addresses are Ring functions, a web client with HttpClient , why requests are asynchronous and what a Task is, status codes."}, {"u": "tut-09-http.html#http-in-one-minute", "t": "Tutorial 9: HTTP", "h": "HTTP in one minute", "x": "HTTP in one minute # A client sends a request : a method ( GET to read, POST to send data, DELETE to remove), an address , optional data. The server sends a response : a status code ( 200 OK, 201 Created, 404 Not Found, 500 error) and a body (text, HTML or JSON)."}, {"u": "tut-09-http.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 9: HTTP", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 9: HTTP - a web server and a web client in one program load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Net.Http\" \"System.Threading.Tasks\" \"RingDotNet.Web\" } oServer = \"\" oClient = \"\" cUrl = \"\" func main # 1. A web server: a Ring function answers each address oServer = new WebServer(\"http://127.0.0.1:0\") # port 0: Windows picks a free port oServer.onGet(\"/hello\",\"hello\") oServer.onGet(\"/time\",\"clock\") oServer.onPost(\"/echo\",\"echo\") cUrl = oServer.url() ? \"The server listens at \" + cUrl # 2. A web client: HttpClient sends requests. Asking is asynchronous: it returns a Task oClient = "}, {"u": "tut-09-http.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 9: HTTP", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The server # using { ..., \"RingDotNet.Web\" } (first!) starts the ASP.NET Core runtime. new WebServer(\"http://127.0.0.1:0\") starts a server on your computer ( 127.0.0.1 is \"this computer\"); port 0 means \"any free port\", and oServer.url() tells you which one. Each address is connected to a Ring function with onGet , onPost , onDelete . The function receives the request and returns the answer : The handler returns The response a text status 200, the text as HTML [status, contentType, text] exactly that webJson(list) status 200, JSON A path that has no handler answers 404 Not found "}, {"u": "tut-09-http.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 9: HTTP", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # requests have a method, an address and maybe a body; responses have a status and a body, handlers return text, [status, type, text] or webJson , asynchronous calls return a Task : wait, then read Result , serveOne() hands requests to Ring."}, {"u": "tut-09-http.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 9: HTTP", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a /hello variant /hello?name=Ann that returns Hello, Ann . ( oRequest.query(\"name\") .) Make /time return the real time with DateTime.Now . Add a DELETE /item handler that answers 404 for unknown items. Call a real web site: oClient.GetStringAsync(\"https://example.com\") and print the length of the answer (needs internet)."}, {"u": "tut-09-http.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 9: HTTP", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 10: Time, tasks and other programs ."}, {"u": "tut-10-tasks-processes.html", "t": "Tutorial 10: Time, tasks and other programs", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-10-tasks-processes.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 10: Time, tasks and other programs", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # Stopwatch to measure time, tasks and Wait , Environment : the computer and its variables, Process : start another program, the one important rule about threads."}, {"u": "tut-10-tasks-processes.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 10: Time, tasks and other programs", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 10: time, tasks and other programs - Stopwatch, Task, Process, Environment load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Diagnostics\" \"System.Threading.Tasks\" \"System.Threading\" } func main # Measuring time: Stopwatch oWatch = new Stopwatch() oWatch.Start() new Thread().Sleep(120) oWatch.Stop() ? \"Slept about 120 ms, measured at least 100: \" + (oWatch.ElapsedMilliseconds >= 100) # Tasks run work in the background; Wait() waits for the end on the Ring thread oTask = new Task().Delay(100) ? \"Task finished at once? : \" + oTask.IsCompleted oTask.Wait(NULL) ? \"Task finished a"}, {"u": "tut-10-tasks-processes.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 10: Time, tasks and other programs", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Stopwatch # Start() , Stop() , then ElapsedMilliseconds (or Elapsed for a TimeSpan ). Thread.Sleep(120) pauses the program for 120 ms. Measure the parts of a program that feel slow before you try to speed anything up. Tasks # A Task represents work that happens \"in the background\" or in the future. Task.Delay(100) is a task that completes after 100 ms. IsCompleted says whether it is done; Wait(NULL) blocks until it is. Task.FromResult(\"done\") is a task that is already finished, with the result done , read with getProp(\"Result\") . The rule: Ring runs on one thread # Warning .NET "}, {"u": "tut-10-tasks-processes.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 10: Time, tasks and other programs", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # time measuring and waiting, tasks and their results, the Ring-thread rule, the environment and starting other programs."}, {"u": "tut-10-tasks-processes.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 10: Time, tasks and other programs", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Measure how long it takes to compute 100000 square roots in a Ring loop. Run cmd /c dir and count the lines in its output. Print the three biggest environment variables by length ( GetEnvironmentVariables() ). Start notepad.exe with a Process , wait 3 seconds and close it with Kill() ."}, {"u": "tut-10-tasks-processes.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 10: Time, tasks and other programs", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Part 2 builds programs with windows: Tutorial 11: Your first window ."}, {"u": "tut-11-first-window.html", "t": "Tutorial 11: Your first window", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-11-first-window.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 11: Your first window", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the parts of a Windows Forms program: runtime, form, controls, events, message loop, the helper functions uiForm , uiLabel , uiButton , uiRun , why event code can only see global variables, what the helpers do, by writing the program without them."}, {"u": "tut-11-first-window.html#windows-forms-in-one-minute", "t": "Tutorial 11: Your first window", "h": "Windows Forms in one minute", "x": "Windows Forms in one minute # Windows Forms is the classic way to build desktop windows in .NET. A window is a Form . Things in it \u2014 labels, buttons, text boxes \u2014 are controls . A control is an object with properties ( Text , Location , Size ) and events ( Click ). The program runs a message loop : it waits for the user to do something, runs your code, and waits again until the window closes."}, {"u": "tut-11-first-window.html#step-1-the-smallest-window", "t": "Tutorial 11: Your first window", "h": "Step 1: the smallest window", "x": "Step 1: the smallest window # # Tutorial 11, step 1: the smallest window load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } oForm = uiForm(\"My first window\",320,120) uiTest(oForm,\"dnAssert('the title',oForm.Text,'My first window')\") uiRun(oForm) # show the window and wait until the user closes it uiEnd() Step 1: a window with a title The using { } block comes first . Because it names System.Windows.Forms , it starts the Windows desktop runtime and makes the Ring thread suitable for windows (this is what dnUseDesktop() does). uiForm(\"My first window\",320,120) makes a form with"}, {"u": "tut-11-first-window.html#step-2-a-label-and-a-button", "t": "Tutorial 11: Your first window", "h": "Step 2: a label and a button", "x": "Step 2: a label and a button # # Tutorial 11, step 2: a label and a button load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } oForm = uiForm(\"Click counter\",320,120) oLabel = uiLabel(oForm,\"The button was not clicked yet\",20,15,280,24) # parent, text, x, y, width, height oButton = uiButton(oForm,\"Click me\",20,55,120,32,\"\") # no code yet for the click uiTest(oForm,\"dnAssert('the label',oLabel.Text,'The button was not clicked yet')\") uiRun(oForm) uiEnd() Step 2: controls uiLabel(parent, text, x, y, width, height) and uiButton(parent, text, x, y, width, height, code) create a co"}, {"u": "tut-11-first-window.html#step-3-the-button-does-something", "t": "Tutorial 11: Your first window", "h": "Step 3: the button does something", "x": "Step 3: the button does something # # Tutorial 11, step 3: the button does something - events and global variables load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } # The code of an event runs later, outside this part of the program: it can only see GLOBAL variables. # So the controls and the counter are global variables, created here at the top. oForm = \"\" oLabel = \"\" nClicks = 0 func main oForm = uiForm(\"Click counter\",320,120) oLabel = uiLabel(oForm,\"The button was not clicked yet\",20,15,280,24) uiButton(oForm,\"Click me\",20,55,120,32,\"clicked()\") # the click calls the fun"}, {"u": "tut-11-first-window.html#step-4-without-the-helpers", "t": "Tutorial 11: Your first window", "h": "Step 4: without the helpers", "x": "Step 4: without the helpers # The helpers are only short-cuts. This program builds the same window with the real classes, so you can see what is going on: # Tutorial 11, step 4: the same window without the helper functions - what uiForm, uiLabel and uiButton do load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } oForm = \"\" oLabel = \"\" oButton = \"\" nClicks = 0 func main # every control is an object of a class, configured with properties inside braces oForm = new Form() { Text = \"Click counter (no helpers)\" ClientSize = new Size(320,120) StartPosition = \"CenterScreen\" # an enum "}, {"u": "tut-11-first-window.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 11: Your first window", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Idea Example start the desktop runtime first using { \"System.Windows.Forms\" } a form and controls uiForm , uiLabel , uiButton positions and sizes in pixels 20, 15, 280, 24 an event is Ring code in a text \"clicked()\" events see only globals oLabel , nClicks at the top the same without helpers new Form() { ... }"}, {"u": "tut-11-first-window.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 11: Your first window", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a third button, Add 10 , that adds ten to the counter. Make the label red when the counter is above 5 ( oLabel.ForeColor = new Color().Red ). Add a second label that shows even or odd . Change the form so that it cannot be resized ( FormBorderStyle = \"FixedDialog\" , MaximizeBox = False )."}, {"u": "tut-11-first-window.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 11: Your first window", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 12: A form with many controls ."}, {"u": "tut-12-controls.html", "t": "Tutorial 12: A form with many controls", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-12-controls.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 12: A form with many controls", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the common controls and their main properties, reading and changing what the user entered, validating input and giving feedback, finding controls in Controls for tests."}, {"u": "tut-12-controls.html#the-common-controls", "t": "Tutorial 12: A form with many controls", "h": "The common controls", "x": "The common controls # Control The user... Main property Label reads text Text TextBox types text Text NumericUpDown picks a number Value , Minimum , Maximum ComboBox picks one item of a drop-down list SelectedIndex , Text CheckBox switches something on or off Checked ListBox sees (and picks from) a list Items , SelectedIndex Button clicks Text , event Click"}, {"u": "tut-12-controls.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 12: A form with many controls", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 12: a form with many controls - text boxes, check box, combo box, list box, validation load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } # controls that the event code uses are global variables oForm = \"\" oName = \"\" oAge = \"\" oCountry = \"\" oNewsletter = \"\" oResult = \"\" oPeople = \"\" func main oForm = uiForm(\"Registration\",400,330) uiLabel(oForm,\"Name\",20,20,80,22) oName = uiTextBox(oForm,110,18,260,24) uiLabel(oForm,\"Age\",20,56,80,22) oAge = new NumericUpDown() { Location = new Point(110,54) Size = new Size(80,24) Minimum = 1 Maximum = 120 Value = 30 "}, {"u": "tut-12-controls.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 12: A form with many controls", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Creating the controls # The helpers uiTextBox , uiComboBox , uiCheckBox and uiListBox create the control and add it to the form. uiComboBox(oForm, x, y, width, aItems) fills the drop-down list from a Ring list. A NumericUpDown has no helper, so it is created with the class and configured in braces: oAge = new NumericUpDown() { Location = new Point(110,54) Size = new Size(80,24) Minimum = 1 Maximum = 120 Value = 30 } oForm.Controls.Add(oAge) The control only appears in the window after oForm.Controls.Add(...) . Reading what was entered # Question Code what was typed? oName.Text w"}, {"u": "tut-12-controls.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 12: A form with many controls", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # controls are objects; read and write their properties, Items , Checked , Value , SelectedIndex for the common ones, validate first, then act, PerformClick() lets a test press a button."}, {"u": "tut-12-controls.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 12: A form with many controls", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Clear the age and country after a registration. Refuse ages under 18 with a message. Add a RadioButton pair ( Male / Female ) in a GroupBox and add the choice to the line. Add a Remove button that deletes the selected person ( oPeople.Items.RemoveAt(...) )."}, {"u": "tut-12-controls.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 12: A form with many controls", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 13: A small text editor ."}, {"u": "tut-13-editor.html", "t": "Tutorial 13: A small text editor", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-13-editor.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 13: A small text editor", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # menus with uiMenu , a multi-line TextBox that fills the window ( Dock ), a StatusStrip , the open and save dialogs, reacting to a change with an event, a layout rule: add the filling control first."}, {"u": "tut-13-editor.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 13: A small text editor", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 13: a small text editor - menus, a status bar, files and a message when you quit with unsaved text load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } oForm = \"\" oEditor = \"\" oStatus = \"\" cFileName = \"\" lChanged = False func main oForm = uiForm(\"Tiny editor\",560,360) # The editor fills the window. Add the control that fills FIRST: Windows Forms docks the controls added later # (the menu and the status bar) before it, so the editor gets what is left. oEditor = uiMemo(oForm,0,0,560,338) oEditor { Dock = \"Fill\" Font = new Font(\"Consol"}, {"u": "tut-13-editor.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 13: A small text editor", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Menus # uiMenu(oForm, aMenus) builds the menu bar from a list: [title, [ [item, code], ... ]] . Each item has the Ring code to run when it is chosen; \"-\" is a separator line. Filling the window: Dock # Dock = \"Fill\" makes a control take all the free space of its parent; other values are \"Top\" , \"Bottom\" , \"Left\" , \"Right\" . When the window is resized the control follows. Warning Order matters. Windows Forms places docked controls starting with the last added . If you add the menu first and the filling editor after it, the editor is placed first and fills everything \u2014 under the m"}, {"u": "tut-13-editor.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 13: A small text editor", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Idea Example a menu from a list uiMenu(oForm, [...]) fill the window Dock = \"Fill\" add the filling control first editor, then menu, then status bar status information StatusStrip file dialogs uiOpenFileName , uiSaveFileName react to changes setTextChangedEvent"}, {"u": "tut-13-editor.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 13: A small text editor", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Warn before New or Exit when the text is not saved ( uiAsk ). Add Edit \u2192 Select all ( oEditor.SelectAll() ) and Copy ( oEditor.Copy() ). Show the cursor line number in the status bar ( oEditor.GetLineFromCharIndex(oEditor.SelectionStart) ). Add a Font menu item that opens a FontDialog and changes oEditor.Font . The full-size version of this program is the Notepad application."}, {"u": "tut-13-editor.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 13: A small text editor", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 14: Drawing and animation ."}, {"u": "tut-14-animation.html", "t": "Tutorial 14: Drawing and animation", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-14-animation.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 14: Drawing and animation", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # drawing with UiCanvas , animation with a timer and a \"game loop\", keyboard events and why a form needs KeyPreview , keeping the program state in global variables."}, {"u": "tut-14-animation.html#the-ingredients-of-a-game", "t": "Tutorial 14: Drawing and animation", "h": "The ingredients of a game", "x": "The ingredients of a game # State : numbers that describe the world (position, speed, score). Update : a function that changes the state a little (move the ball). Draw : a function that shows the state. A timer that calls update and draw, again and again. Input : keys change the state."}, {"u": "tut-14-animation.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 14: Drawing and animation", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 14: drawing and animation - a canvas, a timer and the keyboard load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } C_WIDTH = 400 C_HEIGHT = 260 oForm = \"\" oPicture = \"\" oCanvas = \"\" oTimer = \"\" nX = 60 nY = 40 nDX = 4 nDY = 3 nPaddle = 160 nScore = 0 func main oForm = uiForm(\"Bounce\",C_WIDTH,C_HEIGHT) oPicture = uiPictureBox(oForm,0,0,C_WIDTH,C_HEIGHT) oCanvas = new UiCanvas(oPicture) # a canvas draws on the picture box draw() # the form (not a button) must receive the keys: arrows move the paddle oForm.KeyPreview = True oForm.setKeyDownEvent(\"keyPress"}, {"u": "tut-14-animation.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 14: Drawing and animation", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The canvas # uiPictureBox(oForm, x, y, w, h) creates a picture box, and new UiCanvas(oPicture) gives it a bitmap and simple drawing calls: Call Draws clear(color) fills everything fillCircle(x,y,radius,color) a filled circle fillRect(x,y,w,h,color) a filled rectangle text(text,x,y,color,size) text show() puts the finished picture on the screen A color is a name ( \"Orange\" ), a #RRGGBB text, or a .NET Color . Every frame the program clears the canvas and draws everything again; show() makes the result visible. The timer # uiTimer(20,\"step()\") creates a timer that calls step() eve"}, {"u": "tut-14-animation.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 14: Drawing and animation", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # state, update, draw, timer, input, UiCanvas for drawing, show() to display, KeyPreview and KeyCode , stop the timer in tests and call the update by hand."}, {"u": "tut-14-animation.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 14: Drawing and animation", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make the ball faster each time it hits the paddle. Add three lives: when the ball falls, take one life and show it; at zero show Game over . Add the Space key to pause ( oTimer.Stop() / Start() ). Draw a row of bricks at the top and remove the one the ball hits. The Pong and Snake applications show complete games."}, {"u": "tut-14-animation.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 14: Drawing and animation", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 15: WPF ."}, {"u": "tut-15-wpf.html", "t": "Tutorial 15: WPF", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-15-wpf.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 15: WPF", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the difference between WPF and Windows Forms, XAML, finding elements by name, events, data binding."}, {"u": "tut-15-wpf.html#wpf-and-windows-forms", "t": "Tutorial 15: WPF", "h": "WPF and Windows Forms", "x": "WPF and Windows Forms # Windows Forms WPF Layout positions in pixels panels that arrange their children ( StackPanel , Grid ) Description code only XAML text or code Look classic Windows controls any look: shapes, brushes, animation Best for simple tools, quick results modern, scalable UIs Both work from Ring with the same ideas: objects, properties, events."}, {"u": "tut-15-wpf.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 15: WPF", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 15: WPF - describe the window in XAML, find elements by name, bind a slider to a text load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Windows\" \"System.Windows.Controls\" \"System.Windows.Controls.Primitives\" \"System.Windows.Media\" \"System.Windows.Shapes\" \"System.Windows.Data\" \"System.Windows.Threading\" \"System\" } oWindow = \"\" oSlider = \"\" oShape = \"\" oValue = \"\" oGreet = \"\" oNameBox = \"\" func main # 1. The window is described in XAML: a text format of elements and attributes cXaml = '<Window xmlns=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation\" xmlns:x=\"http://schemas.mic"}, {"u": "tut-15-wpf.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 15: WPF", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # XAML # XAML is XML where each element is a WPF object and each attribute is a property: <\"t\">class=\"k\">Button \"t\">x:Name=\"greet\" \"t\">Content=\"Greet\" \"t\">Margin=\"0,8,0,0\"/> means \"a Button , named greet , with the content Greet , and a margin on top\". <StackPanel> is a panel that stacks its children vertically. The Window element is the window. The program builds the XAML text and calls wpfXaml(text) , which creates all the objects. Note WPF has classes with the same names as Windows Forms ones. In RingDotNet the WPF version of a clashing name starts with Wpf : WpfButton , WpfTex"}, {"u": "tut-15-wpf.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 15: WPF", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # WPF windows can be described in XAML, wpfXaml , wpfFind , wpfRun , events work as before, bindings keep elements in sync without code."}, {"u": "tut-15-wpf.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 15: WPF", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a CheckBox named shout and make the greeting upper case when it is ticked. Add a ProgressBar and bind its Value to the slider as well. Change the circle's colour with a second slider ( Brush ). See the WPF samples for shapes, grids, menus, tabs and timers."}, {"u": "tut-15-wpf.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 15: WPF", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 16: A web service with a database ."}, {"u": "tut-16-web-service.html", "t": "Tutorial 16: A web service with a database", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-16-web-service.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 16: A web service with a database", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the shape of a small web API ( GET list, POST add, DELETE remove), storing data with RingDatabase from a web handler, answering with JSON and the right status codes, validating a request."}, {"u": "tut-16-web-service.html#the-design", "t": "Tutorial 16: A web service with a database", "h": "The design", "x": "The design # A REST-style API uses the HTTP method to say what to do and the address to say what to do it with: Request Meaning Answer GET /notes list the notes 200 and a JSON array POST /notes (the text is the body) add a note 201 and the new id, or 400 if the text is empty DELETE /notes?id=1 delete a note 200 , or 404 if there is no such note"}, {"u": "tut-16-web-service.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 16: A web service with a database", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 16: a web service with a database - JSON over HTTP, stored with Entity Framework load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Net.Http\" \"RingDotNet.Web\" \"RingDotNet.Data\" } oServer = \"\" oClient = \"\" cUrl = \"\" oDb = \"\" func main # 1. The database: one table, kept in memory for this tutorial (use \"Data Source=notes.db\" for a file) oDb = new RingDatabase(\"Data Source=:memory:\",[ [\"Notes\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Text\",\"string\",0,[[\"required\",1],[\"maxlength\",200]]],[\"Done\",\"int\",0,[[\"default\",0]]]]] ]) # 2. The web service: each address is a Ring function oServer = new WebServer(\"http://12"}, {"u": "tut-16-web-service.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 16: A web service with a database", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Two start-up calls # \"RingDotNet.Web\" in the using block starts the web runtime and \"RingDotNet.Data\" loads Entity Framework. Both before any other .NET call. The database # A RingDatabase with a table Notes : a numbered key Id , a required Text (at most 200 characters), and a Done flag with a default of 0. \"Data Source=:memory:\" keeps the data in memory, which is perfect for a tutorial; use \"Data Source=notes.db\" for a file. See tutorial 17 . The handlers # Each handler is a Ring function that receives the request and returns the response. listNotes reads every row with oDb.all"}, {"u": "tut-16-web-service.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 16: A web service with a database", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # methods + addresses + status codes make an API, handlers use the database and return JSON, validate input and answer with the right status, globals for what handlers need."}, {"u": "tut-16-web-service.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 16: A web service with a database", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add PUT /notes?id=1 that marks a note as done ( update ). Add GET /notes?id=2 that returns one note, or 404 . Make the server serve an HTML page at / that lists the notes, as the WebApi application does. Switch to a file database and restart the program: are the notes still there?"}, {"u": "tut-16-web-service.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 16: A web service with a database", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 17: Databases step by step ."}, {"u": "tut-17-databases.html", "t": "Tutorial 17: Databases step by step", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-17-databases.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 17: Databases step by step", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a database, a table, a row and a key are, describing tables as data and creating them, insert, read, update, delete, searching safely with SQL parameters, relations between tables, queries, aggregates, transactions and file databases."}, {"u": "tut-17-databases.html#database-words", "t": "Tutorial 17: Databases step by step", "h": "Database words", "x": "Database words # Word Meaning table a list of rows of the same shape (like a spreadsheet sheet) column one field of every row ( Title , Year ) row one record key a column that identifies a row ( Id ); the database numbers it for you foreign key a column that holds the key of a row in another table ( AuthorId ) SQL the language of databases; you only need a little of it RingDotNet uses Entity Framework Core with SQLite , a database in one file (or in memory). You describe the tables as Ring data; there is no SQL to write for normal work."}, {"u": "tut-17-databases.html#part-1-tables-rows-searches", "t": "Tutorial 17: Databases step by step", "h": "Part 1: tables, rows, searches", "x": "Part 1: tables, rows, searches # # Tutorial 17: databases step by step - a library catalog with Entity Framework Core load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"RingDotNet.Data\" } func main # STEP 1. Describe the tables: [table name, [ [column, type, isKey, options], ... ]] aSchema = [ [\"Authors\",[ [\"Id\",\"int\",1], # a key of type int is numbered by the database [\"Name\",\"string\",0,[[\"required\",1],[\"unique\",1]]] ]], [\"Books\",[ [\"Id\",\"int\",1], [\"Title\",\"string\",0,[[\"required\",1],[\"maxlength\",80]]], [\"Year\",\"int\",0,[[\"nullable\",1]]], [\"Copies\",\"int\",0,[[\"default\",1]]], [\"AuthorId\",\"int\",0,[[\"references\",\"Authors"}, {"u": "tut-17-databases.html#part-2-relations-queries-transactions-files", "t": "Tutorial 17: Databases step by step", "h": "Part 2: relations, queries, transactions, files", "x": "Part 2: relations, queries, transactions, files # # Tutorial 17, part 2: relations, queries, transactions and a file database load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.IO\" \"RingDotNet.Data\" } func main cFile = new Path().Combine([new Path().GetTempPath(),\"ringdotnet_tutorial17.db\"]) if new File().Exists(cFile) new File().Delete(cFile) ok aSchema = [ [\"Authors\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Name\",\"string\",0,[[\"required\",1]]]]], [\"Books\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Title\",\"string\",0],[\"Year\",\"int\",0],[\"Stars\",\"double\",0], [\"AuthorId\",\"int\",0,[[\"references\",\"Authors\"],[\"ondelete\",\"cascade\"]]]]] ] oDb = new RingDatabase(\"Data"}, {"u": "tut-17-databases.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 17: Databases step by step", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # describe the schema as data, let the database enforce the rules, parameters, never pasted text, relations with foreign keys, queries, aggregates, transactions, files."}, {"u": "tut-17-databases.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 17: Databases step by step", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a Members table and a Loans table (a member borrows a book) and list who has which book. Write a function that lists the authors with no books. Use migrate() after adding a Pages column to the schema ( Entity Framework ). Use RingEntityDb for the same data and compare ( Entity Framework )."}, {"u": "tut-17-databases.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 17: Databases step by step", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 18: A library written in C# ."}, {"u": "tut-18-csharp-library.html", "t": "Tutorial 18: A library written in C#", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-18-csharp-library.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 18: A library written in C#", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the steps to use any .NET library from Ring, what a C# class looks like, so you can read the ones you meet, how properties, methods, overloads, static methods, out parameters, events and enums arrive in Ring."}, {"u": "tut-18-csharp-library.html#the-steps", "t": "Tutorial 18: A library written in C#", "h": "The steps", "x": "The steps # Get the library as a .dll (build it, or copy it from a NuGet package). Wrap it : ring wrapassembly.ring Library.dll classes writes a Ring class for every public C# class. Load it : dnLoad(\"Library.dll\") and dnImportFrom(\"classes/\",\"Namespace\") . Use it : new ClassName() , exactly like the built-in classes."}, {"u": "tut-18-csharp-library.html#the-c-library", "t": "Tutorial 18: A library written in C#", "h": "The C# library", "x": "The C# library # You do not have to write C#, but reading a little helps. This library is in docs/tutorials/Greeter : using System; namespace Tutorial.Greetings { // An enum: Ring uses its values as texts (\"Polite\", \"Casual\") public enum Style { Polite, Casual, Shout } // A class written in C#: the properties, methods and events below are what Ring can use public class Greeter { // a property that can be read and written public string Name { get; set; } = \"World\"; // a property that can only be read public int Greetings { get; private set; } // a method without parameters, and a method with pa"}, {"u": "tut-18-csharp-library.html#the-ring-program", "t": "Tutorial 18: A library written in C#", "h": "The Ring program", "x": "The Ring program # # Tutorial 18: using a library written in C# - build it, wrap it, call it # (the library is in the folder Greeter: Greeter.cs and Greeter.csproj) load \"dotnet.ring\" nTimes = 0 # event code can only see globals cHere = currentdir() cDll = cHere + \"/Greeter/bin/Greeter.dll\" cClasses = cHere + \"/Greeter/classes\" # STEP 1. Build the C# project (the .NET SDK is needed for this step only) if not fexists(cDll) ? \"building the library...\" system(\"dotnet build Greeter -c Release -o Greeter/bin -nologo -v q > nul\") ok # STEP 2. Make Ring classes for it. The generator is a Ring program"}, {"u": "tut-18-csharp-library.html#how-c-members-look-in-ring", "t": "Tutorial 18: A library written in C#", "h": "How C# members look in Ring", "x": "How C# members look in Ring # In C# In Ring public string Name { get; set; } oGreeter.Name and oGreeter.Name = \"Ring\" public int Greetings { get; private set; } oGreeter.Greetings \u2014 read only Hello() and Hello(string name, Style style) Hello(NULL) and Hello([ :name = \"Ann\", :style = \"Casual\" ]) public enum Style { Polite, Casual, Shout } the texts \"Polite\" , \"Casual\" , \"Shout\" public static int Add(int a, int b) new Greeter().Add(20,22) bool TryDivide(double a, double b, out double result) a list [worked, result] public event EventHandler<string> Greeted; oGreeter.setGreetedEvent(\"code\") Overl"}, {"u": "tut-18-csharp-library.html#using-a-nuget-package", "t": "Tutorial 18: A library written in C#", "h": "Using a NuGet package", "x": "Using a NuGet package # A package is a set of .dll files. Download it (or copy it from ~/.nuget/packages ), put the DLLs together, and follow the same steps: wrapassembly.ring , dnLoad , dnImportFrom . See Your own assemblies ."}, {"u": "tut-18-csharp-library.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 18: A library written in C#", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # wrap \u2192 load \u2192 use, C# properties, overloads, statics, out , events and enums map to Ring as shown, you can use any .NET library without writing C#."}, {"u": "tut-18-csharp-library.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 18: A library written in C#", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a property Prefix (default \"Hello\" ) to Greeter and use it in Hello . Rebuild, rewrap, run. Add a method Repeat(string text, int times) and call it with named arguments. Find a small NuGet package (for example a text or math helper), copy its DLL, wrap it and call one method."}, {"u": "tut-18-csharp-library.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 18: A library written in C#", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 19: Callbacks and events \u2014 Part 3 goes deeper into the ideas that you have used so far."}, {"u": "tut-19-callbacks.html", "t": "Tutorial 19: Callbacks and events", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-19-callbacks.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 19: Callbacks and events", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a callback (a delegate ) is, the three ways to write one: a function name, a text of code, an event handler, the argument and result convention: aDotNetArgs and xDotNetResult , what happens with errors inside a callback, why callbacks must run on the Ring thread."}, {"u": "tut-19-callbacks.html#what-is-a-callback", "t": "Tutorial 19: Callbacks and events", "h": "What is a callback?", "x": "What is a callback? # Sometimes a .NET method does not know what exactly to do: Method The part it does not know List.Sort how to compare two items List.FindAll which items to keep List.ForEach what to do with each item an event such as Click what to do when it happens You give it a callback : code that .NET calls back when it needs the answer. In .NET the type of such a thing is a delegate ( Action , Func , Predicate , Comparison , EventHandler ...). In Ring you create one with dnCallback(...) ."}, {"u": "tut-19-callbacks.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 19: Callbacks and events", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 19: callbacks and events - giving Ring code to .NET load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Collections.Generic\" \"System.Collections.ObjectModel\" } # Callback code can only see global variables nLetters = 0 nChanges = 0 func main oWords = new StringList() oWords.Add(\"pear\") oWords.Add(\"apple\") oWords.Add(\"fig\") oWords.Add(\"avocado\") # 1. A callback that is the NAME of a function. .NET calls it once for each item. ? \"1. ForEach calls showItem for every word:\" oWords.ForEach_(dnCallback(\"showItem\")) # 2. A callback that decides: sort with your own comparison oWords.Sort([ :co"}, {"u": "tut-19-callbacks.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 19: Callbacks and events", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # 1. A function name # dnCallback(\"showItem\") is the simplest form: the name of a Ring function. .NET calls it once per item. The function gets one parameter, a list of the arguments that .NET passes, and returns the result: func showItem aArgs ? \" item: \" + aArgs[1] ForEach_ ends with _ because ForEach is a Ring keyword. 2. A callback that decides # Sort([ :comparison = dnCallback(\"compareLength\") ]) passes a comparison. .NET gives the function two items ( aArgs[1] , aArgs[2] ); it returns a negative number if the first goes first, a positive one if the second goes first, and 0 f"}, {"u": "tut-19-callbacks.html#the-rules", "t": "Tutorial 19: Callbacks and events", "h": "The rules", "x": "The rules # Warning Code in a callback sees only global variables. It runs later, outside the function that created it. nLetters and nChanges are global in the program. A local variable of main would be unknown to the callback. Warning Callbacks run on the Ring thread, during a call. A callback that .NET tries to run from another thread (a thread-pool timer, a task) is ignored with a message on stderr . Use Windows Forms timers ( uiTimer ) for repeating work, and wait for tasks on the Ring thread."}, {"u": "tut-19-callbacks.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 19: Callbacks and events", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Idea Example a callback by function name dnCallback(\"showItem\") arguments and result aArgs[1] , return code in a text aDotNetArgs[1] , xDotNetResult an event setCollectionChangedEvent(\"...\") , clear...Event() errors travel back try around the .NET call"}, {"u": "tut-19-callbacks.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 19: Callbacks and events", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Sort the words alphabetically by their last letter. Use FindAll to keep the words with more than four letters, and ForEach_ to print them in upper case. Make a handler for CollectionChanged that prints the new item ( aDotNetArgs[2].getProp('NewItems') ). Set the same event twice and see that the second handler replaces the first."}, {"u": "tut-19-callbacks.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 19: Callbacks and events", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 20: Exploring .NET with reflection ."}, {"u": "tut-20-reflection.html", "t": "Tutorial 20: Exploring .NET with reflection", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-20-reflection.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 20: Exploring .NET with reflection", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what reflection is, how to get a Type from a name or from an object, listing properties, methods and their parameters, enums and their values, where the manual gets its class reference from."}, {"u": "tut-20-reflection.html#reflection", "t": "Tutorial 20: Exploring .NET with reflection", "h": "Reflection", "x": "Reflection # Reflection means that a program looks at its own types: names, members, parameters. .NET offers it in the class Type and the namespace System.Reflection . It is how the class reference of this manual was made: a Ring program asked every wrapped class what it contains."}, {"u": "tut-20-reflection.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 20: Exploring .NET with reflection", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 20: exploring .NET from Ring - ask a class what it can do load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text\" \"System.Reflection\" } func main # Reflection means: a program looks at its own types. Start from a type name. oType = new Type().GetType([ :typeName = \"System.Text.StringBuilder\" ]) ? \"Type : \" + oType.FullName ? \"Is a class : \" + oType.IsClass ? \"Base type : \" + oType.BaseType.FullName ? \"Assembly : \" + oType.Assembly.GetName(NULL).Name # Its public properties ? nl + \"Properties:\" for oProperty in oType.GetProperties(NULL).toList() ? \" \" + oProperty.Name + \" : \""}, {"u": "tut-20-reflection.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 20: Exploring .NET with reflection", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # A Type # A Type describes a class. Two ways to get one: oType = new Type().GetType([ :typeName = \"System.Text.StringBuilder\" ]) # from the full name oType = oObject.GetType() # from an object that exists Its properties say what it is: FullName , Namespace , IsClass , BaseType , Assembly . Properties and methods # GetProperties(NULL) and GetMethods(NULL) return arrays of PropertyInfo and MethodInfo ; toList() turns an array into a Ring list. Each has a Name . A PropertyInfo has PropertyType and CanWrite ; a MethodInfo has GetParameters() , whose items have ParameterType and Name "}, {"u": "tut-20-reflection.html#using-the-manual-instead", "t": "Tutorial 20: Exploring .NET with reflection", "h": "Using the manual instead", "x": "Using the manual instead # You often need only to look . The class reference shows, for every wrapped class, its constructors, properties, methods with the Ring calls , and events \u2014 produced by exactly this kind of reflection. Use search ( / ) to jump to a class by name."}, {"u": "tut-20-reflection.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 20: Exploring .NET with reflection", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # reflection lets a program inspect types, GetProperties , GetMethods , GetParameters , overloads show up as repeated names, the class reference is made from reflection."}, {"u": "tut-20-reflection.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 20: Exploring .NET with reflection", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # List the public properties of System.IO.FileInfo and which of them can be written. Print every overload of Math.Round with its parameter types and names. How many are there? List the values of System.IO.FileAttributes . Write a function describe(cTypeName) that prints the name, the base type and the number of methods and properties of any type."}, {"u": "tut-20-reflection.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 20: Exploring .NET with reflection", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 21: A project: analyze a log file ."}, {"u": "tut-21-log-analyzer.html", "t": "Tutorial 21: A project: analyze a log file", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-21-log-analyzer.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 21: A project: analyze a log file", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # how to plan a small data-processing program, combining File , Regex , NumberDictionary , LINQ and JSON, writing helper functions for the things you do many times."}, {"u": "tut-21-log-analyzer.html#the-task", "t": "Tutorial 21: A project: analyze a log file", "h": "The task", "x": "The task # A server writes lines like these to a log file: 2024-05-17 08:15:02 ERROR [db] connection lost 2024-05-17 08:16:10 INFO [web] request done We want to know: how many lines of each level, which parts have errors, which error texts occur, and to save a short report."}, {"u": "tut-21-log-analyzer.html#the-plan", "t": "Tutorial 21: A project: analyze a log file", "h": "The plan", "x": "The plan # Read the file line by line. Cut each line into date, time, level, part, text with a regular expression. Lines that do not match are skipped. Count with a dictionary: lines per level, errors per part. Collect the error texts and ask LINQ for the distinct ones, sorted. Write a JSON report. The program makes its own sample log first, so that you can run it anywhere."}, {"u": "tut-21-log-analyzer.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 21: A project: analyze a log file", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 21: a project - analyze a log file (files, regular expressions, dictionaries, LINQ, JSON) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Text.RegularExpressions\" \"System.Collections.Generic\" \"System.Linq\" \"System.Text.Json\" } func main # STEP 1. Make a sample log file (a real program would read an existing file) cLog = new Path().Combine([new Path().GetTempPath(),\"tutorial21.log\"]) makeLog(cLog) ? \"STEP 1: log written, \" + len(new File().ReadAllLines(cLog).toList()) + \" lines\" # STEP 2. Read it and pick the parts of each line with a regular expression. # a lin"}, {"u": "tut-21-log-analyzer.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 21: A project: analyze a log file", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The regular expression # ^(?<date>\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}) (?<time>\\d{2}:\\d{2}:\\d{2}) (?<level>[A-Z]+) \\[(?<part>\\w+)\\] (?<text>.+)$ Read it left to right: four digits, a dash, two digits... then a space, a time, a word in capital letters (the level), a word in square brackets (the part), and the rest of the line. (?<level>...) is a named group : after a match, oMatch.Groups.Item(\"level\").Value reads it. oMatch.Success says whether the line matched at all; the last line of the sample file does not, so it is skipped (30 of 31 lines were understood). Counting with a dictionary # A dicti"}, {"u": "tut-21-log-analyzer.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 21: A project: analyze a log file", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # plan: read \u2192 parse \u2192 count \u2192 summarise \u2192 write, named regex groups make parsing readable, dictionaries count, small helper functions keep main short."}, {"u": "tut-21-log-analyzer.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 21: A project: analyze a log file", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Count the errors per day : put the date in a dictionary. Print the busiest hour (use the first two characters of the time). Take the file name from the command line ( sysargv[3] ) and report an error if it does not exist. Write the report with webJson instead of the helper (with \"RingDotNet.Web\" in the using block)."}, {"u": "tut-21-log-analyzer.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 21: A project: analyze a log file", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 22: Images ."}, {"u": "tut-22-images.html", "t": "Tutorial 22: Images", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-22-images.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 22: Images", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # bitmaps and pixels, Graphics , brushes and pens, GetPixel and SetPixel , saving and loading image files, when to use a different way to call a method (positional arguments and objects)."}, {"u": "tut-22-images.html#concepts", "t": "Tutorial 22: Images", "h": "Concepts", "x": "Concepts # An image is a grid of pixels ; each pixel has a colour made of red, green, blue (0\u2013255) and an alpha value for transparency. A Bitmap is such a grid in memory. To draw on it you use a Graphics object created from the bitmap. A Brush fills areas, a Pen draws lines, a Font draws text. Note System.Drawing is part of the desktop runtime, so the using block with System.Drawing starts the desktop runtime even though the program shows no window."}, {"u": "tut-22-images.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 22: Images", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 22: images - create, draw, read pixels, resize, convert to gray and save (no window needed) # ring t22_images.ring [output folder] load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Drawing.Imaging\" } func main cFolder = new Path().GetTempPath() if len(sysargv) >= 3 cFolder = sysargv[3] new Directory().CreateDirectory(cFolder) ok # STEP 1. A bitmap is a rectangle of pixels. Graphics draws on it. oBitmap = new Bitmap(240,160) oGraphics = new Graphics().FromImage(oBitmap) oGraphics.Clear(new Color().White) # STEP 2. Draw: brushes fill, pens draw lin"}, {"u": "tut-22-images.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 22: Images", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Drawing # new Bitmap(240,160) is an empty picture; new Graphics().FromImage(oBitmap) gives the drawing tool; Clear(color) fills the background. Then: Call Draws FillRectangle(brush, new Rectangle(x,y,w,h)) a filled rectangle FillEllipse(brush, new Rectangle(x,y,w,h)) a filled ellipse (a circle if w = h) DrawLine(pen, new Point(x1,y1), new Point(x2,y2)) a line DrawString(text, font, brush, new PointF(x,y)) text Colours are new Color().SteelBlue , new Color().Black ... (the names of the .NET Color properties). A pen's width is a property: new Pen(color) { Width = 3 } . Tip These d"}, {"u": "tut-22-images.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 22: Images", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # bitmap, pixel, colour, Graphics , brushes and pens, GetPixel / SetPixel loops, save, load, resize, Dispose() when finished."}, {"u": "tut-22-images.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 22: Images", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Draw a 100 \u00d7 100 chessboard with FillRectangle . Turn the picture into a negative (255 \u2212 each channel). Make a thumbnail that keeps the proportions of any picture: width 100, height 100 \u00d7 h / w . Save the same bitmap as a JPEG and compare the file sizes. Show the picture in a window with a PictureBox ( oPicture.Image = oBitmap )."}, {"u": "tut-22-images.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 22: Images", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 23: Layout and richer controls ."}, {"u": "tut-23-layout.html", "t": "Tutorial 23: Layout and richer controls", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-23-layout.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 23: Layout and richer controls", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the layout tools: Dock , SplitContainer , TabControl , TableLayoutPanel , DataGridView showing a DataTable through a DataView , connecting controls with events, two layout details that cost time if you do not know them."}, {"u": "tut-23-layout.html#layout-in-windows-forms", "t": "Tutorial 23: Layout and richer controls", "h": "Layout in Windows Forms", "x": "Layout in Windows Forms # Placing controls with x and y numbers (as in tutorial 11) is fine for a small fixed window. A window that the user can resize needs layout containers : Tool What it does Dock glue a control to an edge ( Top , Bottom , Left , Right ) or fill the rest ( Fill ) SplitContainer two panels with a bar the user can drag TabControl / TabPage pages with tabs TableLayoutPanel a grid of rows and columns FlowLayoutPanel controls placed one after the other, wrapping Anchor keep the distance to the edges when the window grows"}, {"u": "tut-23-layout.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 23: Layout and richer controls", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 23: layout and richer controls - SplitContainer, TabControl, TableLayoutPanel, DataGridView with a filter load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Data\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } # the controls that event code uses oForm = \"\" oList = \"\" oNameValue = \"\" oCityValue = \"\" oCountValue = \"\" oGrid = \"\" oView = \"\" aCustomers = [[\"Ann\",\"Cairo\"],[\"Bob\",\"Oslo\"],[\"Cy\",\"Tokyo\"]] func main oForm = uiForm(\"Customers\",620,300) # A SplitContainer has two panels with a bar between them that the user can drag. oSplit = new SplitContainer() { Dock = \"Fill\" } oForm.Controls.Add(o"}, {"u": "tut-23-layout.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 23: Layout and richer controls", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # SplitContainer # A SplitContainer has Panel1 (left) and Panel2 (right). You add controls to the panels: oSplit.Panel1.Controls.Add(oList) . Give each child Dock = \"Fill\" so that it follows its panel. Warning Set SplitterDistance after the container was added to the form. Before that, the container has its default size (200 pixels) and the position is relative to that: the bar jumps when the form's real size arrives. Tabs # Create a TabPage for each tab, set its Text (the title on the tab), and add the pages to oTabs.TabPages . The page is a container: add its controls to oDetail"}, {"u": "tut-23-layout.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 23: Layout and richer controls", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # containers instead of pixel positions, Dock , SplitContainer , TabControl , TableLayoutPanel , DataTable \u2192 DataView \u2192 DataGridView , set the splitter after adding it, ask the view for counts."}, {"u": "tut-23-layout.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 23: Layout and richer controls", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a FlowLayoutPanel at the bottom with three buttons that wrap when the window is narrow. Sort the grid by quantity when the user clicks a button ( oView.Sort = \"Quantity DESC\" ). Add a text box that filters the customer list by what the user types. Add a third tab with a PictureBox that shows the picture made in tutorial 22."}, {"u": "tut-23-layout.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 23: Layout and richer controls", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 24: A project: a to-do list that remembers ."}, {"u": "tut-24-todo.html", "t": "Tutorial 24: A project: a to-do list that remembers", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-24-todo.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 24: A project: a to-do list that remembers", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # planning a small application: data, window, behaviour, saving, a CheckedListBox , saving to JSON and loading it again, a trap: lists of pairs and named arguments, keeping a window and its data in step."}, {"u": "tut-24-todo.html#the-design", "t": "Tutorial 24: A project: a to-do list that remembers", "h": "The design", "x": "The design # Part Decision Data the tasks live in the list control itself; each has a text and a done flag Window a text box and Add / Remove buttons on top, the list in the middle, a status line at the bottom Behaviour Add ignores blank text; clicking an item ticks it; the status says \"n of m done\" Saving after every change the tasks are written to a JSON file; at start-up the file is read"}, {"u": "tut-24-todo.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 24: A project: a to-do list that remembers", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 24: a project - a to-do list that remembers its tasks (Windows Forms + JSON + files) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Text.Json\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } oForm = \"\" oNew = \"\" oTasks = \"\" oStatus = \"\" cFile = \"\" lLoading = False func main # the tasks are saved here (a test uses a temporary file) cFile = new Path().Combine([new Path().GetTempPath(),\"tutorial24-tasks.json\"]) if uiSelfTest() cFile = new Path().Combine([new Path().GetTempPath(),\"tutorial24-test.json\"]) if new File().Exists(cFile) new File().Delete(cFile) ok ok oForm ="}, {"u": "tut-24-todo.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 24: A project: a to-do list that remembers", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The window # Remember tutorial 13 : the control that fills the window is added first , then the top panel and the status label (the helpers uiPanel , uiLabel and Dock do the rest). CheckOnClick = True makes one click tick or untick an item. Add and remove # addTask trims the text, ignores a blank one, adds Items.Add([ :item = text, :isChecked = False ]) (the second parameter is the initial checked state), clears the text box, saves and refreshes the status. removeTask removes the selected item ( SelectedIndex is -1 when nothing is selected). Reacting to a tick # setMouseUpEvent("}, {"u": "tut-24-todo.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 24: A project: a to-do list that remembers", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # design first: data, window, behaviour, saving, CheckedListBox , events after a click, JSON for saving, with the pair list trap in mind, a flag to avoid saving while loading, tests that use their own file."}, {"u": "tut-24-todo.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 24: A project: a to-do list that remembers", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Show the done tasks in gray (use DrawItem , or simply move done tasks to the bottom). Add Clear done that removes all ticked tasks. Save to the user's documents folder ( Environment.GetFolderPath(\"MyDocuments\") ). Add a due date to each task and sort by it. The full-size version is the TodoList application."}, {"u": "tut-24-todo.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 24: A project: a to-do list that remembers", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 25: Sockets ."}, {"u": "tut-25-sockets.html", "t": "Tutorial 25: Sockets", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-25-sockets.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 25: Sockets", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what an address, a port, TCP and a socket are, TcpListener (the server) and TcpClient (the client), streams, readers and writers over a connection, the order in which a conversation happens."}, {"u": "tut-25-sockets.html#words", "t": "Tutorial 25: Sockets", "h": "Words", "x": "Words # Word Meaning IP address the address of a computer: 127.0.0.1 means this computer port a number (0\u201365535) that says which program on that computer TCP a reliable connection: bytes arrive in order, none lost socket one end of a connection listener a program that waits for others to connect"}, {"u": "tut-25-sockets.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 25: Sockets", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 25: sockets - a TCP server and client in one program load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.IO\" \"System.Net\" \"System.Net.Sockets\" } func main # STEP 1. The server: a listener waits for connections on a port of this computer. oListener = new TcpListener(new IPAddress().Loopback,0) # port 0: any free port oListener.Start(NULL) nPort = oListener.LocalEndpoint.Port ? \"STEP 1: the server listens on 127.0.0.1, port is chosen: \" + (nPort > 0) # STEP 2. The client connects. The connection is accepted by the operating system at once. oClient = new TcpClient() oClient.Connect([ :hos"}, {"u": "tut-25-sockets.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 25: Sockets", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The server side # new TcpListener(new IPAddress().Loopback, 0) prepares a listener on the loopback address ( 127.0.0.1 , reachable only from this computer) and port 0 , which means \"any free port\". Start(NULL) starts it, and LocalEndpoint.Port tells which port was chosen. The client side # new TcpClient() is the client; Connect(\"127.0.0.1\", nPort) connects. The operating system accepts the connection at once, even before the program calls AcceptTcpClient ; this is why client and server can live in one program, without threads. Accepting # oListener.AcceptTcpClient() returns the "}, {"u": "tut-25-sockets.html#real-servers", "t": "Tutorial 25: Sockets", "h": "Real servers", "x": "Real servers # In a real system the server and the clients are different programs. A server loops: accept a client, talk, accept the next. Ring code runs on one thread, so a server that serves many clients at once checks oListener.Pending() and polls its clients in turn; for a web server use the ASP.NET WebServer ( tutorial 9 ), which handles the threading for you."}, {"u": "tut-25-sockets.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 25: Sockets", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # an address and a port identify the other side, listener/accept on the server, connect on the client, streams, readers and writers, each read needs a matching write."}, {"u": "tut-25-sockets.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 25: Sockets", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make the server answer pong to ping and unknown to anything else. Let the client send a number; the server answers its square. Try a port that nobody listens on and catch the error from Connect . Print the address the client sees: oClient.Client.RemoteEndPoint.ToString() ."}, {"u": "tut-25-sockets.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 25: Sockets", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 26: A small web site ."}, {"u": "tut-26-website.html", "t": "Tutorial 26: A small web site", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-26-website.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 26: A small web site", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # serving HTML pages that share a frame, an HTML form and reading its fields, redirecting the work by status code ( 400 for bad input), cross-site scripting and why you escape text, running the site for real."}, {"u": "tut-26-website.html#the-site", "t": "Tutorial 26: A small web site", "h": "The site", "x": "The site # Address Page GET / the home page GET /about an about page GET /guestbook the entries, and a form to add one POST /guestbook the form sends its fields here"}, {"u": "tut-26-website.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 26: A small web site", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 26: a small web site - HTML pages, a form, links and a guestbook (ASP.NET Core) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Net.Http\" \"System.Text\" \"RingDotNet.Web\" } oServer = \"\" oClient = \"\" cUrl = \"\" aEntries = [] # the guestbook: [name, message] # Run it as a web site: ring g26_website.ring --serve then open http://localhost:5090/ func main cAddress = \"http://127.0.0.1:0\" if find(sysargv,\"--serve\") > 0 cAddress = \"http://localhost:5090\" ok oServer = new WebServer(cAddress) oServer.onGet(\"/\",\"homePage\") oServer.onGet(\"/guestbook\",\"guestbookPage\") oServer.onPost(\"/guestbook\","}, {"u": "tut-26-website.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 26: A small web site", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # One frame for all pages # The function page(cTitle, cBody) builds the whole HTML text: a title, a few lines of style, a navigation bar and the body. Every handler calls it, so every page looks the same and a change of design is made in one place. A handler returns a text ; the server sends it as text/html . The form # <\"t\">class=\"k\">form method='post' action='/guestbook'> <\"t\">class=\"k\">input name='name' placeholder='Your name'> <\"t\">class=\"k\">textarea name='message' ...></\"t\">class=\"k\">textarea> <\"t\">class=\"k\">button type='submit'>Send</\"t\">class=\"k\">button> </\"t\">class=\"k\">for"}, {"u": "tut-26-website.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 26: A small web site", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # pages are texts; a function can build the common frame, forms send fields by POST; oRequest.form(\"name\") reads them, answer errors with the right status, always escape what visitors write."}, {"u": "tut-26-website.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 26: A small web site", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a page /stats that shows the number of entries and the longest message. Show the newest entry first. Limit a message to 200 characters and refuse longer ones with a 400 . Store the entries in a SQLite file with RingDatabase , so that they survive a restart."}, {"u": "tut-26-website.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 26: A small web site", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 27: Entity objects and migrations ."}, {"u": "tut-27-entities.html", "t": "Tutorial 27: Entity objects and migrations", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-27-entities.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 27: Entity objects and migrations", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the object style of Entity Framework: RingEntityDb , navigation properties and include , tracked objects and save() , migrations: add columns, tables and indexes to an existing file, named, run-once changes with apply ."}, {"u": "tut-27-entities.html#two-styles-one-schema", "t": "Tutorial 27: Entity objects and migrations", "h": "Two styles, one schema", "x": "Two styles, one schema # Tutorial 17 used RingDatabase : a row is a list of [column, value] pairs. RingEntityDb uses the same schema but turns each table into a real .NET class. A row is an object; related rows are properties of the object. RingDatabase RingEntityDb a row [[\"Name\",\"Ann\"],[\"Goals\",5]] an object: oPlayer.getProp(\"Name\") a related row parent(...) , children(...) oPlayer.getProp(\"Team\") , oTeam.getProp(\"Players\") changing update(table, key, values) oPlayer.setProp(\"Goals\",8) then save() best for scripts, quick tools programs that work with objects for a while"}, {"u": "tut-27-entities.html#part-1-objects", "t": "Tutorial 27: Entity objects and migrations", "h": "Part 1: objects", "x": "Part 1: objects # # Tutorial 27, part 1: entity objects - the rows of a table as real objects with navigation properties load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"RingDotNet.Data\" } func main # The schema is the same as with RingDatabase. RingEntityDb turns every table into a .NET class. oDb = new RingEntityDb(\"Data Source=:memory:\",[ [\"Teams\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Name\",\"string\",0,[[\"required\",1]]]]], [\"Players\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Name\",\"string\",0],[\"Goals\",\"int\",0,[[\"default\",0]]],[\"TeamId\",\"int\",0,[[\"references\",\"Teams\"]]]]] ]) # The generated class has a property for every column, plus navigations that follo"}, {"u": "tut-27-entities.html#part-2-migrations", "t": "Tutorial 27: Entity objects and migrations", "h": "Part 2: migrations", "x": "Part 2: migrations # You shipped version 1 of a program. Users have data. In version 2 you need a Phone column and a Notes table. If you only change the schema in the program, the old file still has the old tables : new code meets old data. A migration brings the file up to date. # Tutorial 27, part 2: changing a database that already has data - migrations load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.IO\" \"RingDotNet.Data\" } func main cFile = new Path().Combine([new Path().GetTempPath(),\"ringdotnet_tutorial27.db\"]) if new File().Exists(cFile) new File().Delete(cFile) ok cConnection = \"Data Source=\" + cFi"}, {"u": "tut-27-entities.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 27: Entity objects and migrations", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # objects and navigation properties, include loads related objects, change a property, save() , migrate() for schema changes, apply() for one-time data changes."}, {"u": "tut-27-entities.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 27: Entity objects and migrations", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a Matches table (two teams, a score) and print every match with both team names. Move a player to another team by changing the Team navigation and saving. Add a Email column in version 3 and write the migration steps. Count the goals of each team with a for loop over query(\"Teams\", [[\"include\",[\"Players\"]]]) ."}, {"u": "tut-27-entities.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 27: Entity objects and migrations", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 28: Testing your programs ."}, {"u": "tut-28-testing.html", "t": "Tutorial 28: Testing your programs", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-28-testing.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 28: Testing your programs", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # why and when to test, writing code that is easy to test, dnAssert and dnTestSummary , testing for errors, self-testing programs for windows."}, {"u": "tut-28-testing.html#why-test", "t": "Tutorial 28: Testing your programs", "h": "Why test?", "x": "Why test? # A test is a small program that checks that another piece of code gives the right answers. You run all the tests after every change: if one fails, you know what broke and when . The tests of this package ( samples/UsingDotNet/tests/run.sh ) are what makes it possible to change the generator and regenerate 4,500 classes with confidence."}, {"u": "tut-28-testing.html#make-code-testable", "t": "Tutorial 28: Testing your programs", "h": "Make code testable", "x": "Make code testable # Do Because small functions with one job each can be tested alone return results instead of printing or showing them a test can check a returned value pass data in as parameters instead of reading globals a test can try many inputs keep window code thin: the logic is in functions logic is easy to test, windows are not"}, {"u": "tut-28-testing.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 28: Testing your programs", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 28: testing your programs - small functions, dnAssert, expected errors, a test summary # ring t28_testing.ring all tests pass # ring t28_testing.ring --break one test fails on purpose, to show what a failure looks like load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text.RegularExpressions\" } func main testFizzBuzz() testWordCount() testDates() testErrors() if find(sysargv,\"--break\") > 0 testBrokenOnPurpose() ok nFailed = dnTestSummary() # prints \"TESTS: n passed, m failed\" and returns the number of failures # ---------------------------------------------------------------"}, {"u": "tut-28-testing.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 28: Testing your programs", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # dnAssert # dnAssert(\"name\", actual, expected) compares two values. Texts are compared as text, numbers as numbers (with a small tolerance for decimals). A good name says what should be true : \"30 is FizzBuzz\", \"spaces only\", \"a week\". What to test # Kind Example in the program normal cases fizzBuzz(3) is Fizz the edges an empty text, spaces only, the same day surprising input many spaces between words errors a text that is not a number Testing an error # You cannot assert an error directly, so catch it, remember that it happened, and assert on the flag and the message: lRaised ="}, {"u": "tut-28-testing.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 28: Testing your programs", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # small, returning functions are testable, dnAssert + dnTestSummary , test the normal case, the edges, and the errors, windows can test themselves, run all tests after every change."}, {"u": "tut-28-testing.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 28: Testing your programs", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write isLeapYear(nYear) and test years 1900, 2000, 2023 and 2024. Write a function that formats a number of seconds as h:mm:ss and test five values, including 0 and 3600. Add a test for a function that you wrote in an earlier tutorial. Break a function on purpose and see which tests fail."}, {"u": "tut-28-testing.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 28: Testing your programs", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 29: Regular expressions in depth \u2014 Part 4 adds more lessons and projects. When you want to look something up, keep Core concepts , the class reference and Gotchas & limits open."}, {"u": "tut-29-regex.html", "t": "Tutorial 29: Regular expressions in depth", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-29-regex.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 29: Regular expressions in depth", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the building blocks of a pattern, finding, replacing and splitting, groups and named groups, greedy and lazy matching, validating input, escaping text that is not a pattern."}, {"u": "tut-29-regex.html#why-learn-them", "t": "Tutorial 29: Regular expressions in depth", "h": "Why learn them", "x": "Why learn them # Whenever a job says \"find the part of the text that looks like...\", a regular expression does it in one line: phone numbers, dates, e-mails, log lines, codes. The same patterns work in every language, so what you learn here is useful everywhere."}, {"u": "tut-29-regex.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 29: Regular expressions in depth", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 29: regular expressions in depth - patterns, groups, replace, split, validation load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text.RegularExpressions\" } func main cText = \"Ann: 555-0101, Bob: 555-0199, Cy: 555-0123 (call after 18:30 on 2024-05-17)\" # 1. Literal text matches itself ? \"1. 'Bob' found? : \" + new Regex(\"Bob\").IsMatch(cText) # 2. Character classes: \\d a digit, \\w a letter/digit/underscore, \\s a space, [abc] one of these, . any character ? \"2. First number : \" + new Regex(\"\\d+\").Match(cText).Value # 3. Quantifiers: + one or more, * zero or more, ? zero or one,"}, {"u": "tut-29-regex.html#the-building-blocks", "t": "Tutorial 29: Regular expressions in depth", "h": "The building blocks", "x": "The building blocks # Pattern Matches Example abc exactly those letters Bob . any one character b.b finds bob , bab \\d \\w \\s a digit, a letter/digit/underscore, a space \\d+ finds 555 [abc] [a-z] [^0-9] one of these, a range, not these [A-Z] + * ? one or more, zero or more, zero or one \\d+ {3} {2,4} exactly three, two to four \\d{3}-\\d{4} ^ $ the start, the end of the text ^Ann \\b the edge of a word \\bcat\\b a|b a or b Ann|Cy ( ) a group (ab)+ (?<name> ) a named group (?<year>\\d{4})"}, {"u": "tut-29-regex.html#how-the-program-uses-them", "t": "Tutorial 29: Regular expressions in depth", "h": "How the program uses them", "x": "How the program uses them # IsMatch answers yes or no (step 1). Match(text).Value is the first match, Matches(text) finds all of them: use Count and Item(i) (steps 2, 3). Groups pick parts of a match: Groups.Item(\"year\").Value . The pattern (?<year>\\d{4})-(?<month>\\d{2})-(?<day>\\d{2}) gives the three numbers of a date (step 5). Alternation Ann|Cy finds either name (step 6). Replace can use the groups in the replacement text ( $1 , $2 , $3 ): turning 2024-05-17 into 17/05/2024 is one call (step 7). Split cuts a text at every match of the pattern: ,\\s* is \"a comma and any spaces\" (step 8). Greed"}, {"u": "tut-29-regex.html#good-habits", "t": "Tutorial 29: Regular expressions in depth", "h": "Good habits", "x": "Good habits # Build a pattern in small steps and test each step. Put a pattern with comments in a variable with a clear name. Prefer a simple pattern plus some code over one unreadable pattern. Never use a regex to parse a language with nesting (HTML, JSON): use a parser ( tutorial 6 )."}, {"u": "tut-29-regex.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 29: Regular expressions in depth", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # classes, quantifiers, anchors, groups, alternation, IsMatch , Match , Matches , Replace , Split , greedy vs lazy, anchored patterns for validation, Escape for user text."}, {"u": "tut-29-regex.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 29: Regular expressions in depth", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write a pattern for a Ring variable name that starts with n or c followed by a capital letter ( nCount , cName ) and find them all in a source file. Validate a simple e-mail address and test it with five good and five bad examples. Replace every run of spaces and tabs in a text by a single space. Extract all #hashtags from a sentence."}, {"u": "tut-29-regex.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 29: Regular expressions in depth", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 30: Tabular data ."}, {"u": "tut-30-tables.html", "t": "Tutorial 30: Tabular data", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-30-tables.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 30: Tabular data", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what CSV is and how to read it, DataTable , DataRow , DataView , Compute for sums, averages, counts, sorting and filtering without changing the data, grouping by hand and writing CSV."}, {"u": "tut-30-tables.html#csv", "t": "Tutorial 30: Tabular data", "h": "CSV", "x": "CSV # CSV (comma separated values) is the simplest table format: a header line with the column names, then one line per row with the values separated by commas. Region,Seller,Amount North,Ann,1200 South,Bob,800 Spreadsheets, databases and many programs read and write it. Note Real CSV files can contain commas inside quoted values ( \"Smith, John\" ). This tutorial's data has none, so splitting on commas is enough. For messy files use a CSV library (a NuGet package, tutorial 18 ) or a regular expression."}, {"u": "tut-30-tables.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 30: Tabular data", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 30: tabular data - read a CSV text, use a DataTable, filter, sort, summarise, write a CSV load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Data\" \"System.IO\" } func main # STEP 1. The data: a CSV text (comma separated values): a header line and one line per row cCsv = \"Region,Seller,Amount\" + nl + \"North,Ann,1200\" + nl + \"South,Bob,800\" + nl + \"North,Cy,450\" + nl + \"East,Dee,2000\" + nl + \"South,Eve,300\" + nl + \"North,Ann,700\" # STEP 2. A DataTable has typed columns and rows oTable = new DataTable() oTable.Columns.Add([ :columnName = \"Region\", :type = \"System.String\" ]) oTabl"}, {"u": "tut-30-tables.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 30: Tabular data", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Step 2: the table # A DataTable has typed columns : Region and Seller are texts, Amount is an integer ( System.Int32 ). Types matter: sums and sorting treat numbers as numbers, not as text (as text, 800 would sort after 2000 ). Rows.Add([...]) adds a row; the values are in column order. The program turns the lines into rows: str2list splits the text into lines, substr(line,\",\",nl) turns the commas into line breaks, and number() converts the amount. Step 3: Compute # Compute(expression, filter) evaluates an aggregate over the rows that pass the filter: Expression Meaning SUM(Amou"}, {"u": "tut-30-tables.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 30: Tabular data", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # tables have typed columns, Compute summarises, DataView sorts and filters, group by with a loop, reading and writing CSV is a text job."}, {"u": "tut-30-tables.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 30: Tabular data", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add the average sale per seller. Sort by region, then by amount descending ( oView.Sort = \"Region, Amount DESC\" ). Write only the rows of one region to a new CSV file. Add a calculated column Commission = Amount * 0.1 ( Columns.Add with an Expression )."}, {"u": "tut-30-tables.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 30: Tabular data", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 31: A project: find duplicate files ."}, {"u": "tut-31-duplicates.html", "t": "Tutorial 31: A project: find duplicate files", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-31-duplicates.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 31: A project: find duplicate files", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # recursive folder listing, content hashes as fingerprints, grouping with a dictionary, a report that is useful, cleaning up after a demo."}, {"u": "tut-31-duplicates.html#the-idea", "t": "Tutorial 31: A project: find duplicate files", "h": "The idea", "x": "The idea # Two files are the same when their content is the same, whatever their names. Comparing every pair of files byte by byte is slow. A hash (SHA-256) is a short fingerprint of the content: the same content always gives the same hash and different content (practically) never does. So: compute the hash of every file, put the files in a dictionary keyed by the hash , every key with more than one file is a set of duplicates."}, {"u": "tut-31-duplicates.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 31: A project: find duplicate files", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 31: a project - find duplicate files (folders, hashes, dictionaries) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Security.Cryptography\" \"System.Collections.Generic\" } func main # STEP 1. A folder with some files: three of them have the same content, two others as well cFolder = new Path().Combine([new Path().GetTempPath(),\"tutorial31\"]) if new Directory().Exists(cFolder) new Directory().Delete([ :path = cFolder, :recursive = True ]) ok new Directory().CreateDirectory(cFolder) new Directory().CreateDirectory(new Path().Combine([cFolder,\"old\"])) makeFile(cFol"}, {"u": "tut-31-duplicates.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 31: A project: find duplicate files", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Step 1: make test data # The program creates its own folder with files: three with the same text (one in a subfolder, one with a different name), two with the same other text, and one unique file. A demo that makes its own data runs anywhere and can be checked: the answer is known. Step 2: list recursively # Directory.GetFiles([ :path = folder, :searchPattern = \"*.txt\", :searchOption = \"AllDirectories\" ]) returns the files of the folder and of every subfolder. \"TopDirectoryOnly\" would skip the subfolders. The third parameter is an enum, written as a text. Step 3: hash and group "}, {"u": "tut-31-duplicates.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 31: A project: find duplicate files", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # a hash identifies content, a dictionary groups by key, recursive listing, tests with data that you made yourself."}, {"u": "tut-31-duplicates.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 31: A project: find duplicate files", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Group by file size first; hash only the files whose size occurs more than once. Also compare the first 1 MB only for a quick check of very big files. Add an option to move duplicates to a duplicates folder instead of just reporting. Make it a window: a folder box, a Scan button, and the result in a TreeView ( tutorial 23 )."}, {"u": "tut-31-duplicates.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 31: A project: find duplicate files", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 32: Settings ."}, {"u": "tut-32-settings.html", "t": "Tutorial 32: Settings", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-32-settings.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 32: Settings", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the special folders of the user, defaults, loading and saving settings, a Ring class that hides the details, what to do when the file is missing or damaged."}, {"u": "tut-32-settings.html#where-do-settings-live", "t": "Tutorial 32: Settings", "h": "Where do settings live?", "x": "Where do settings live? # Windows has a folder for every kind of file; .NET finds them with Environment.GetFolderPath : Name For MyDocuments files the user creates ApplicationData settings that follow the user to other computers LocalApplicationData settings and caches of this computer Desktop the desktop A program normally keeps its settings in a file inside its own folder under ApplicationData , for example \u2026\\ApplicationData\\MyProgram\\settings.json . The tutorial uses the temp folder so that it leaves nothing behind."}, {"u": "tut-32-settings.html#the-design", "t": "Tutorial 32: Settings", "h": "The design", "x": "The design # Rule Why every setting has a default the first run, and a missing key, work the file is JSON , readable by people users and support can read and edit it loading never fails the program a damaged file must not stop the application saving happens when the user changes something a crash does not lose the choices"}, {"u": "tut-32-settings.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 32: Settings", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 32: settings - defaults, a JSON file, loading, saving, and special folders load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Text.Json\" } func main # Where do programs keep their files? .NET knows the special folders of the user. oEnvironment = new Environment() ? \"Special folders exist:\" for cFolder in [\"MyDocuments\",\"ApplicationData\",\"LocalApplicationData\",\"Desktop\"] ? \" \" + cFolder + \" : \" + (len(oEnvironment.GetFolderPath(cFolder)) > 0) next # For this tutorial the file is in the temp folder cFile = new Path().Combine([new Path().GetTempPath(),\"tutorial32-set"}, {"u": "tut-32-settings.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 32: Settings", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # A class for settings # Everything about settings is in one Ring class, Settings , so the rest of the program only says oSettings.getValue(\"name\") and oSettings.setValue(\"fontSize\",14) . The class keeps a list of [key, value] pairs: its starting content is the list of defaults , so the keys that exist are defined in one place. Note The method that reads the file is called readFile , not load : load is a Ring keyword and cannot be a name. Saving # save writes a JSON object by hand: texts in quotes, numbers without. For real data with arbitrary text use JsonSerializer ( tutorial 6 "}, {"u": "tut-32-settings.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 32: Settings", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # the user's special folders, defaults first, then the file on top, unknown and missing keys are fine, loading must never crash the program."}, {"u": "tut-32-settings.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 32: Settings", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Move the file to ApplicationData\\TutorialApp\\settings.json (create the folder if needed with Directory.CreateDirectory ). Add a recentFiles setting that holds a list of up to five names. Make setValue refuse an unknown key with an error. Use the settings in a window: remember its position and size, and restore them when it opens ( tutorial 13 )."}, {"u": "tut-32-settings.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 32: Settings", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 33: Cultures ."}, {"u": "tut-33-cultures.html", "t": "Tutorial 33: Cultures", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-33-cultures.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 33: Cultures", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a culture is, formatting numbers, money and dates for other cultures, reading numbers with the right culture, the invariant culture for data, comparing and changing the case of text per language."}, {"u": "tut-33-cultures.html#what-is-a-culture", "t": "Tutorial 33: Cultures", "h": "What is a culture?", "x": "What is a culture? # A culture is a language plus, optionally, a country: en-US (English in the United States), de-DE (German in Germany), ar-EG (Arabic in Egypt). It decides: the symbols: the decimal separator ( . or , ), the thousands separator, the currency sign, the order and names of dates: 7/4/2024 , 04.07.2024 , the names of days and months, the rules for sorting and for upper and lower case. Every ToString and Parse that you call without saying a culture uses the culture of the computer's user . That is exactly right for text that people read, and exactly wrong for data that programs e"}, {"u": "tut-33-cultures.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 33: Cultures", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 33: cultures - the same number, date and text written, read and sorted in different languages load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Globalization\" } func main oString = new String() oConvert = new Convert() aCultures = [\"en-US\",\"de-DE\",\"fr-FR\",\"ar-EG\",\"ja-JP\"] # A culture (language + country) decides how numbers, money and dates are written ? \"A number, money and a date in five cultures:\" oDate = new DateTime(2024,7,4) for cName in aCultures oCulture = new CultureInfo(cName) cNumber = oString.Format([ :provider = oCulture, :format = \"{0:N2}\", :arg0 = 1234567.891 "}, {"u": "tut-33-cultures.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 33: Cultures", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Writing # String.Format(culture, \"{0:N2}\", value) formats with a format code : N2 is a number with two decimals and a thousands separator, C is currency, D (on a date) the long date. The same value 1234567.891 becomes 1,234,567.89 in English, 1.234.567,89 in German, 1 234 567,89 in French (with a special space) and uses Arabic digits for ar-EG . Reading # Double.Parse(text, culture) reads a number written in that culture . The text 1.234,5 is 1234.5 for the German culture, but an error or a different number for the English culture, where the comma means \"thousands\". TryParse avo"}, {"u": "tut-33-cultures.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 33: Cultures", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # a culture is a set of writing rules, say the culture when you format or parse, invariant for data, user culture for people, sorting and case depend on the language."}, {"u": "tut-33-cultures.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 33: Cultures", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Print today's date in a short format in six cultures of your choice. Parse \"3,5\" with fr-FR and with en-US : which works? Sort the words zebra apple \u00c4rger \u00d6l ananas in German and in Swedish. Write a function toData(nNumber) that formats a number with the invariant culture, and fromData(cText) that reads it back. Test both."}, {"u": "tut-33-cultures.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 33: Cultures", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 34: Timers and progress ."}, {"u": "tut-34-timers.html", "t": "Tutorial 34: Timers and progress", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-34-timers.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 34: Timers and progress", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the Windows Forms Timer and why it is safe, measuring time with Stopwatch , the progress bar, why a long job freezes a window, and the fix: work in small steps, how to test code that depends on time."}, {"u": "tut-34-timers.html#the-message-loop-and-freezing", "t": "Tutorial 34: Timers and progress", "h": "The message loop and freezing", "x": "The message loop and freezing # A window program lives in a message loop : it waits for something to happen (a click, a key, a repaint), runs your code, and returns to waiting. While your code runs, nothing else happens : the window is not repainted, buttons do not react. A job that takes five seconds, run in one go, freezes the window for five seconds, and Windows may mark it \"not responding\". In languages with threads the usual answer is \"do the work on another thread\". Ring code runs on one thread , so the answer here is different: cut the job into small parts and do one part each time a ti"}, {"u": "tut-34-timers.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 34: Timers and progress", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 34: timers and progress - a stopwatch and a long job that keeps the window alive load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Diagnostics\" \"System.Threading\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } oForm = \"\" oTime = \"\" oBar = \"\" oInfo = \"\" oClock = \"\" # the timer that updates the time every 50 ms oJob = \"\" # the timer that does one small part of the job every 20 ms oWatch = \"\" # a Stopwatch object nDone = 0 # how many parts of the job are done nTotal = 100 lRunning = False func main oWatch = new Stopwatch() oForm = uiForm(\"Timers\",360,200) oTime = uiLabel(oForm,\"0:00"}, {"u": "tut-34-timers.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 34: Timers and progress", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The timer # uiTimer(50,\"showTime()\") creates a Windows Forms Timer that calls showTime() every 50 milliseconds once it is started. This timer raises its Tick event on the Ring thread, inside the message loop , so the code can use Ring variables and controls freely. (The System.Threading.Timer of .NET ticks on another thread: Ring code cannot run there.) The stopwatch # A Stopwatch measures time exactly: Start() , Stop() , Reset() , IsRunning and ElapsedMilliseconds . The function showTime splits the milliseconds into minutes, seconds and tenths and writes them into the label. Th"}, {"u": "tut-34-timers.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 34: Timers and progress", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # never run a long job in one go inside an event, a timer calls a small step again and again, Stopwatch measures, Timer displays, put the work in functions so tests can call them."}, {"u": "tut-34-timers.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 34: Timers and progress", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a Cancel button that stops the job. Make the step size depend on how long the last step took. Add a countdown: a text box with seconds, a Start button, and a message when it reaches zero. Show the estimated time remaining next to the progress bar."}, {"u": "tut-34-timers.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 34: Timers and progress", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 35: A project: Tic-Tac-Toe ."}, {"u": "tut-35-tictactoe.html", "t": "Tutorial 35: A project: Tic-Tac-Toe", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-35-tictactoe.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 35: A project: Tic-Tac-Toe", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # separating the rules from the window , representing a game as data, creating controls in a loop, testing a game without clicking."}, {"u": "tut-35-tictactoe.html#the-structure", "t": "Tutorial 35: A project: Tic-Tac-Toe", "h": "The structure", "x": "The structure # Layer Contains Knows about windows? State aBoard (nine cells), cTurn , lOver no Rules winnerOf(aBoard) , boardFull(aBoard) no Moves play(nCell) , newGame() yes: they update the buttons and the status Window nine buttons, a label, New game yes The rules take the board as a parameter and return an answer. They do not read or write buttons. That makes them easy to test and easy to reuse: the same winnerOf could drive a computer opponent."}, {"u": "tut-35-tictactoe.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 35: A project: Tic-Tac-Toe", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 35: a project - Tic-Tac-Toe (game logic in functions, nine buttons, a status line) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } # THE STATE: nine cells, \"\" (empty), \"X\" or \"O\"; whose turn it is; is the game over aBoard = [\"\",\"\",\"\",\"\",\"\",\"\",\"\",\"\",\"\"] cTurn = \"X\" lOver = False # THE WINDOW oForm = \"\" oStatus = \"\" aButtons = [] func main oForm = uiForm(\"Tic-Tac-Toe\",300,376) oStatus = uiLabel(oForm,\"X to play\",10,10,280,26) oStatus.Font = new Font(\"Segoe UI\",14) for nCell = 1 to 9 nRow = floor((nCell - 1) / 3) nColumn = (nCell - 1) % 3 oButton = ui"}, {"u": "tut-35-tictactoe.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 35: A project: Tic-Tac-Toe", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The board as data # A list of nine texts, \"\" for an empty cell, \"X\" or \"O\" . Cell numbers go 1\u20139 across and down: 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9 The rules # There are only eight ways to win: three rows, three columns, two diagonals. winnerOf keeps them as a list of eight lists of three cell numbers. For each line it asks: is the first cell not empty, and are the other two the same? The first line where that is true gives the winner. Compare this with eight nested if s: the data describes the game, the code is short. Nine buttons in a loop # for nCell = 1 to 9 nRow = floor((nCell - 1) / 3) nC"}, {"u": "tut-35-tictactoe.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 35: A project: Tic-Tac-Toe", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # state, rules, moves, window: four layers, data that describes the game (the eight lines), controls from a loop, with click code built as a text, tests that play."}, {"u": "tut-35-tictactoe.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 35: A project: Tic-Tac-Toe", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Highlight the three winning buttons. Add a score: how many games X and O have won. Write bestMove(aBoard, cPlayer) for a computer opponent: win if you can, block if you must, otherwise take the centre. Make the board size 4 \u00d7 4 with four in a row. The TicTacToe application is the full-size version."}, {"u": "tut-35-tictactoe.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 35: A project: Tic-Tac-Toe", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 36: A chart ."}, {"u": "tut-36-chart.html", "t": "Tutorial 36: A chart", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-36-chart.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 36: A chart", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the arithmetic of a chart: scaling values to pixels, a \"nice\" axis maximum, drawing a grid, bars, numbers and labels, saving the picture, testing a drawing by reading its pixels."}, {"u": "tut-36-chart.html#the-plan", "t": "Tutorial 36: A chart", "h": "The plan", "x": "The plan # A chart is a picture of numbers, and every point of the picture is computed: a plot area : left margin for the numbers, top margin for the title, bottom margin for the labels, a scale : the biggest value (rounded up to a nice number) fills the height of the plot area, for each value a bar : its height is plotHeight \u00d7 value / scaleMax , its left edge is left + slot \u00d7 index , the text : title, numbers on the axis, value above each bar, label below."}, {"u": "tut-36-chart.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 36: A chart", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 36: a chart - turn numbers into a bar chart with UiCanvas, show it in a window and save it as a PNG load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Drawing.Imaging\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } C_WIDTH = 480 C_HEIGHT = 300 aLabels = [\"Jan\",\"Feb\",\"Mar\",\"Apr\",\"May\",\"Jun\"] aValues = [12,19,8,25,17,22] aColors = [\"SteelBlue\",\"Orange\",\"SeaGreen\",\"Crimson\",\"MediumPurple\",\"Goldenrod\"] oForm = \"\" oCanvas = \"\" func main oForm = uiForm(\"Sales chart\",C_WIDTH,C_HEIGHT + 36) oPicture = uiPictureBox(oForm,0,0,C_WIDTH,C_HEIGHT) oCanvas = new UiCanvas(oPicture) dr"}, {"u": "tut-36-chart.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 36: A chart", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The scale # The biggest value is 25. If 25 touched the top of the chart the axis would read 0, 6.25, 12.5... Ugly. niceMax finds a round number at or above the maximum: 25 \u2192 30, 8 \u2192 8, 120 \u2192 150. It takes the power of ten below the value ( 10 for 25), tries the factors 1, 1.5, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10 and returns the first product that is big enough. The axis then runs 0, 7.5, 15, 22.5, 30. (Ring prints 7.5 as 7.50 ; use rounding or a format if you want 7.5 .) Pixels from values # For a bar: nHeight = (nBottom - nTop) * value / nScaleMax . A value of nScaleMax fills the whole plot a"}, {"u": "tut-36-chart.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 36: A chart", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # chart = scale + bars + text, y grows downwards, a round axis maximum, tests can read pixels."}, {"u": "tut-36-chart.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 36: A chart", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Draw a line chart : points joined by oCanvas.line . Add a legend that lists the months with their colours. Read the data from a CSV file ( tutorial 30 ). Draw a pie chart with FillPie ( Graphics ), using the percentage of each value."}, {"u": "tut-36-chart.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 36: A chart", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 37: A project: a command runner ."}, {"u": "tut-37-runner.html", "t": "Tutorial 37: A project: a command runner", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-37-runner.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 37: A project: a command runner", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # starting another program from a window application, reading its output and error output, exit codes, handling failure with try / catch , a read-only, console-style text area."}, {"u": "tut-37-runner.html#the-idea", "t": "Tutorial 37: A project: a command runner", "h": "The idea", "x": "The idea # Almost every tool has a command-line version: git , ping , dotnet , ffmpeg , robocopy . A Ring program starts it with a Process , collects what it prints, and shows it. The same pattern is how you would build a front-end for any such tool: gather the options in controls, build the command line from them, run, show the result."}, {"u": "tut-37-runner.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 37: A project: a command runner", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 37: a project - a command runner (a window that starts a program and shows what it prints) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Diagnostics\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } oForm = \"\" oCommand = \"\" oOutput = \"\" oStatus = \"\" func main oForm = uiForm(\"Command runner\",560,380) uiLabel(oForm,\"Command:\",10,12,70,22) oCommand = uiTextBox(oForm,84,10,380,24) oCommand.Text = \"echo hello from the command line\" uiButton(oForm,\"Run\",472,8,76,28,\"runCommand()\") oOutput = uiMemo(oForm,10,46,538,300) oOutput { Font = new Font(\"Consolas\",10) ReadOnly = True BackColor"}, {"u": "tut-37-runner.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 37: A project: a command runner", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Describing the program to start # A ProcessStartInfo says what to run: Property Meaning FileName = \"cmd\" the program: the Windows command interpreter Arguments = \"/c \" + cCommand /c means \"run this command and then exit\" RedirectStandardOutput = True capture what it prints RedirectStandardError = True capture its error messages (a different stream) UseShellExecute = False start it directly (needed for redirecting) CreateNoWindow = True do not open a console window Using cmd /c lets the user type anything that the command prompt understands, including && to chain commands and bui"}, {"u": "tut-37-runner.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 37: A project: a command runner", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # ProcessStartInfo + Process run any program, output and error output are separate streams, the exit code tells success or failure, a window around a command-line tool."}, {"u": "tut-37-runner.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 37: A project: a command runner", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a Stop button that kills a long-running command ( oProcess.Kill() ; the process must be a global variable). Keep a history: a ComboBox with the last ten commands. Make a ping window: a host box, a Ping button and the result in plain text. Run a command in a given folder ( WorkingDirectory )."}, {"u": "tut-37-runner.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 37: A project: a command runner", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 38: WPF layout and styles ."}, {"u": "tut-38-wpf-layout.html", "t": "Tutorial 38: WPF layout and styles", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-38-wpf-layout.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 38: WPF layout and styles", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the Grid and its row and column definitions ( Auto , * ), Grid.Row , Grid.Column , Grid.ColumnSpan , resources and styles, building XAML with a loop, many events with one function."}, {"u": "tut-38-wpf-layout.html#layout-in-wpf", "t": "Tutorial 38: WPF layout and styles", "h": "Layout in WPF", "x": "Layout in WPF # WPF does not use pixel positions. A panel arranges its children: Panel Arrangement StackPanel one after another, vertically or horizontally Grid rows and columns DockPanel edges and a rest WrapPanel like text: wraps to the next line Canvas exact positions The window can be resized and everything follows. The Grid is the most useful: you divide the space and put each element into a cell."}, {"u": "tut-38-wpf-layout.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 38: WPF layout and styles", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 38: WPF layout and styles - Grid, resources, a Style for all buttons, a keypad load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Windows\" \"System.Windows.Controls\" \"System.Windows.Controls.Primitives\" \"System.Windows.Media\" \"System.Windows.Data\" \"System\" } oWindow = \"\" oDisplay = \"\" cEntered = \"\" func main # The window: a Grid with a display row on top and a keypad below. # - Grid.RowDefinitions / ColumnDefinitions divide the space: \"Auto\" = as big as the content, \"*\" = share what is left # - Window.Resources holds a Style: every Button inside gets the same look without repeating it "}, {"u": "tut-38-wpf-layout.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 38: WPF layout and styles", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The grid # <\"t\">class=\"k\">Grid.RowDefinitions> <\"t\">class=\"k\">RowDefinition \"t\">Height=\"Auto\"/> <!-- the display: as high as its content --> <\"t\">class=\"k\">RowDefinition \"t\">Height=\"*\"/> <!-- four rows that share the rest equally --> ... </\"t\">class=\"k\">Grid.RowDefinitions> <\"t\">class=\"k\">Grid.ColumnDefinitions> <\"t\">class=\"k\">ColumnDefinition \"t\">Width=\"*\"/> ... three equal columns </\"t\">class=\"k\">Grid.ColumnDefinitions> Auto is as big as the content needs, * takes a share of what is left ( 2* would take double). A fixed number is pixels. The keypad's four rows therefore grow a"}, {"u": "tut-38-wpf-layout.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 38: WPF layout and styles", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Grid rows and columns with Auto and * , Grid.Row , Grid.Column , Grid.ColumnSpan , one Style for all controls of a type, XAML assembled by a loop, many events with one function."}, {"u": "tut-38-wpf-layout.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 38: WPF layout and styles", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make the equal row heights 2* for the display and * for the keys. Add a style for TextBlock with a bigger font. Add a . key and a + key that appends the symbol (use another row in the grid). Give the window a minimum size ( MinWidth , MinHeight ) and resize it to see the layout follow."}, {"u": "tut-38-wpf-layout.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 38: WPF layout and styles", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 39: A REST client ."}, {"u": "tut-39-rest-client.html", "t": "Tutorial 39: A REST client", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-39-rest-client.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 39: A REST client", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what REST means in practice, sending GET and POST requests with a JSON body, reading the status and the JSON answer, timeouts and errors, keeping the request code in one helper function."}, {"u": "tut-39-rest-client.html#rest-in-one-minute", "t": "Tutorial 39: A REST client", "h": "REST in one minute", "x": "REST in one minute # A REST service gives you things ( resources ) at addresses and lets you use the HTTP methods on them: Request Meaning GET /api/books the list of books GET /api/book?id=2 one book POST /api/books + JSON add a book PUT / DELETE change / remove The answers are JSON, and the status code tells whether it worked: 200 OK, 201 Created, 404 Not Found, 500 server error. A good client always looks at the status before it trusts the body."}, {"u": "tut-39-rest-client.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 39: A REST client", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # For this tutorial the service is started in the same program, so that it needs no internet. The client code is exactly what you would write for a real service: only cUrl would be different. # Tutorial 39: a REST client - call a JSON service, read the answer, handle the errors # (the service is started in the same program so that the tutorial needs no internet) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Net.Http\" \"System.Text\" \"System.Text.Json\" \"RingDotNet.Web\" } oServer = \"\" oClient = \"\" cUrl = \"\" aBooks = [[1,\"The Hobbit\",1937],[2,\"Dune\",1965],[3,\"Neuromancer\",1984]] func main # --- th"}, {"u": "tut-39-rest-client.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 39: A REST client", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # One helper for all requests # request(method, path, body) builds the request, sends it, waits and returns [status, body] . Everything else calls it. A request is an HttpRequestMessage created with named arguments : [ :method = new HttpMethod(cMethod), :requestUri = cUrl + cPath ] . For a POST the body is a StringContent with the text, the encoding ( UTF8 ) and the media type application/json , which tells the server how to read it. Note The content is created in a variable first ( oContent = dnNew(...) ) and then assigned to oMessage.Content . Putting new Encoding().UTF8 inside "}, {"u": "tut-39-rest-client.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 39: A REST client", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # GET and POST with JSON, status before body, Task + Wait + Result , timeouts and catching network errors, one request helper."}, {"u": "tut-39-rest-client.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 39: A REST client", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add deleteBook(nId) using the DELETE method (and a handler on the service). Retry a failed request up to three times with a pause ( Thread.Sleep ). Add an Accept header ( oClient.DefaultRequestHeaders.Add(...) ). Call a real public API (for example a free JSON placeholder service) and print three fields. You need internet for this one."}, {"u": "tut-39-rest-client.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 39: A REST client", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 40: Your own dialog \u2014 Part 5 has more window programs, projects and web topics."}, {"u": "tut-40-dialog.html", "t": "Tutorial 40: Your own dialog", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-40-dialog.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 40: Your own dialog", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what modal means and how ShowDialog works, building a dialog form with OK and Cancel , AcceptButton , CancelButton and DialogResult , validating input and keeping the dialog open, returning the answer to the caller, testing a modal dialog with a timer."}, {"u": "tut-40-dialog.html#modal-and-modeless", "t": "Tutorial 40: Your own dialog", "h": "Modal and modeless", "x": "Modal and modeless # A modal window blocks the window behind it until it is closed: the user must answer first. That is what you want when the program needs an answer now \u2014 a file name, a choice, a few settings. form.ShowDialog() shows a window modally and waits : the line after it runs only after the dialog closed, and it returns how it was closed ( \"OK\" or \"Cancel\" ). A modeless window ( Show() ) lets the user continue working elsewhere."}, {"u": "tut-40-dialog.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 40: Your own dialog", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 40: your own dialog - a modal form that asks for data and gives it back load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } # the main window oForm = \"\" oShow = \"\" cName = \"Ann\" nAge = 31 # the dialog and its controls (the dialog code is run by events, so they are global too) oDialog = \"\" oNameBox = \"\" oAgeBox = \"\" oError = \"\" oOk = \"\" oTimer = \"\" nStep = 0 # which step of the test is next cNextName = \"\" func main oForm = uiForm(\"Profile\",340,150) oShow = uiLabel(oForm,\"\",20,20,300,50) oShow.Font = new Font(\"Segoe UI\",12) uiButton(oForm,\"Edit profile.."}, {"u": "tut-40-dialog.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 40: Your own dialog", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # A function that asks # askProfile(name, age) builds the dialog, shows it, and returns [name, age] or \"\" . The rest of the program does not know that a window was involved: editProfile calls it, checks islist(aAnswer) , and uses the answer. A dialog function hides the window behind a plain call, which also makes the logic easy to follow and to test. Making it look like a dialog # Property Effect FormBorderStyle = \"FixedDialog\" a thin border, not resizable MaximizeBox = False , MinimizeBox = False no maximise or minimise buttons StartPosition = \"CenterParent\" opens in the middle o"}, {"u": "tut-40-dialog.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 40: Your own dialog", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # ShowDialog is modal: it waits and returns the result, AcceptButton , CancelButton , DialogResult , validate before closing with OK, a dialog function that returns data or \"\" , a timer drives a modal dialog in a test."}, {"u": "tut-40-dialog.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 40: Your own dialog", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a Country combo box to the dialog. Show the current profile in the dialog title ( Edit profile - Ann ). Add an Apply button that changes the main window without closing the dialog. Use the standard dialogs for a change: ColorDialog , FontDialog , FolderBrowserDialog (they also use ShowDialog and return \"OK\" )."}, {"u": "tut-40-dialog.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 40: Your own dialog", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 41: Menus, toolbars and shortcuts ."}, {"u": "tut-41-menus.html", "t": "Tutorial 41: Menus, toolbars and shortcuts", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-41-menus.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 41: Menus, toolbars and shortcuts", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the menu bar, toolbar, context menu and status bar controls, shortcut keys, a menu item that can be ticked, the rule that keeps a program tidy: write each action once ."}, {"u": "tut-41-menus.html#the-principle", "t": "Tutorial 41: Menus, toolbars and shortcuts", "h": "The principle", "x": "The principle # A user may reach \"make upper case\" from the Text menu, a toolbar button, the right-click menu, or Ctrl+U . If each of those had its own code, a fix would have to be made four times. Instead each action is one function ( upperCase , newText ), and every control only calls it."}, {"u": "tut-41-menus.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 41: Menus, toolbars and shortcuts", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 41: menus, toolbars, context menus and shortcuts - one action, several ways to reach it load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } oForm = \"\" oText = \"\" oStatus = \"\" oWrapItem = \"\" nActions = 0 func main oForm = uiForm(\"Menus\",480,300) # The text area first (it fills the window: see tutorial 13) oText = uiMemo(oForm,0,0,480,260) oText { Dock = \"Fill\" Font = new Font(\"Consolas\",11) Text = \"Select some text, then use the menu, the toolbar, the right mouse button or a shortcut key.\" } # 1. A toolbar: buttons for the same actions. Controls docked "}, {"u": "tut-41-menus.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 41: Menus, toolbars and shortcuts", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The menu bar # uiMenu builds the bar from a list: [title, [ [item, code, shortcut], ... ]] . The code is the Ring code to run (a function call), \"-\" is a separator, and the optional shortcut is the .NET text of the Keys enum: \"Control, N\" , \"Control, A\" , \"Alt, F4\" . The shortcut also appears next to the item in the menu, and works even when the menu is closed. A tick in the menu # Word wrap is a ToolStripMenuItem created by hand with CheckOnClick = True : each click toggles Checked and raises CheckedChanged . The handler copies the state to the text box ( oText.WordWrap = oWrap"}, {"u": "tut-41-menus.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 41: Menus, toolbars and shortcuts", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # write each action once, menu items, toolbar buttons, context menus all call it, shortcuts are enum texts, CheckOnClick for ticks, docking order decides the layout."}, {"u": "tut-41-menus.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 41: Menus, toolbars and shortcuts", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add Redo and Undo ( oText.Undo() ) with Ctrl+Z . Enable or disable Cut and Copy depending on whether text is selected ( Enabled , SelectionChanged ). Add a recent-files list that grows as the user opens files. Add a toolbar button with an image ( oButton.Image = new Bitmap(...) , tutorial 22 )."}, {"u": "tut-41-menus.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 41: Menus, toolbars and shortcuts", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 42: A folder browser ."}, {"u": "tut-42-browser.html", "t": "Tutorial 42: A folder browser", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-42-browser.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 42: A folder browser", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # TreeView and its nodes, and the Tag property, ListView in details mode with columns, building a tree from folders recursively, reacting to the user's choice, formatting file sizes."}, {"u": "tut-42-browser.html#the-controls", "t": "Tutorial 42: A folder browser", "h": "The controls", "x": "The controls # Control Shows You use TreeView a hierarchy Nodes , Node.Nodes , SelectedNode , event AfterSelect ListView a list with columns View = \"Details\" , Columns , Items , ListViewItem.SubItems"}, {"u": "tut-42-browser.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 42: A folder browser", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 42: a folder browser - a TreeView of folders, a ListView of files, and the details of a file load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } oForm = \"\" oTree = \"\" oList = \"\" oStatus = \"\" cRoot = \"\" func main # The folder to browse: this program makes a small demo folder (give another folder as the first argument to browse it) cRoot = makeDemoFolder() if len(sysargv) >= 3 and sysargv[3] != \"--selftest\" cRoot = sysargv[3] ok oForm = uiForm(\"Folder browser\",640,360) # The pieces: a SplitContainer with the folders on the left and t"}, {"u": "tut-42-browser.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 42: A folder browser", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # A demo folder # makeDemoFolder creates folders and files in the temp folder, so the tutorial can show a result and be tested anywhere. Give the program a real folder as its first argument to browse it: ring g42_browser.ring \"C:\\Users\\you\\Documents\" . Building the tree # addFolderNode(nodes, folder) makes one node for the folder and then calls itself for every subfolder: that is recursion , the natural way to walk a tree. Folders are sorted so the order is stable. The important line: oNode.Tag = cFolder Tag is a free property that can hold anything : here the full path of the fol"}, {"u": "tut-42-browser.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 42: A folder browser", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # recursion builds a tree, Tag carries data on a node, ListView details mode, selection events fill the other control."}, {"u": "tut-42-browser.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 42: A folder browser", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Show an icon for folders and files (an ImageList ). Sort the list when the user clicks a column header ( ColumnClick ). Open a file with a double-click ( Process.Start with UseShellExecute = True ). Add a Refresh button that reloads the tree. The full-size version is the FileExplorer application."}, {"u": "tut-42-browser.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 42: A folder browser", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 43: Calendars and dates ."}, {"u": "tut-43-calendar.html", "t": "Tutorial 43: Calendars and dates", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-43-calendar.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 43: Calendars and dates", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # DaysInMonth , DayOfWeek , DayOfYear , adding months and years safely, computing an age, business days and the next birthday, Subtract and TimeSpan ."}, {"u": "tut-43-calendar.html#why-not-do-it-yourself", "t": "Tutorial 43: Calendars and dates", "h": "Why not do it yourself", "x": "Why not do it yourself # Imagine adding one month to 31 January by hand. Is it 31 February? Does the answer depend on the year? DateTime knows all of this. The rule of this lesson: **let DateTime do the calendar arithmetic**; you only decide what to ask."}, {"u": "tut-43-calendar.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 43: Calendars and dates", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 43: calendars - month tables, ages, business days and birthdays with DateTime load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" } func main # A text calendar of a month: how many days it has, and on which weekday the 1st falls ? monthCalendar(2024,2) # 2. How old is somebody? Years, months and days between two dates ? \"Age of a person born on 1990-03-15, on 2024-03-14: \" + ageText(1990,3,15,2024,3,14) ? \" and on 2024-03-15 : \" + ageText(1990,3,15,2024,3,15) # 3. Days between dates, and business days (Monday to Friday) oFrom = new DateTime(2024,12,20) oTo = new DateTime(2025,1,6) ? n"}, {"u": "tut-43-calendar.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 43: Calendars and dates", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The month calendar # DaysInMonth(year, month) gives the number of days (29 for February 2024). DayOfWeek of the first day tells in which column it starts: the week starts on Monday, so the column number is the position of the weekday name in the list Monday ... Sunday , minus one. The program then prints the days with right(\" \" + day,2) so that the numbers line up, and starts a new line after the seventh column. Age # An age is complete years, months and days . The simple subtraction year - birthYear is wrong before the birthday. The method: subtract years, months and days separ"}, {"u": "tut-43-calendar.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 43: Calendars and dates", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # let DateTime do the calendar work, ages need borrowing, business days need a loop and a weekday check, additions clamp to the end of the month."}, {"u": "tut-43-calendar.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 43: Calendars and dates", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Print the calendar of the current month ( DateTime.Now ) and mark today with [ ] . Find the next Friday the 13th after today. Add a list of holidays to businessDays and skip them. Write isLeapYear(year) by your own rule and test it against DateTime.IsLeapYear ."}, {"u": "tut-43-calendar.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 43: Calendars and dates", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 44: Statistics and simulation ."}, {"u": "tut-44-statistics.html", "t": "Tutorial 44: Statistics and simulation", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-44-statistics.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 44: Statistics and simulation", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the common statistics and how to compute them, writing each formula as a function, cross-checking with LINQ, the sieve of Eratosthenes, a Monte Carlo simulation with a seeded Random ."}, {"u": "tut-44-statistics.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 44: Statistics and simulation", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 44: numbers - statistics, primes and a simulation (Monte Carlo estimate of pi) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Linq\" } func main aData = [4,8,15,16,23,42,8,15,8,4] # 1. The usual summaries of a set of numbers ? \"Data : \" + joinList(aData) ? \"Count, sum : \" + len(aData) + \", \" + sumOf(aData) ? \"Mean (average) : \" + mean(aData) ? \"Median (middle): \" + median(aData) ? \"Mode (common) : \" + mode(aData) ? \"Smallest, largest: \" + min(aData) + \", \" + max(aData) ? \"Standard deviation: \" + stdDev(aData) # 2. .NET gives the same answer with LINQ: a check of our own co"}, {"u": "tut-44-statistics.html#the-statistics", "t": "Tutorial 44: Statistics and simulation", "h": "The statistics", "x": "The statistics # Name Meaning Function Mean the average: sum \u00f7 count mean Median the middle value of the sorted list (the average of the two middle ones when the count is even) median Mode the value that occurs most often mode Standard deviation how far values are from the mean, typically: the square root of the average squared distance stdDev For the data 4 8 15 16 23 42 8 15 8 4 (sorted: 4 4 8 8 8 15 15 16 23 42 ): the mean is 14.3 ; the median : there are ten values, so the middle is the pair at positions 5 and 6, which are 8 and 15 : (8 + 15) / 2 = 11.5 ; the mode is 8 (it occurs three tim"}, {"u": "tut-44-statistics.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 44: Statistics and simulation", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # One function per formula # Each statistic is a small function with one job that takes a list and returns a number. You can test each alone (see tutorial 28 ), reuse them, and the main program reads like a description of the results. The program also asks LINQ for the average ( dnQuery(aData).Average(\"\") ) and gets the same 14.3 : when two independent ways agree, you can trust the code. The histogram # A histogram counts how often each value occurs and shows it as a bar of # characters. The program collects the distinct values ( find prevents duplicates), sorts them, and for each"}, {"u": "tut-44-statistics.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 44: Statistics and simulation", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # mean, median, mode, standard deviation, histograms from counts, the sieve, gcd, lcm, simulation with a seeded Random , verify a result with a second method."}, {"u": "tut-44-statistics.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 44: Statistics and simulation", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Compute the 90th percentile of a list. Simulate 1000 throws of two dice and print the histogram of the sums. Find all perfect numbers below 10000 (a number that equals the sum of its divisors). Estimate the probability that two random people in a group of 23 share a birthday (the birthday paradox) with 10000 simulated groups."}, {"u": "tut-44-statistics.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 44: Statistics and simulation", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 45: Binary files ."}, {"u": "tut-45-binary.html", "t": "Tutorial 45: Binary files", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-45-binary.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 45: Binary files", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the difference between text and binary files, FileStream , BinaryWriter , BinaryReader , designing a small file format with a magic number, a hex dump, bytes, little endian and the type that you pass, closing files in every case."}, {"u": "tut-45-binary.html#text-and-binary", "t": "Tutorial 45: Binary files", "h": "Text and binary", "x": "Text and binary # A text file is a list of characters (encoded as bytes). A binary file is a list of bytes with a meaning that only the format tells: the first four bytes are a number, the next ones a name... A program that does not know the format sees garbage. A binary format is smaller and faster to read, and exact (a number is stored as a number, not as the text 21.5 )."}, {"u": "tut-45-binary.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 45: Binary files", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 45: binary files - bytes, BinaryWriter and BinaryReader, a file format, and a hex dump load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Text\" } func main cFile = new Path().Combine([new Path().GetTempPath(),\"tutorial45.dat\"]) # 1. A binary file stores values as bytes, not as readable text. We invent a tiny format: # the text \"RDN1\" (a \"magic number\": it says what kind of file this is), then # a 32-bit whole number (the count), a text (the title), and that many decimal numbers. # Note: Write(21.5) writes a decimal number (8 bytes); Write(23) would write a whole n"}, {"u": "tut-45-binary.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 45: Binary files", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The format # We invented it: first the text RDN1 , then the count (3) as a 32-bit whole number, the title as a text, then that many decimal numbers. Any reader must follow exactly the same order . The BinaryWriter writes each value in a fixed, compact way, and the BinaryReader reads them back with matching calls: ReadString , ReadInt32 , ReadDouble . Reading the hex dump # Each line of the dump shows an offset (position), eight bytes in hexadecimal, and the same bytes as characters ( . for unprintable ones). Read the real file: 0000 04 52 44 4e 31 03 00 00 .RDN1... 04 is the len"}, {"u": "tut-45-binary.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 45: Binary files", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # binary files are bytes with a format, BinaryWriter / BinaryReader read and write in the same order, a hex dump shows the truth, little endian, magic numbers, always close."}, {"u": "tut-45-binary.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 45: Binary files", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a version number to the format, and make the reader accept versions 1 and 2. Write a list of names (count, then each name) and read it back. Extend hexDump to take a start offset and a length. Read the first 8 bytes of a PNG file and check that they are 89 50 4E 47 0D 0A 1A 0A ."}, {"u": "tut-45-binary.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 45: Binary files", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 46: A project: a backup tool ."}, {"u": "tut-46-backup.html", "t": "Tutorial 46: A project: a backup tool", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-46-backup.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 46: A project: a backup tool", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # zipping and unzipping folders, names that sort in time order, keeping the newest N files (retention), restoring and verifying, the shape of a program that is safe to run again and again."}, {"u": "tut-46-backup.html#the-design", "t": "Tutorial 46: A project: a backup tool", "h": "The design", "x": "The design # Need Decision a backup must not overwrite the previous one every backup has its own file: backup-<date and time>.zip the files should be easy to find and to sort the stamp is year-month-day_hourminute : sorting the names sorts by time disk space is limited keep only the newest N (here 3) and delete the older ones a backup that was never restored is not a backup the program restores the newest one and checks it"}, {"u": "tut-46-backup.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 46: A project: a backup tool", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 46: a project - a backup tool (zip a folder, name by date, keep the newest few, restore) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.IO.Compression\" } func main oPath = new Path() oDirectory = new Directory() oFile = new File() cWork = oPath.Combine([oPath.GetTempPath(),\"tutorial46\"]) if oDirectory.Exists(cWork) oDirectory.Delete([ :path = cWork, :recursive = True ]) ok cSource = oPath.Combine([cWork,\"documents\"]) cBackups = oPath.Combine([cWork,\"backups\"]) oDirectory.CreateDirectory(oPath.Combine([cSource,\"notes\"])) oDirectory.CreateDirectory(cBackups) oFi"}, {"u": "tut-46-backup.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 46: A project: a backup tool", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Names that sort # 2024-05-03_0900 sorts correctly as text because the biggest unit comes first and every part has a fixed width ( 05 , not 5 ). That is why sort(list of names) puts the oldest backup first. A stamp like 3-5-2024 would sort wrongly. A real program makes the stamp from the clock: new DateTime().Now.Format(\"yyyy-MM-dd_HHmm\") ; the tutorial passes fixed stamps so that the output is repeatable. Making a backup # ZipFile.CreateFromDirectory(folder, zipFile) compresses the folder, with its subfolders, into a zip. It raises an error if the zip file already exists: anothe"}, {"u": "tut-46-backup.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 46: A project: a backup tool", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # zip with ZipFile , fixed-width date stamps sort as text, retention by deleting from the front of a sorted list, restore and verify."}, {"u": "tut-46-backup.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 46: A project: a backup tool", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Use the real clock for the stamp and run the program twice. Skip a backup when no file changed since the last one (compare file dates or hashes: tutorial 31 ). Put the list of folders to back up in a settings file ( tutorial 32 ). Add a window with a Back up now button and a list of the backups."}, {"u": "tut-46-backup.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 46: A project: a backup tool", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 47: A project: Markdown to HTML ."}, {"u": "tut-47-markdown.html", "t": "Tutorial 47: A project: Markdown to HTML", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-47-markdown.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 47: A project: Markdown to HTML", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # converting one text format into another, a line-by-line scan with a little state, inline formatting with regular expressions, why you escape first, a Ring feature to know: using objects during definition, x = new Class().Method(x) ."}, {"u": "tut-47-markdown.html#markdown-in-one-minute", "t": "Tutorial 47: A project: Markdown to HTML", "h": "Markdown in one minute", "x": "Markdown in one minute # Markdown is a way to write formatted text with plain characters: # Title is a heading, **bold** , *italic* , code between back-quotes, - item a list item, [text](address) a link. Many sites turn it into HTML."}, {"u": "tut-47-markdown.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 47: A project: Markdown to HTML", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 47: a project - a small Markdown to HTML converter (regular expressions, StringBuilder, files) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Text\" \"System.Text.RegularExpressions\" } func main cMarkdown = \"# Shopping\" + nl + \"\" + nl + \"Things to buy **today** for the *party*, see [the list](http://example.com/list).\" + nl + \"\" + nl + \"## Food\" + nl + \"- bread\" + nl + \"- cheese & `olives`\" + nl + \"- <fresh> fruit\" + nl + \"\" + nl + \"Thank you!\" ? \"---- Markdown ----\" ? cMarkdown cHtml = markdownToHtml(cMarkdown) ? nl + \"---- HTML ----\" ? cHtml # save it as a com"}, {"u": "tut-47-markdown.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 47: A project: Markdown to HTML", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Two levels # A document has blocks (headings, paragraphs, lists) and, inside the blocks, inline parts (bold, links). The program handles them separately: markdownToHtml decides what each line is, inlineHtml formats what is inside a line. Blocks: a scan with state # The function goes through the lines. The one thing it must remember is whether it is inside a list : the first - line opens <ul> , further items continue it, and the first line that is not an item (or an empty line) closes it with </ul> . Headings are recognised by the number of # : check ### first, then ## , then # ,"}, {"u": "tut-47-markdown.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 47: A project: Markdown to HTML", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # block-level and inline conversion are separate jobs, state for lists, StringBuilder for output, escape before formatting, lazy regexes, ordering of the steps, with x = new Class().Method(x) the variable already holds the new object (using objects during definition): use another variable."}, {"u": "tut-47-markdown.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 47: A project: Markdown to HTML", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add numbered lists ( 1. item ) and <ol> . Add > quote lines as <blockquote> . Add code blocks between three backticks lines. Read a .md file from disk and write the matching .html file."}, {"u": "tut-47-markdown.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 47: A project: Markdown to HTML", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 48: A project: an address book with a database ."}, {"u": "tut-48-addressbook.html", "t": "Tutorial 48: A project: an address book with a database", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-48-addressbook.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 48: A project: an address book with a database", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # connecting a database to a window, a DataGridView as a view of the database (not the data itself), searching with SQL parameters while typing, keeping the grid, the fields and the database consistent, tests for a complete application."}, {"u": "tut-48-addressbook.html#the-design", "t": "Tutorial 48: A project: an address book with a database", "h": "The design", "x": "The design # Three layers, as in tutorial 35 : Layer Holds Database ( oDb ) the truth: the contacts, stored in a SQLite file Grid data ( oTable ) a copy of the rows that match the search, for display Window the grid, the search box, the fields and the buttons The golden rule: the database changes first, then the grid is reloaded . The grid is never edited directly, so it can never disagree with the database."}, {"u": "tut-48-addressbook.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 48: A project: an address book with a database", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 48: a project - an address book with a database (Windows Forms + Entity Framework + a data grid) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Data\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" \"RingDotNet.Data\" } oForm = \"\" oDb = \"\" oTable = \"\" # a DataTable: what the grid shows, filled from the database oGrid = \"\" oName = \"\" oPhone = \"\" oCity = \"\" oSearch = \"\" oStatus = \"\" func main # THE DATABASE: a file next to the program (a test uses memory, so that it leaves nothing behind) cConnection = \"Data Source=addressbook.db\" if uiSelfTest() cConnection = \"Data Source=:memory:\" ok oDb = "}, {"u": "tut-48-addressbook.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 48: A project: an address book with a database", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The database # A table Contacts with a required Name (at most 60 characters), Phone and City . The program uses a file addressbook.db next to it, so the contacts are there the next time; a test uses memory ( :memory: ) so it leaves nothing behind. ( Tutorial 17 explains the schema.) The grid and reload # DataGridView.DataSource = oTable shows a DataTable . reload() is the heart of the program: it clears the table, asks the database for the rows that match the search text, and adds one row per contact. It is called at the start and after every change. The status line says 3 of 4 "}, {"u": "tut-48-addressbook.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 48: A project: an address book with a database", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # the database is the truth, the grid is a view, one reload() after every change, search with LIKE and parameters, DataBindingComplete for column settings, test through the same controls the user uses."}, {"u": "tut-48-addressbook.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 48: A project: an address book with a database", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Sort the contacts by name ( [\"orderby\",...] with query , tutorial 17 ). After an update, select the row with the same key again. Add a Notes table with several notes per contact and show them in a second grid ( tutorial 27 ). Export the contacts to CSV ( tutorial 30 )."}, {"u": "tut-48-addressbook.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 48: A project: an address book with a database", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 49: Login, sessions and cookies ."}, {"u": "tut-49-login.html", "t": "Tutorial 49: Login, sessions and cookies", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-49-login.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 49: Login, sessions and cookies", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # why web servers need sessions, storing users with salted password hashes, creating and checking a session token, reading and setting cookies, the right status codes: 401 , 409 , 400 , security details that matter."}, {"u": "tut-49-login.html#the-problem", "t": "Tutorial 49: Login, sessions and cookies", "h": "The problem", "x": "The problem # Every HTTP request stands alone: the server does not know that the request for /profile comes from the person who logged in a minute ago. The solution is a session : the user logs in with a name and password, the server creates a long random token and remembers token \u2192 user , it sends the token to the browser in a cookie ( Set-Cookie ), the browser sends the cookie back with every later request ( Cookie ), the server finds the user by the token."}, {"u": "tut-49-login.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 49: Login, sessions and cookies", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 49: login, sessions and cookies - a web site that knows who you are load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Net.Http\" \"System.Security.Cryptography\" \"System.Text\" \"RingDotNet.Web\" \"RingDotNet.Data\" } oServer = \"\" oClient = \"\" cUrl = \"\" oDb = \"\" aSessions = [] # [token, user name]: who is logged in func main # THE USERS: a table; the password is never stored, only a salted slow hash of it (tutorial 8) oDb = new RingDatabase(\"Data Source=:memory:\",[ [\"Users\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Name\",\"string\",0,[[\"required\",1],[\"unique\",1]]],[\"Salt\",\"string\",0],[\"Hash\",\"string\",0]]] ])"}, {"u": "tut-49-login.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 49: Login, sessions and cookies", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Registering # registerUser reads the form fields. It refuses a missing name or a short password with 400 , creates a random salt , and stores the name, the salt and a hash of the password ( PBKDF2 , tutorial 8 ) \u2014 never the password itself. The unique option of the name column makes the database refuse a duplicate, which the handler turns into 409 Conflict . Logging in # loginUser looks up the name, hashes the typed password with that user's salt and compares it with the stored hash. If the name does not exist or the password is wrong, the answer is the same : 401 wrong name or "}, {"u": "tut-49-login.html#security-notes", "t": "Tutorial 49: Login, sessions and cookies", "h": "Security notes", "x": "Security notes # Do Because hash passwords with a salt and many rounds a stolen database must not reveal passwords long random tokens from RandomNumberGenerator a guessable token is a stolen account the same answer for a wrong name and a wrong password do not reveal who has an account HttpOnly cookies scripts cannot steal them use HTTPS (not shown here) and add Secure to the cookie on plain HTTP the cookie travels readable expire sessions after some time a forgotten login should not live forever escape text put into pages ( tutorial 26 ) otherwise a script can act as the user Warning This is a"}, {"u": "tut-49-login.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 49: Login, sessions and cookies", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # sessions turn separate requests into a conversation, token \u2192 user, token in a cookie, store hashes, never passwords, 401 / 409 / 400 , the cookie header in both directions."}, {"u": "tut-49-login.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 49: Login, sessions and cookies", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add an expiry time to each session (a date) and refuse expired ones. Limit failed logins: after five wrong passwords for a name, answer 429 for a minute. Add a /change-password route that needs the old password. Store the sessions in the database so that they survive a restart."}, {"u": "tut-49-login.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 49: Login, sessions and cookies", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 50: A command-line tool"}, {"u": "tut-50-cli.html", "t": "Tutorial 50: A command-line tool", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-50-cli.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 50: A command-line tool", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # reading the arguments of a program ( sysargv ), options with and without values ( --top 5 , --help ), printing a usage text and clear error messages, exit codes: 0 success, non-zero a problem, keeping the logic in a function that is easy to test."}, {"u": "tut-50-cli.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 50: A command-line tool", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 51: a command-line tool - arguments, options, usage text and results # ring t51_cli.ring runs a demonstration of several command lines # ring t51_cli.ring file.txt --top 5 is a real use of the tool: the 5 most frequent words of file.txt load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.IO\" \"System.Text.RegularExpressions\" } func main # the arguments after the program name: sysargv = [ring, program, arg1, arg2, ...] (the first two are not ours) aArguments = [] for nIndex = 3 to len(sysargv) aArguments + sysargv[nIndex] next if len(aArguments) > 0 nCode = run(aArguments) ? \"(exit code "}, {"u": "tut-50-cli.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 50: A command-line tool", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # sysargv holds [ring, program, arg1, arg2, ...] , so your arguments start at item 3. run(aArguments) does everything and returns an exit code instead of stopping the program: the demonstration calls it with several lists of arguments, a real use passes the real ones. This is the trick that makes a command-line tool easy to test. parseArguments walks the list. A word that starts with -- is an option; --top and --min take the next word as their value, and it is checked with a regular expression ( ^[0-9]+$ ). Any other word is the file. A mistake prints one clear line ( error: --top"}, {"u": "tut-50-cli.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 50: A command-line tool", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # arguments, options and values, usage text and error lines, exit codes, a run function that is easy to test."}, {"u": "tut-50-cli.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 50: A command-line tool", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add --ignore-case false to count The and the separately. Add --sort alpha to sort by word instead of by count. Read the text from the keyboard when no file is given."}, {"u": "tut-50-cli.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 50: A command-line tool", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 51: Logging"}, {"u": "tut-51-logging.html", "t": "Tutorial 51: Logging", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-51-logging.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 51: Logging", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what to log and at which level, appending lines to a file, the minimum level (development versus production), logging an error you caught, rotation: a log must not grow forever."}, {"u": "tut-51-logging.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 51: Logging", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 52: logging - levels, timestamps, a file, rotation, and what to write load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" } func main cFolder = new Path().Combine([new Path().GetTempPath(),\"tutorial52\"]) if new Directory().Exists(cFolder) new Directory().Delete([ :path = cFolder, :recursive = True ]) ok new Directory().CreateDirectory(cFolder) cFile = new Path().Combine([cFolder,\"app.log\"]) # 1. A logger writes lines: time, level, message. The level says how important a line is. oLog = new Logger(cFile) oLog.setClock(\"2024-05-17 08:00:00\") # a fixed time: the output of the"}, {"u": "tut-51-logging.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 51: Logging", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # A line has a time, a level and a message. The level says how important it is: DEBUG (details for the developer), INFO (normal events), WARN (something is wrong but the program goes on), ERROR (an operation failed). The logger has a minimum level : lines below it are not written, so step 1 skips the debug line, and step 2 shows everything and then only errors. File.AppendAllText adds a line at the end of the file. The clock can be fixed with setClock so that the output of the tutorial is the same every run; a real logger leaves it empty and uses the real time. Step 3 shows the ma"}, {"u": "tut-51-logging.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 51: Logging", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # levels and a minimum level, AppendAllText , logging caught errors, rotation with FileInfo and File.Move ."}, {"u": "tut-51-logging.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 51: Logging", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Keep three old files ( .1 , .2 , .3 ). Add a fatal level and a --verbose option like in tutorial 50 . Write a log line for each request of the web server in tutorial 26 ."}, {"u": "tut-51-logging.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 51: Logging", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 52: Unicode and encodings"}, {"u": "tut-52-unicode.html", "t": "Tutorial 52: Unicode and encodings", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-52-unicode.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 52: Unicode and encodings", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the difference between bytes, code points and the letters you see, UTF-8 and UTF-16 and what Encoding does, why reading with the wrong encoding gives garbage, why two texts that look the same can be different, asking .NET what kind of character something is."}, {"u": "tut-52-unicode.html#the-three-levels", "t": "Tutorial 52: Unicode and encodings", "h": "The three levels", "x": "The three levels # A byte is what is stored in a file. A code point is the number Unicode gives to a character ( \u00e9 is 233, an emoji is 128512). A text element is what you see as one letter. It can be several code points: e plus a combining accent looks like \u00e9 . An encoding turns text into bytes. UTF-8 uses 1 byte for English letters and up to 4 for others; it is the standard for files and the web. UTF-16 is what .NET uses inside. A Ring text holds UTF-8 bytes, so len() counts bytes ."}, {"u": "tut-52-unicode.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 52: Unicode and encodings", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 53: Unicode and text encodings - characters, code points, bytes, and why len() is not the number of letters load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Globalization\" \"System.Text\" } func main oUtf8 = new Encoding().UTF8 oUtf16 = new Encoding().Unicode # .NET's own text format: UTF-16 oLatin1 = new Encoding().Latin1 ? \"Ring len() | UTF-8 bytes | UTF-16 bytes | letters you see | text\" aSamples = [\"Ring\",\"caf\u00e9\",\"\u041f\u0440\u0438\u0432\u0435\u0442\",\"\u0645\u0631\u062d\u0628\u0627\",\"\u65e5\u672c\u8a9e\",\"\ud83d\ude00\"] for cText in aSamples oInfo = textElements(cText) ? padRight(\"\" + len(cText),11) + \" | \" + padRight(\"\" + len(oUtf8.GetBytes(cText)),11"}, {"u": "tut-52-unicode.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 52: Unicode and encodings", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The table compares four counts for six texts. For Ring they are all the same; for caf\u00e9 the byte count is 5 but you see 4 letters; Russian and Arabic use two bytes per letter; Japanese three; an emoji four (two units in UTF-16). StringInfo.LengthInTextElements counts what a person sees. A constructor with an argument is written dnNew(\"StringInfo\",[ :value = cText ]) . Step 2 turns \u00e9 into two bytes (195 and 169) and reads them again as Latin-1 : the result is \u00c3\u00a9 , the classic mojibake . The rule: the encoding that wrote the bytes must be the one that reads them . When you read tex"}, {"u": "tut-52-unicode.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 52: Unicode and encodings", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # bytes, code points, text elements, Encoding.UTF8 , Unicode , Latin1 , GetBytes , GetString , mojibake and how to avoid it, comparing text in a culture-aware way, CharUnicodeInfo.GetUnicodeCategory ."}, {"u": "tut-52-unicode.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 52: Unicode and encodings", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write a text to a file in UTF-8 and in UTF-16 and compare the file sizes. Count the letters of your own name with len() and with StringInfo . Find out what Encoding.ASCII.GetBytes(\"\u00e9\") returns, and why."}, {"u": "tut-52-unicode.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 52: Unicode and encodings", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 53: Speed \u2014 measure first"}, {"u": "tut-53-speed.html", "t": "Tutorial 53: Speed \u2014 measure first", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-53-speed.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 53: Speed \u2014 measure first", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # measuring time with Stopwatch , why you must measure instead of assuming, a cache ( memoization ) that turns a slow function into a fast one, the cost of a call between Ring and .NET, and how to make fewer calls."}, {"u": "tut-53-speed.html#the-rules-of-speed", "t": "Tutorial 53: Speed \u2014 measure first", "h": "The rules of speed", "x": "The rules of speed # Make it work, then make it right, then make it fast \u2014 and only if it is too slow. Measure. The slow part is rarely the part you suspect. The biggest wins come from doing less work (a cache, a better algorithm, fewer calls), not from clever tricks."}, {"u": "tut-53-speed.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 53: Speed \u2014 measure first", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 54: speed - measure first, then choose: Stopwatch, StringBuilder, a cache (memoization) and fewer calls load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Diagnostics\" \"System.Text\" \"System.Collections.Generic\" } nCalls = 0 nCachedCalls = 0 aCache = [] func main # 1. Measure with a Stopwatch. A guess about speed is often wrong. oWatch = new Stopwatch() oWatch.Start() nSum = 0 for nI = 1 to 200000 nSum += nI next oWatch.Stop() ? \"1. A loop of 200000 additions gives \" + nSum + \" (measured: \" + (oWatch.ElapsedMilliseconds >= 0) + \")\" # 2. Building a big text: Ring's own += again"}, {"u": "tut-53-speed.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 53: Speed \u2014 measure first", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Stopwatch # new Stopwatch() , Start() , Stop() , and then ElapsedMilliseconds or Elapsed.TotalMilliseconds . The tutorial prints comparisons (\"which is faster?\") and not the times, so that the output is the same on every computer; when you try your own code, print the numbers. Ring's += against StringBuilder # In C# a StringBuilder is the standard answer to building a long text. In Ring the answer is different: the += of Ring is already efficient, and each Append of a StringBuilder is a call across the bridge . Here the plain Ring version wins. This is the reason for rule 2: adv"}, {"u": "tut-53-speed.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 53: Speed \u2014 measure first", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Stopwatch and measuring, the result may be the opposite of what you expect, a cache saves repeated work, one call for many items beats many calls."}, {"u": "tut-53-speed.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 53: Speed \u2014 measure first", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Measure File.ReadAllLines against StreamReader.ReadLine in a loop on a 10,000-line file. Add a time limit to fibCached 's cache: forget answers older than a minute. Time new Regex(pattern) created inside a loop and created once before the loop."}, {"u": "tut-53-speed.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 53: Speed \u2014 measure first", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 54: An expression calculator"}, {"u": "tut-54-expression.html", "t": "Tutorial 54: An expression calculator", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-54-expression.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 54: An expression calculator", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the two steps of every language tool: tokens , then grammar , how a parser respects precedence ( * before + ) and parentheses, writing a grammar as a few short rules and one function per rule, error messages that say what is wrong, using the .NET Math class for functions like sqrt ."}, {"u": "tut-54-expression.html#the-grammar", "t": "Tutorial 54: An expression calculator", "h": "The grammar", "x": "The grammar # Each rule becomes one function. A rule only calls the rules below it, and that is how precedence works: the rule for + and - calls the rule for * and / to get its operands, so the multiplications are done first. expression = term { (\"+\" | \"-\") term } term = power { (\"*\" | \"/\") power } power = unary [ \"^\" power ] unary = \"-\" unary | primary primary = number | \"(\" expression \")\" | name \"(\" expression { \",\" expression } \")\" { ... } means \"zero or more times\", [ ... ] \"optional\". Parentheses return to the top rule, so (1 + 2) * 3 works. power calls itself on the right side, which mak"}, {"u": "tut-54-expression.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 54: An expression calculator", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 55: an expression calculator - tokens, a recursive-descent parser, precedence and clear errors load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text.RegularExpressions\" } func main aTests = [\"1 + 2 * 3\",\"(1 + 2) * 3\",\"10 / 4 - 1\",\"2 ^ 3 ^ 2\",\"-(2 + 3) * 4\",\"sqrt(16) + max(3, 9)\",\"2 * (3 + \",\"4 $ 4\",\"1 / 0\"] for cText in aTests oCalc = new Evaluator() ? padRight(cText,24) + \" = \" + oCalc.calculate(cText) next func padRight cText, nWidth while len(cText) < nWidth cText += \" \" end return cText # Step 1: the text becomes tokens (numbers, names, operators). Step 2: the parser re"}, {"u": "tut-54-expression.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 54: An expression calculator", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Tokens # tokenize uses one regular expression, \\s*(\\d+\\.?\\d*|[A-Za-z]+|[-+*/^(),]|\\S) : skip spaces, then take a number, a name, an operator \u2014 or any other character, which is reported as unknown character '$' . Regex.Matches gives all the pieces; Groups.Item(1).Value is the piece without the spaces. The parser # aTokens is the list and nPos the position. peek() looks at the next token without taking it; the functions expression , term , power , unary and primary follow the grammar one to one. expect(\")\") checks that the closing parenthesis is there. Errors # When something is w"}, {"u": "tut-54-expression.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 54: An expression calculator", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # tokens and grammar, one function per grammar rule, precedence through the order of the rules, clear error messages, Math functions from a parser."}, {"u": "tut-54-expression.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 54: An expression calculator", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add % (remainder) at the same level as * and / . Add variables: x = 5 stores a value and later expressions can use x . Show the position of the error (the number of the token) in each message."}, {"u": "tut-54-expression.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 54: An expression calculator", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 55: A tiny web framework"}, {"u": "tut-55-miniweb.html", "t": "Tutorial 55: A tiny web framework", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-55-miniweb.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 55: A tiny web framework", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # routes with parameters : /users/:name/posts/:id , one catch-all handler ( notFound ) that decides with a router, a template with {{name}} places and automatic escaping, calling a function by its name ( call ), testing the whole thing in one program."}, {"u": "tut-55-miniweb.html#the-idea", "t": "Tutorial 55: A tiny web framework", "h": "The idea", "x": "The idea # WebServer maps a fixed address to a handler. A real site has addresses with changing parts: /users/ali , /users/sara/posts/7 . Instead of one route for each user, a framework keeps a pattern ( /users/:name ) and, for each request, finds the pattern that fits and extracts the parameters ( name = ali )."}, {"u": "tut-55-miniweb.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 55: A tiny web framework", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 56: a tiny web framework - routes with parameters (/users/:id), templates ({{name}}) and one catch-all handler load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Net\" \"System.Net.Http\" \"RingDotNet.Web\" } oServer = \"\" oClient = \"\" oRouter = \"\" func main oServer = new WebServer(\"http://127.0.0.1:0\") oRouter = new Router() oRouter.add(\"GET\",\"/\",\"homePage\") oRouter.add(\"GET\",\"/users/:name\",\"userPage\") oRouter.add(\"GET\",\"/users/:name/posts/:id\",\"postPage\") oServer.notFound(\"dispatch\") # every request that has no ordinary route comes here: the router decides oClient = new HttpClient() oClie"}, {"u": "tut-55-miniweb.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 55: A tiny web framework", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The router # Router.add(method, pattern, handlerName) stores the pattern split into parts. find(method, path) splits the real path too, and compares part by part: a part that starts with : matches anything and is saved as a parameter, any other part must be equal. A path with a different number of parts can never match. The result is [handler, parameters] \u2014 or an empty list. One handler for everything # oServer.notFound(\"dispatch\") registers a handler for every request that has no ordinary route. Here that is every request, so dispatch is the only entry of the application: it as"}, {"u": "tut-55-miniweb.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 55: A tiny web framework", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # patterns with :parameters , a router that finds the page and the values, notFound as the single entry point, templates with escaping, call for a function name."}, {"u": "tut-55-miniweb.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 55: A tiny web framework", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a route POST /users/:name for a form, and look at oRequest.form(\"field\") . Support a * part that matches the rest of the path ( /files/* ). Put the templates in files in a views folder and read them with File.ReadAllText ."}, {"u": "tut-55-miniweb.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 55: A tiny web framework", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 56: Streams and compression"}, {"u": "tut-56-streams.html", "t": "Tutorial 56: Streams and compression", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-56-streams.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 56: Streams and compression", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a stream is and why .NET uses it everywhere, MemoryStream , StreamWriter and StreamReader , Flush , Position and Dispose , compressing and decompressing with GZipStream , the same code for memory and for files."}, {"u": "tut-56-streams.html#streams-in-one-minute", "t": "Tutorial 56: Streams and compression", "h": "Streams in one minute", "x": "Streams in one minute # A stream is like a pipe: bytes go in at one end and come out at the other. The same few classes work with every kind of pipe: Class Source or target MemoryStream memory FileStream a file ( File.Create , File.OpenRead ) NetworkStream a socket ( tutorial 25 ) GZipStream wraps another stream and compresses or decompresses what passes StreamWriter / StreamReader turn text into bytes and back Because they wrap each other you can stack them: text \u2192 StreamWriter \u2192 GZipStream \u2192 FileStream \u2192 disk."}, {"u": "tut-56-streams.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 56: Streams and compression", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 57: streams and compression - the same data in memory, in a file, and squeezed with GZip load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.IO.Compression\" \"System.Text\" } func main oUtf8 = new Encoding().UTF8 # 1. A stream is a flow of bytes. A MemoryStream keeps them in memory: no file is needed to learn the pattern. oMemory = new MemoryStream() oWriter = new StreamWriter(oMemory) oWriter.WriteLine(\"first line\") oWriter.WriteLine(\"second line\") oWriter.Flush() # the writer keeps a buffer: Flush sends it to the stream ? \"1. bytes in the memory stream: \" + oMemory"}, {"u": "tut-56-streams.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 56: Streams and compression", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Write, rewind, read # Step 1 writes two lines into a MemoryStream . A StreamWriter keeps a buffer, so Flush() sends it to the stream before you look at Length . Step 2 sets Position = 0 to go back to the start (after writing, the position is at the end) and reads the lines with a StreamReader . Compression # The text of step 3 repeats the same sentence 200 times: 9,000 bytes become 120. Compression removes repetition, so text compresses very well and already compressed data (a zip, a JPEG) does not. A GZipStream created with \"Compress\" compresses what you write into it; with \"De"}, {"u": "tut-56-streams.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 56: Streams and compression", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # streams as pipes that can be stacked, Flush , Position , Dispose , GZipStream with \"Compress\" and \"Decompress\" , dnNew(\"GZipStream\",[ :stream = ..., :mode = ..., :leaveOpen = True ]) for a constructor with three values."}, {"u": "tut-56-streams.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 56: Streams and compression", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Compress a text file to .gz and decompress it to a new file, then compare the two with a hash ( tutorial 8 ). Try ZipArchive to put several files in one zip. Compress a text of 200 different lines and compare the size with the repeated text."}, {"u": "tut-56-streams.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 56: Streams and compression", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 57: Money"}, {"u": "tut-57-money.html", "t": "Tutorial 57: Money", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-57-money.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 57: Money", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # why fractions are inexact in every computer language, the cents rule: whole numbers inside, decimals only for display, rounding rules: banker's rounding and school rounding, splitting an amount fairly, taxes on the total, not on each line."}, {"u": "tut-57-money.html#the-problem", "t": "Tutorial 57: Money", "h": "The problem", "x": "The problem # Computers store numbers in binary. Just as 1/3 cannot be written exactly in decimal (0.3333\u2026), 0.1 cannot be written exactly in binary, so 0.1 + 0.2 is 0.30000000000000004 and is not equal to 0.3 . Ten additions of 0.1 do not give exactly 1. For physics this is fine; for money it is not: a shop must never be one cent off."}, {"u": "tut-57-money.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 57: Money", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 58: money - why 0.1 + 0.2 is not 0.3, cents as whole numbers, rounding rules and fair splitting load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" } func main # 1. Computers store fractions in binary: 0.1 and 0.2 cannot be written exactly, so tiny errors appear ? \"1. 0.1 + 0.2 = 0.3 ? \" + yesNo((0.1 + 0.2) = 0.3) nTotal = 0 for nI = 1 to 10 nTotal += 0.1 next ? \" ten times 0.1 equals 1 ? \" + yesNo(nTotal = 1) # 2. The rule for money: keep whole CENTS (integers) and only turn them into \"12.50\" when you show them nPriceCents = toCents(\"19.99\") nTotalCents = nPriceCents * 3 ? nl + \"2. t"}, {"u": "tut-57-money.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 57: Money", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Cents as integers # Convert to whole cents when the amount comes in ( toCents(\"19.99\") \u2192 1999 ), calculate with integers (they are exact), and turn them back to text only for display ( showMoney ). Three items of 19.99 are 5997 cents: no error is possible. Note The .NET Decimal type is exact, but a decimal that crosses the bridge becomes a Ring number (a binary fraction again). In Ring, whole cents are the safe way. A database keeps money in integer columns for the same reason. Rounding # Percentages create parts of cents (7.5 % of 1999 is 149.925). You need a rule, and the rule"}, {"u": "tut-57-money.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 57: Money", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # binary fractions are inexact, whole cents for calculation, text for display, rounding modes, fair splitting, tax on the total."}, {"u": "tut-57-money.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 57: Money", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a discount of 15 % to the invoice and decide when to round. Write showMoneyIn(nCents, cCulture) using CultureInfo ( tutorial 33 ). Split 10.00 between 7 people and check the sum."}, {"u": "tut-57-money.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 57: Money", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 58: Undo and redo"}, {"u": "tut-58-undo.html", "t": "Tutorial 58: Undo, redo and queues", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-58-undo.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 58: Undo, redo and queues", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # stack : last in, first out; queue : first in, first out, Push , Pop , Peek , Enqueue , Dequeue , Count , building undo and redo with two stacks, what happens when you take from an empty collection."}, {"u": "tut-58-undo.html#two-ways-to-wait", "t": "Tutorial 58: Undo, redo and queues", "h": "Two ways to wait", "x": "Two ways to wait # A stack is a pile of plates: you put on top and take from the top. A queue is a line at a shop: the first to arrive is served first. Stack Queue add Push(x) Enqueue(x) take Pop() Dequeue() look Peek() Peek() size Count Count used for undo, the Back button, parsing, the call stack print jobs, tasks to do, messages The ready-made Ring classes are StringStack and StringQueue (see Collections )."}, {"u": "tut-58-undo.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 58: Undo, redo and queues", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 59: stacks and queues - undo/redo with two stacks, and a first-come-first-served job queue load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Collections.Generic\" } func main # A STACK: last in, first out (a pile of plates). A QUEUE: first in, first out (people waiting in a line). # 1. Stack basics oStack = new StringStack() oStack.Push(\"a\") oStack.Push(\"b\") oStack.Push(\"c\") ? \"1. Peek (look at the top): \" + oStack.Peek() + \", Pop (take it): \" + oStack.Pop() + \", now \" + oStack.Count + \" items, the top is \" + oStack.Peek() # 2. Undo and redo with two stacks: every change pushes the ol"}, {"u": "tut-58-undo.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 58: Undo, redo and queues", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Undo and redo # TextHistory keeps the current text and two stacks. Each change pushes the old text on the undo stack . undo pushes the current text on the redo stack and pops the previous text from the undo stack. redo does the reverse. The most recent change is always on top, which is exactly the order a user expects. A new change after an undo clears the redo stack ( Clear() ): the old future no longer exists. The last line of step 2 shows can redo now? no . The same idea works for a drawing program (push each shape), a browser (the Back and Forward buttons are two stacks), or"}, {"u": "tut-58-undo.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 58: Undo, redo and queues", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # stacks and queues and when to use each, undo and redo with two stacks, always check Count ."}, {"u": "tut-58-undo.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 58: Undo, redo and queues", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a limit of 100 undo steps: when the undo stack is full, drop the oldest entry (hint: use a list). Make a job queue with priorities : urgent jobs first (hint: two queues). Check that parentheses in (a + [b]) * c are balanced, using a stack."}, {"u": "tut-58-undo.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 58: Undo, redo and queues", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 59: Paths and the environment"}, {"u": "tut-59-paths.html", "t": "Tutorial 59: Paths and the environment", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-59-paths.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 59: Paths and the environment", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # building and taking apart paths with the Path class, relative and absolute paths, cleaning a text so that it is a valid file name, special folders (documents, temporary files), environment variables with a default value, how .NET null arrives in Ring."}, {"u": "tut-59-paths.html#why-not-add-strings", "t": "Tutorial 59: Paths and the environment", "h": "Why not add strings?", "x": "Why not add strings? # \"reports\" + \"\\\" + \"2024\" works on your computer only: other systems use / , and an extra separator or a missing one breaks the path. Path.Combine chooses the separator of the system and never doubles it."}, {"u": "tut-59-paths.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 59: Paths and the environment", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 60: paths and the environment - building paths safely, splitting them, special folders and environment variables load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" } func main oPath = new Path() # 1. Build a path with Combine, never with + \"\\\": Combine uses the right separator for the system and never doubles it cFile = oPath.Combine([\"reports\",\"2024\",\"january.txt\"]) ? \"1. Combine: \" + cFile ? \" Combine with a rooted second part restarts the path: \" + oPath.Combine([\"reports\",\"/etc/hosts\"]) # 2. Take a path apart cFull = \"C:\\data\\reports\\2024\\summary.final.txt\" ? nl + \"2."}, {"u": "tut-59-paths.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 59: Paths and the environment", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Combine and split # Path.Combine joins parts: give a list of the parts ( Combine([a, b, c]) ) or name them ( Combine([ :path1 = a, :path2 = b ]) ). Careful: if a later part is rooted (starts with a separator or a drive), the earlier parts are dropped. That is correct behaviour but a classic surprise when the part comes from user input. GetFileName , GetFileNameWithoutExtension , GetExtension , GetDirectoryName and ChangeExtension take a path apart or change one piece. Use them instead of searching for dots and slashes yourself: summary.final.txt has two dots, and only the last o"}, {"u": "tut-59-paths.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 59: Paths and the environment", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Path.Combine and the other Path methods, relative or absolute, valid file names, GetFolderPath , GetTempPath , environment variables and the null object."}, {"u": "tut-59-paths.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 59: Paths and the environment", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write uniqueName(cFolder, cName) that adds (2) , (3) to the name until the file does not exist. Read a variable MYAPP_FOLDER for the data folder, with the documents folder as the default. List the extensions found in a folder with how many files each has."}, {"u": "tut-59-paths.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 59: Paths and the environment", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 60: Watching sorting algorithms"}, {"u": "tut-60-sorting.html", "t": "Tutorial 60: Watching sorting algorithms", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-60-sorting.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 60: Watching sorting algorithms", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # how bubble sort, selection sort and insertion sort work, recording work as a list of steps and replaying it with a timer, drawing bars on a canvas and highlighting the two that are touched, counting comparisons and swaps to compare algorithms, a generator of random numbers with a fixed start."}, {"u": "tut-60-sorting.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 60: Watching sorting algorithms", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 61: watching sorting algorithms - an animation, a timer and counting the work load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } C_COUNT = 36 C_BAR = 12 C_HEIGHT = 300 oForm = \"\" oPicture = \"\" oCanvas = \"\" oAlgorithm = \"\" oInfo = \"\" oTimer = \"\" aValues = [] # the bars as they are shown now aSteps = [] # the work of the algorithm: [\"compare\", i, j] or [\"swap\", i, j] nStep = 0 nCompares = 0 nSwaps = 0 nSeed = 12345 aHot = [] # the two bars that are touched by the step being shown func main oForm = uiForm(\"Sorting\",C_COUNT * C_BAR + 30,C_HEIGHT + 100) oP"}, {"u": "tut-60-sorting.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 60: Watching sorting algorithms", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Record, then replay # A sorting algorithm is a loop that finishes in a moment: you could not see it. So the three functions bubbleSort , selectionSort and insertionSort work on a copy of the bars and, instead of drawing, record each step in aSteps : [\"compare\", 3, 4] or [\"swap\", 3, 4] . The timer then calls showStep every 15 milliseconds; each call takes the next recorded step, applies a swap to the bars on screen, adds to the counters and redraws. This separation \u2014 computing in one place and showing in another \u2014 also makes the self test easy: it runs all the steps without waiti"}, {"u": "tut-60-sorting.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 60: Watching sorting algorithms", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # how three sorting algorithms work and how they differ, recording steps and replaying them with a timer, the canvas for bars and highlights, measuring work with counters."}, {"u": "tut-60-sorting.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 60: Watching sorting algorithms", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a Shell sort or quicksort and compare the counters. Show a bar's value as text over the bar ( oCanvas.text ). Add a speed track bar that changes the timer interval. Count how many comparisons insertion sort needs for bars that are already sorted."}, {"u": "tut-60-sorting.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 60: Watching sorting algorithms", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 61: A maze"}, {"u": "tut-61-maze.html", "t": "Tutorial 61: A maze", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-61-maze.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 61: A maze", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # generating a maze with depth-first search and a stack, finding the shortest path with breadth-first search and a queue, storing a grid as a list of lists, drawing walls and a path on a canvas, testing an algorithm with properties instead of fixed answers."}, {"u": "tut-61-maze.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 61: A maze", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 62: a maze - make it with a stack (depth-first), solve it with a queue (breadth-first), draw it on a canvas load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } C_COLUMNS = 21 C_ROWS = 13 C_CELL = 26 oForm = \"\" oPicture = \"\" oCanvas = \"\" oInfo = \"\" aOpen = [] # aOpen[row][column] = a text of the open sides: \"N\", \"E\", \"S\", \"W\" in any combination aPath = [] # the solution: a list of [row, column] nSeed = 2024 func main oForm = uiForm(\"Maze\",C_COLUMNS * C_CELL + 30,C_ROWS * C_CELL + 100) oPicture = uiPictureBox(oForm,5,5,C_COLUMNS * C_CELL,C_ROWS * C_CELL)"}, {"u": "tut-61-maze.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 61: A maze", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The grid # The maze is C_ROWS rows of C_COLUMNS cells. aOpen[row][column] is a text with the open sides of the cell: \"N\" , \"E\" , \"S\" and \"W\" in any order (for example \"NE\" ). A cell that has no letter is closed on all sides. Walls are drawn for every side that is not in the text. Making the maze: depth-first with a stack # Start in the top left cell and walk: choose a random neighbour that has not been visited, open the wall between the two cells and move there, pushing the new cell on the stack . When a cell has no unvisited neighbour, go back by popping the stack. The algorith"}, {"u": "tut-61-maze.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 61: A maze", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # stack and queue in real algorithms, a grid as a list of lists, shortest path by breadth-first search, testing properties."}, {"u": "tut-61-maze.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 61: A maze", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a button that shows the maze being built, one step per timer tick ( tutorial 60 ). Remove some walls at random (a maze with loops) and check that the solver still finds the shortest way. Let the user move a dot with the arrow keys from the start to the exit."}, {"u": "tut-61-maze.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 61: A maze", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 62: An image filter lab"}, {"u": "tut-62-filters.html", "t": "Tutorial 62: An image filter lab", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-62-filters.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 62: An image filter lab", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # a picture as rows of [red, green, blue] numbers, filters as functions that take a pixel (or its neighbours) and return a new one, why per-pixel calls across the bridge are slow, and a faster way, the structure of a BMP file and writing it with Ring, little-endian numbers, showing an image in a PictureBox ."}, {"u": "tut-62-filters.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 62: An image filter lab", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 63: an image filter lab - pixels as numbers, filters as small functions, a BMP file written by hand, before and after side by side load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.IO\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } C_WIDTH = 120 C_HEIGHT = 90 oForm = \"\" oBefore = \"\" oAfter = \"\" oInfo = \"\" aSource = [] # the pixels of the original: aSource[y][x] = [red, green, blue] aResult = [] cFolder = \"\" nFile = 0 func main oForm = uiForm(\"Filter lab\",C_WIDTH * 2 + 60,C_HEIGHT + 190) oBefore = uiPictureBox(oForm,10,10,C_WIDTH,C_HEIGHT) oAfter = uiPictureBox(oForm,C_WIDTH + 40,10,C_WIDT"}, {"u": "tut-62-filters.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 62: An image filter lab", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Pixels as numbers # aSource[y][x] is [r, g, b] , each from 0 to 255. The sample picture is made by pictureColor , a function that returns the colour of a point: a sky that gets lighter near the horizon, a circle for the sun (a point is inside if (x \u2212 94)\u00b2 + (y \u2212 22)\u00b2 \u2264 14\u00b2 ), grass, a house and a roof. Everything is plain Ring, so it is fast. Filters # Most filters change each pixel on its own, so mapPixels applies a function to every pixel ( call cFunction(aPixel) ): Filter Rule Gray 0.299 r + 0.587 g + 0.114 b \u2014 the eye sees green brighter than blue Invert 255 \u2212 value for each"}, {"u": "tut-62-filters.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 62: An image filter lab", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # pixels as numbers and filters as functions, 3 \u00d7 3 neighbourhoods and writing to a new list, clamping, writing a BMP file and little-endian numbers, avoiding thousands of bridge calls."}, {"u": "tut-62-filters.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 62: An image filter lab", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a Threshold filter: black below 128 gray, white above. Add a Mirror filter that reverses each row. Make Blur stronger by repeating it three times. Add a button that saves the result with File.Copy to a file chosen with uiSaveFileName ."}, {"u": "tut-62-filters.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 62: An image filter lab", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 63: A chat over TCP"}, {"u": "tut-63-chat.html", "t": "Tutorial 63: A chat over TCP", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-63-chat.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 63: A chat over TCP", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the structure of a chat: one server, many clients, a text protocol with one message per line, why a window program must never wait for the network, reading the bytes that are available, and building lines from them, polling with a timer, testing network code with a pump and a short wait."}, {"u": "tut-63-chat.html#the-idea", "t": "Tutorial 63: A chat over TCP", "h": "The idea", "x": "The idea # The server listens on a port. Each client connects and sends lines such as Alice: Hello . The server sends every line it receives to all clients, including the sender, so everybody sees the same conversation in the same order. The protocol is the simplest possible: one message per line, the sender's name at the start. Tutorial 25 explains sockets; here they are used in a window program."}, {"u": "tut-63-chat.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 63: A chat over TCP", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 64: a chat over TCP - a server that relays lines, two clients, and a timer that polls the sockets load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Net\" \"System.Net.Sockets\" \"System.Threading\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } oForm = \"\" oServer = \"\" aClients = [] # the two chat clients aLogs = [] # the two text boxes that show the conversation aInputs = [] # the two text boxes where the user types oTimer = \"\" func main oServer = new ChatServer() nPort = oServer.start() oForm = uiForm(\"Chat on port \" + nPort,620,380) cNames = [\"Alice\",\"Bob\"] for nIndex = "}, {"u": "tut-63-chat.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 63: A chat over TCP", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Never wait in a window program # StreamReader.ReadLine() waits until a whole line arrives. In a window program that means the window freezes: it cannot repaint or answer clicks. Ring has one thread, so waiting is not allowed. The solution has two parts: Ask first. NetworkStream.DataAvailable says whether bytes are waiting. LineReader.lines() reads only the bytes that are there ( ReadByte ), adds them to a buffer, and returns a line each time it sees a line feed (byte 10). An unfinished line stays in cPartial until the next call. Ask often. A uiTimer calls pump() every 100 millis"}, {"u": "tut-63-chat.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 63: A chat over TCP", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # server and clients with TcpListener and TcpClient , a line-based protocol, polling with a timer instead of waiting, DataAvailable and ReadByte , testing asynchronous code with a bounded wait."}, {"u": "tut-63-chat.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 63: A chat over TCP", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Show a list of the connected names and tell everybody when somebody joins. Add a third window. Send a private message with /to Bob text : the server must send it only to Bob. Close a client and make the server remove the dead connection from its list."}, {"u": "tut-63-chat.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 63: A chat over TCP", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 64: URLs and encodings"}, {"u": "tut-64-urls.html", "t": "Tutorial 64: URLs and encodings", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-64-urls.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 64: URLs and encodings", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the parts of a URL and the Uri class, percent-encoding of special characters ( WebUtility.UrlEncode / UrlDecode ), building and reading a query string, combining a base address with a relative path, Base64, and why it is not encryption, checking that an address is valid."}, {"u": "tut-64-urls.html#the-parts-of-a-url", "t": "Tutorial 64: URLs and encodings", "h": "The parts of a URL", "x": "The parts of a URL # https://shop.example.com:8443/products/shoes?color=red&size=42#reviews \u2514scheme\u2518 \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500host\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\u2514port\u2518\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500path\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518 \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 query \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\u2514fragment\u2518 The query holds name=value pairs separated by & . The fragment (after # ) stays in the browser and is never sent to the server."}, {"u": "tut-64-urls.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 64: URLs and encodings", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 64: URLs and encodings - taking an address apart, building a query string safely, Base64 and percent-encoding load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Net\" \"System.Text\" } func main # 1. A URL has parts. The Uri class splits them: no searching for \":\" and \"/\" yourself. oUri = new Uri(\"https://shop.example.com:8443/products/shoes?color=red&size=42#reviews\") ? \"1. scheme : \" + oUri.Scheme ? \" host : \" + oUri.Host ? \" port : \" + oUri.Port ? \" path : \" + oUri.AbsolutePath ? \" query : \" + oUri.Query ? \" fragment : \" + oUri.Fragment # 2. Default ports: no port in the text"}, {"u": "tut-64-urls.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 64: URLs and encodings", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Uri # new Uri(text) parses the address and gives you Scheme , Host , Port , AbsolutePath , Query and Fragment . Never cut an address with substr and find : ports, user names, ? inside a fragment and many other details make it go wrong. If no port is written, the port of the scheme is used (80 for http , 443 for https ). Encoding # Some characters have a meaning in an address: & separates parameters, = joins a name and a value, ? starts the query, % starts an escape. If the text of the user contains them, they must be percent-encoded or they change the meaning: rock & roll would "}, {"u": "tut-64-urls.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 64: URLs and encodings", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Uri and its parts, encoding and decoding for query strings, the order: split first, decode last, relative addresses with named constructor arguments, Base64."}, {"u": "tut-64-urls.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 64: URLs and encodings", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write getParameter(cUrl, cName) that returns one value of the query of an address. Build the URL of a search for the text C# & Ring and check it with Uri . Turn a small text file into a Base64 data URL ( data:text/plain;base64,... )."}, {"u": "tut-64-urls.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 64: URLs and encodings", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 65: Enums and flags"}, {"u": "tut-65-enums.html", "t": "Tutorial 65: Enums and flags", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-65-enums.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 65: Enums and flags", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what an enum is and how it looks in Ring (a text), listing the names, testing a name, parsing a text, flags : several choices at once ( ReadOnly, Hidden ), your own flags with the bit operators & , | and ~ ."}, {"u": "tut-65-enums.html#enums", "t": "Tutorial 65: Enums and flags", "h": "Enums", "x": "Enums # An enum is a type with a fixed set of named values: DayOfWeek ( Sunday \u2026 Saturday ), FileAttributes , StringComparison , Environment.SpecialFolder . In C# you write DayOfWeek.Friday ; behind the name there is a number. In Ring an enum value is simply the text of its name : you read oDate.DayOfWeek and get \"Friday\" , and when a method wants an enum you pass \"Friday\" . You never need the numbers."}, {"u": "tut-65-enums.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 65: Enums and flags", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 65: enums and flags - named choices from .NET as text, checking and parsing them, and combining options with bits load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" } C_READ = 1 # the options of the permission bits in step 5 C_WRITE = 2 C_RUN = 4 func main oEnum = new Enum() oDays = dnType(\"System.DayOfWeek\") # 1. An enum is a fixed list of names. In Ring an enum value is a TEXT with the name: there is no number to remember. oDate = new DateTime(2024,5,17) ? \"1. 2024-05-17 is a \" + oDate.DayOfWeek + \". Is it a Friday? \" + yesNo(oDate.DayOfWeek = \"Friday\") # 2. All the nam"}, {"u": "tut-65-enums.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 65: Enums and flags", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Comparing # Step 1 compares the value with a text: oDate.DayOfWeek = \"Friday\" . It reads like English and cannot be confused with another number. Names, IsDefined and Parse # Enum.GetNames(type) lists all the names ( tutorial 20 explains dnType ). IsDefined asks whether a text is a valid name; use IsDefined([ :enumType = type, :value = text ]) (the two-argument form). Enum.Parse checks and converts: a wrong name raises an error that says Requested value 'Funday' was not found . When the text comes from a user or a file, either test it first or try / catch ( tutorial 7 ). Flags #"}, {"u": "tut-65-enums.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 65: Enums and flags", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # enum values are texts in Ring, GetNames , IsDefined , Parse , flag enums are comma-separated texts, bit flags with | , & and ~ ."}, {"u": "tut-65-enums.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 65: Enums and flags", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Print all the names of FileAttributes , then read the attributes of a folder with File.GetAttributes and decide whether it is a directory. Add a C_DELETE = 8 option and a function parse(\"read,write\") that turns a text into the number. Use StringComparison ( \"OrdinalIgnoreCase\" ) with String.Equals to compare two texts without regard to case."}, {"u": "tut-65-enums.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 65: Enums and flags", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 66: A spell checker"}, {"u": "tut-66-spelling.html", "t": "Tutorial 66: A spell checker", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-66-spelling.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 66: A spell checker", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # a set ( StringSet ) for fast \"is it known?\" questions, the edit distance (Levenshtein) and the table that computes it, sorting suggestions by distance and then by name, finding words with their positions in a text, why algorithms with tables (\"dynamic programming\") are worth knowing."}, {"u": "tut-66-spelling.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 66: A spell checker", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 66: a spell checker - a set of words, edit distance (Levenshtein) and the best suggestions load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Collections.Generic\" \"System.Text.RegularExpressions\" } aDictionary = [\"the\",\"quick\",\"brown\",\"fox\",\"jumps\",\"over\",\"lazy\",\"dog\",\"and\",\"then\",\"there\",\"their\",\"brain\",\"crown\",\"frown\",\"jump\",\"quack\",\"quiet\",\"lake\",\"dig\",\"dot\",\"dug\"] func main # 1. A StringSet (a .NET HashSet) answers \"is this word known?\" at once, however many words it holds oWords = new StringSet() for cWord in aDictionary oWords.Add(cWord) next ? \"1. the dictionary has \" + oWords."}, {"u": "tut-66-spelling.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 66: A spell checker", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The set # A list answers \"is this word in it?\" by looking at the items one by one: ten times more words, ten times more work. A set ( StringSet , a .NET HashSet ) answers with Contains in about the same time however big it is. A real dictionary has hundreds of thousands of words, and a set is the right place for it ( tutorial 4 ). The edit distance # The edit distance between two words is the smallest number of single-letter changes \u2014 insert , delete or replace \u2014 that turn one into the other: kitten \u2192 sitting is 3 ( k\u2192s , e\u2192i , add g ). Words with a small distance are probably t"}, {"u": "tut-66-spelling.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 66: A spell checker", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # a set for membership tests, edit distance and its table, ranking suggestions, cheap checks first."}, {"u": "tut-66-spelling.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 66: A spell checker", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add the swap of neighbours (Damerau distance) and see teh \u2192 the become 1. Read the dictionary from a text file with one word per line. Print the checked text with [brackets] around the unknown words. Suggest only words that start with the same letter, and measure how much faster it is ( tutorial 53 )."}, {"u": "tut-66-spelling.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 66: A spell checker", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 67: Number systems and big numbers"}, {"u": "tut-67-bases.html", "t": "Tutorial 67: Number systems and big numbers", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-67-bases.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 67: Number systems and big numbers", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # writing a number in base 2, 8, 10 and 16 and reading it back, why programmers like hexadecimal (colors, bytes), counting bits and shifting, how negative numbers look in binary, the limit of a Ring number (a double ) and how BigInteger removes it."}, {"u": "tut-67-bases.html#bases", "t": "Tutorial 67: Number systems and big numbers", "h": "Bases", "x": "Bases # The number two hundred fifty-five can be written in any base : Base Name Digits 255 is 2 binary 0 1 11111111 8 octal 0\u20137 377 10 decimal 0\u20139 255 16 hexadecimal 0\u20139 a\u2013f ff Computers store bits, so base 2 is what they use; but it is long to read. One hexadecimal digit is exactly 4 bits , so a byte is exactly two hex digits ( 00 to ff ). That is why colors ( #ff8000 ), memory addresses, hashes and byte dumps ( tutorial 45 ) are written in hex."}, {"u": "tut-67-bases.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 67: Number systems and big numbers", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 67: number systems and big numbers - binary, hexadecimal, bits, and numbers that do not fit in a double load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Numerics\" } func main oConvert = new Convert() # 1. The same number in different bases: 2 (binary), 8 (octal), 10 (decimal), 16 (hexadecimal) ? \"1. 255 in base 2 : \" + oConvert.Format([ :value = 255, :toBase = 2 ]) ? \" 255 in base 8 : \" + oConvert.Format([ :value = 255, :toBase = 8 ]) ? \" 255 in base 16: \" + oConvert.Format([ :value = 255, :toBase = 16 ]) # 2. And back: a text in a base to a number ? nl + \"2. 'ff' in base 1"}, {"u": "tut-67-bases.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 67: Number systems and big numbers", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Converting # Convert.ToString(number, base) writes a number in a base and Convert.ToInt32(text, base) reads it. In Ring the two-argument forms take a list of named values: Format([ :value = 255, :toBase = 16 ]) and ToInt32([ :value = \"ff\", :fromBase = 16 ]) ( ToString with parameters is Format ). A color is three numbers: hex2 writes each as two hex digits, with a leading zero when needed, and the reverse takes each pair of digits. Bits # BitOperations.PopCount(n) counts the one-bits (255 has 8, 256 has 1) and IsPow2(n) tests for a power of two \u2014 a number that is a power of two "}, {"u": "tut-67-bases.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 67: Number systems and big numbers", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Convert.ToString and ToInt32 with a base, hex, bytes and colors, PopCount , IsPow2 , << , >> , two's complement, the 2\u2075\u00b3 limit and BigInteger ."}, {"u": "tut-67-bases.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 67: Number systems and big numbers", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write toBinary(n) yourself with division by 2 and compare with Convert . Compute 100! and count its digits. Parse a color like #1e90ff into three numbers and back. Find the largest power of two below 10 million with PopCount or IsPow2 ."}, {"u": "tut-67-bases.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 67: Number systems and big numbers", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 68: Time zones"}, {"u": "tut-68-timezones.html", "t": "Tutorial 68: Time zones", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-68-timezones.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 68: Time zones", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # UTC and local time, and why you store UTC, TimeZoneInfo and converting between zones, the effect of daylight saving time , the duration of a flight, computed in UTC, the day that has 23 hours."}, {"u": "tut-68-timezones.html#the-rule", "t": "Tutorial 68: Time zones", "h": "The rule", "x": "The rule # Note Store and calculate in UTC. Convert to local time only when you show a time to a person. UTC ( coordinated universal time ) is the same everywhere and has no daylight saving. A local time is UTC plus an offset that depends on the place and the date : New York is UTC\u22125 in winter and UTC\u22124 in summer. If you keep local times in a database you cannot even tell which of two events came first."}, {"u": "tut-68-timezones.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 68: Time zones", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 68: time zones - UTC, local time, daylight saving, a meeting for several cities and a flight load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" } func main oZones = new TimeZoneInfo() aCities = [[\"New York\",\"Eastern Standard Time\"],[\"London\",\"GMT Standard Time\"],[\"Cairo\",\"Egypt Standard Time\"],[\"Tokyo\",\"Tokyo Standard Time\"]] # 1. A meeting at 12:00 UTC. Store and compare times in UTC; convert to local time only to SHOW them. ? \"1. the meeting at 12:00 UTC on 1 July 2024:\" oSummer = new DateTime(2024,7,1).AddHours(12) for aCity in aCities oLocal = localTime(oZones,oSummer,aCity[2]) ?"}, {"u": "tut-68-timezones.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 68: Time zones", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Zones # TimeZoneInfo.FindSystemTimeZoneById(\"Eastern Standard Time\") finds a zone by its identifier. On Windows the identifiers are names such as Eastern Standard Time , GMT Standard Time , Egypt Standard Time and Tokyo Standard Time (Linux and macOS use America/New_York ). ConvertTimeFromUtc(utc, zone) gives the local time and ConvertTimeToUtc([ :dateTime = local, :sourceTimeZone = zone ]) goes back. GetUtcOffset(time) tells the offset at that moment , so it includes daylight saving. Daylight saving # Steps 1 and 2 show the same UTC moment in July and in January. New York goes "}, {"u": "tut-68-timezones.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 68: Time zones", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # UTC for storing, local time for showing, FindSystemTimeZoneById , ConvertTimeFromUtc , ConvertTimeToUtc , GetUtcOffset , daylight saving rules differ and change, durations are added in UTC, invalid and ambiguous times."}, {"u": "tut-68-timezones.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 68: Time zones", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Show the current time in four cities ( DateTime.UtcNow and ConvertTimeFromUtc ). Find the hours of the day when offices in New York and Tokyo are both open (9\u201317). Find a time that is ambiguous in New York in November 2024 ( IsAmbiguousTime ). List all the zones with TimeZoneInfo.GetSystemTimeZones and print those with a +05:30 offset."}, {"u": "tut-68-timezones.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 68: Time zones", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 69: Randomness"}, {"u": "tut-69-random.html", "t": "Tutorial 69: Randomness", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-69-random.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 69: Randomness", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the Random class and the seed , throwing dice and checking that they are fair, shuffling with the Fisher\u2013Yates algorithm, weighted choices, Guid for unique ids, secure randomness for passwords and tokens."}, {"u": "tut-69-random.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 69: Randomness", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 69: randomness - seeds, dice, shuffling cards, weighted choices, unique ids, and when \"random\" must be secure load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Security.Cryptography\" } func main # 1. Random with a SEED gives the same sequence every time: perfect for tests and for repeating a simulation oFirst = new Random(2024) oSecond = new Random(2024) ? \"1. two generators with the same seed: \" + oFirst.Next_(100) + \" \" + oFirst.Next_(100) + \" \" + oFirst.Next_(100) + \" and \" + oSecond.Next_(100) + \" \" + oSecond.Next_(100) + \" \" + oSecond.Next_(100) # 2. Next_(max) is 0..ma"}, {"u": "tut-69-random.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 69: Randomness", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Random numbers are calculated # Random does not roll dice: it follows a formula that gives numbers that look random. The starting value is the seed . The same seed gives the same numbers (step 1), which is exactly what you want for a test or a replay: a game level that is the same for the same seed, a simulation you can repeat. Without a seed, new Random(NULL) starts from the clock. Next_(100) gives 0\u202699 ( Next is a Ring keyword, so the method is called Next_ ). Dice # Next_(6) + 1 is 1\u20266. With 6,000 throws every side comes up about 1,000 times; the test in the program accepts 9"}, {"u": "tut-69-random.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 69: Randomness", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # seeds and repeatable randomness, ranges and testing random code, Fisher\u2013Yates shuffling, weighted choice, Guid , Random against RandomNumberGenerator ."}, {"u": "tut-69-random.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 69: Randomness", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Throw two dice 36,000 times and print how often each sum (2\u202612) appears. Write a password generator with RandomNumberGenerator.GetInt32 and a list of allowed characters. Shuffle a deck of 52 cards (numbers 1\u201352) and deal four hands of five. Estimate the probability of at least one six in four throws by simulation, then calculate it exactly."}, {"u": "tut-69-random.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 69: Randomness", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 70: WPF animation"}, {"u": "tut-70-wpf-animation.html", "t": "Tutorial 70: WPF animation", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-70-wpf-animation.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 70: WPF animation", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the idea of an animation : a property, a start, an end and a duration, DoubleAnimation , Duration , AutoReverse and RepeatBehavior , easing , so that movements start and stop smoothly, animating a position ( Canvas.Left ), Opacity and a rotation Angle , stopping an animation, testing an animated window."}, {"u": "tut-70-wpf-animation.html#timer-or-animation", "t": "Tutorial 70: WPF animation", "h": "Timer or animation?", "x": "Timer or animation? # In tutorial 14 a timer changes the position 50 times a second and the program redraws. In WPF the rendering system knows about time: you describe the change once and WPF calculates every frame, uses the graphics card and keeps the window responsive. You animate dependency properties : Opacity , Width , Canvas.Left , Angle , colors..."}, {"u": "tut-70-wpf-animation.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 70: WPF animation", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 70: WPF animation - move, fade and rotate with animations that WPF runs for you load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Windows\" \"System.Windows.Controls\" \"System.Windows.Media\" \"System.Windows.Media.Animation\" \"System.Windows.Shapes\" \"System.Windows.Threading\" } oWindow = \"\" oBall = \"\" oBox = \"\" oSpin = \"\" oStatus = \"\" oCheck = \"\" func main # 1. The window: a canvas (free positions) with three shapes, and two buttons cXaml = '<Window xmlns=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation\" xmlns:x=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml\" Title=\"Anim"}, {"u": "tut-70-wpf-animation.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 70: WPF animation", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The window # The window is described in XAML ( tutorial 15 ): a Canvas for free positions with an ellipse, a rectangle and a second rectangle that has a RotateTransform named spin as its RenderTransform ; and a row with two buttons and a text. One animation # oMove = new DoubleAnimation() oMove.SetProp(\"From\",10) oMove.SetProp(\"To\",340) oMove.Duration = new Duration().New_([ :timeSpan = new TimeSpan().FromSeconds(2) ]) oMove.AutoReverse = True oMove.RepeatBehavior = new RepeatBehavior().Forever oBall.BeginAnimation(new Canvas().LeftProperty,oMove) A DoubleAnimation changes a num"}, {"u": "tut-70-wpf-animation.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 70: WPF animation", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # DoubleAnimation with From , To , Duration , AutoReverse and RepeatBehavior , easing, BeginAnimation and stopping with dnNull() , testing with a timer and the current values."}, {"u": "tut-70-wpf-animation.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 70: WPF animation", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make the ball bounce with BounceEase and EasingMode = \"EaseOut\" . Animate the Width of the blue box. Animate a color: ColorAnimation on Fill.Color of the ball. Add a slider that changes the speed (the Duration ) of the ball."}, {"u": "tut-70-wpf-animation.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 70: WPF animation", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 71: Releasing resources"}, {"u": "tut-71-dispose.html", "t": "Tutorial 71: Releasing resources", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-71-dispose.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 71: Releasing resources", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a resource is and why programs must release them, seeing a file stay locked, Dispose (and Close ), clean-up that runs after an error, which classes need it."}, {"u": "tut-71-dispose.html#resources", "t": "Tutorial 71: Releasing resources", "h": "Resources", "x": "Resources # Memory is cleaned automatically, but some things are lent by the operating system: open files, network connections, database connections, window handles. While you hold one, others may be unable to use it \u2014 a file that is open for writing cannot be deleted or moved, a connection counts against the limit of the server. You must give them back. In .NET the method is Dispose() (many classes also have Close() , which does the same)."}, {"u": "tut-71-dispose.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 71: Releasing resources", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 71: releasing resources - files that stay locked, Dispose, and cleaning up even when an error happens load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" } func main cFile = new Path().Combine([new Path().GetTempPath(),\"tutorial71.txt\"]) new File().WriteAllText([ :path = cFile, :contents = \"some text\" ]) # 1. A resource is something the operating system lends to your program: a file, a socket, a database connection. # While you hold it, others may not be able to use it. Open the file for writing and try to delete it. oStream = new File().Open([ :path = cFile, :mode = \"Open"}, {"u": "tut-71-dispose.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 71: Releasing resources", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Locked while open # Step 1 opens the file and tries to delete it: no , the system refuses, because the file is in use. Step 2 calls Dispose() and now the delete works. A program that forgets this leaves files locked until it ends, and a long-running program (a service, a window) can run out of handles. An error skips the clean-up # Step 3 is the typical bug. The function opens a reader, then does some work that fails (here a number that cannot be read), and the line oReader.Dispose() after the failing call is never reached: the file is still locked after the error. Note the comm"}, {"u": "tut-71-dispose.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 71: Releasing resources", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # resources and locks, Dispose and Close , an error can skip a clean-up, the clean-up goes after the try block, the .NET object outlives the Ring variable."}, {"u": "tut-71-dispose.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 71: Releasing resources", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write readFirstLine(cFile) that opens a StreamReader , reads a line, disposes it and returns the line \u2014 even if the file is empty. Open the same file twice for writing and see the error message. Write a function that copies a file with two streams and releases both even if the copy fails."}, {"u": "tut-71-dispose.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 71: Releasing resources", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 72: XML in depth"}, {"u": "tut-72-xml.html", "t": "Tutorial 72: XML in depth", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-72-xml.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 72: XML in depth", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the structure of an XML document: elements, attributes, text, reading with XDocument (LINQ to XML), searching with XPath , creating, changing and removing elements, saving and reading back, what happens with bad XML."}, {"u": "tut-72-xml.html#the-data", "t": "Tutorial 72: XML in depth", "h": "The data", "x": "The data # <\"t\">class=\"k\">library> <\"t\">class=\"k\">book \"t\">id=\"1\" \"t\">year=\"1937\"><\"t\">class=\"k\">title>The Hobbit</\"t\">class=\"k\">title><\"t\">class=\"k\">price>9.5</\"t\">class=\"k\">price></\"t\">class=\"k\">book> <\"t\">class=\"k\">book \"t\">id=\"2\" \"t\">year=\"1965\"><\"t\">class=\"k\">title>Dune</\"t\">class=\"k\">title><\"t\">class=\"k\">price>12</\"t\">class=\"k\">price></\"t\">class=\"k\">book> ... </\"t\">class=\"k\">library> An element has a name and can contain text and other elements; an attribute ( id=\"1\" ) belongs to an element. There is exactly one root element. Tutorial 6 read XML in a simple way; here are the tools for re"}, {"u": "tut-72-xml.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 72: XML in depth", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 72: XML in depth - reading with XDocument, searching with XPath, building and changing a document load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Xml\" \"System.Xml.Linq\" } func main cXml = '<library><book id=\"1\" year=\"1937\"><title>The Hobbit</title><price>9.5</price></book><book id=\"2\" year=\"1965\"><title>Dune</title><price>12</price></book><book id=\"3\" year=\"1984\"><title>Neuromancer</title><price>8.25</price></book></library>' # 1. XDocument (LINQ to XML) reads a text into a tree of elements. The root is the outer element. oDocument = new XDocument().Parse(cXml)"}, {"u": "tut-72-xml.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 72: XML in depth", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # XDocument # new XDocument().Parse(text) builds the tree. oDocument.Root is the root element. Elements(NULL) gives all child elements ( NULL because the method has other forms, see Core concepts ), and .toList() makes a Ring list. For each element Attribute(\"year\").Value reads an attribute and Element(\"title\").Value the text of a child. The values are always texts : convert with number() when you need a number (step 2 adds the prices). XPath # XPath describes a path through the tree: XPath Finds /library/book all books under the root //book every book at any depth /library/book[@"}, {"u": "tut-72-xml.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 72: XML in depth", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # elements, attributes, the root, XDocument : Root , Elements , Element , Attribute , XPath with XmlDocument , building, changing and removing, errors in bad XML."}, {"u": "tut-72-xml.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 72: XML in depth", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write the books as a table: title, year and price in columns ( tutorial 30 ). Use XPath to find the books cheaper than 10. Add a <tags> element with several <tag> children to each book. Convert the list of books to JSON ( tutorial 6 ) and back."}, {"u": "tut-72-xml.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 72: XML in depth", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 73: Reports with SQL"}, {"u": "tut-73-sql-reports.html", "t": "Tutorial 73: Reports with SQL", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-73-sql-reports.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 73: Reports with SQL", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # joining two tables with JOIN \u2026 ON , totals per group with GROUP BY and the aggregates COUNT , SUM , AVG , MIN , MAX , the difference between WHERE and HAVING , LEFT JOIN to keep rows that have no partner, a sub-query, parameters instead of pasted values."}, {"u": "tut-73-sql-reports.html#the-data", "t": "Tutorial 73: Reports with SQL", "h": "The data", "x": "The data # Two tables ( tutorial 17 explains how to create them): Customers (Ann and Cy in Cairo, Bob in Oslo, Dee in Tokyo) and Orders with a CustomerId that points to the customer, a product, an amount and a month. Dee has never ordered anything \u2014 that matters in step 4."}, {"u": "tut-73-sql-reports.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 73: Reports with SQL", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 73: reports with SQL - JOIN, GROUP BY, HAVING, LEFT JOIN, sub-queries and parameters load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"RingDotNet.Data\" } func main aSchema = [ [\"Customers\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Name\",\"string\",0],[\"City\",\"string\",0]]], [\"Orders\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"CustomerId\",\"int\",0,[[\"references\",\"Customers\"]]],[\"Product\",\"string\",0],[\"Amount\",\"int\",0],[\"Month\",\"string\",0]]] ] oDb = new RingDatabase(\"Data Source=:memory:\",aSchema) nAnn = oDb.insert(\"Customers\",[[\"Name\",\"Ann\"],[\"City\",\"Cairo\"]]) nBob = oDb.insert(\"Customers\",[[\"Name\",\"Bob\"],[\"City\",\"Oslo\"]]) nCy = oDb.insert(\"Cu"}, {"u": "tut-73-sql-reports.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 73: Reports with SQL", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # oDb.sqlRows(sql, parameters) runs any SELECT and returns the rows as lists of [column, value] pairs; dbValue(row, \"Total\") reads one column. The show helper prints a few columns as a small table. 1. JOIN # FROM Orders o JOIN Customers c ON c.Id = o.CustomerId puts together each order and the customer whose Id equals its CustomerId . o and c are short aliases of the tables. The result has one row per order, with the customer's name next to it. 2. GROUP BY # GROUP BY c.Name makes one row per customer ; COUNT(o.Id) and SUM(o.Amount) are calculated over the orders of that customer. "}, {"u": "tut-73-sql-reports.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 73: Reports with SQL", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # JOIN , LEFT JOIN , GROUP BY with aggregates, WHERE against HAVING , sub-queries and scalar , parameters against injection."}, {"u": "tut-73-sql-reports.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 73: Reports with SQL", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Show the average order per customer, rounded. List the products that were never ordered by a customer from Cairo. Find the month with the highest total. Add a Products table with prices and join it to the orders."}, {"u": "tut-73-sql-reports.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 73: Reports with SQL", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 74: Asking the computer"}, {"u": "tut-74-system.html", "t": "Tutorial 74: Asking the computer", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-74-system.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 74: Asking the computer", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the operating system and the .NET runtime, processors, memory, drives and the user, the current process and the list of all processes, the .NET memory manager ( GC ), writing a program that behaves differently on Windows and on other systems."}, {"u": "tut-74-system.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 74: Asking the computer", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 74: asking the computer - the operating system, the runtime, processors, memory, drives and running programs load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Diagnostics\" \"System.Text\" \"System.Runtime.InteropServices\" } func main oEnvironment = new Environment() oRuntime = new RuntimeInformation() # 1. Which system runs the program? A program that must behave differently on Windows, Linux and macOS asks first. ? \"1. platform : \" + oEnvironment.OSVersion.Platform ? \" runtime : \" + oRuntime.FrameworkDescription ? \" process type : \" + oRuntime.ProcessArchitecture +"}, {"u": "tut-74-system.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 74: Asking the computer", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The system and the runtime # Environment.OSVersion.Platform is Win32NT on Windows and Unix on Linux and macOS. RuntimeInformation.FrameworkDescription is the .NET version and ProcessArchitecture the type of the processor ( X64 , Arm64 ...). A program that needs a different path or command on each system asks first and then chooses ( tutorial 59 shows paths that work everywhere). The machine and the user # ProcessorCount is the number of logical processors; it tells you how many tasks can really run at the same time ( tutorial 10 ). TickCount64 is the number of milliseconds since"}, {"u": "tut-74-system.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 74: Asking the computer", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Environment , RuntimeInformation , Process.GetCurrentProcess and GetProcesses , GC.GetTotalMemory , testing values that differ between computers."}, {"u": "tut-74-system.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 74: Asking the computer", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Print the memory used by your program (in MB) before and after you fill a list with a million numbers. Show the 10 programs that use the most memory (name and MB) \u2014 a text task manager. Read the environment variable PATH and print each folder on its own line ( tutorial 59 )."}, {"u": "tut-74-system.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 74: Asking the computer", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 75: Recursion"}, {"u": "tut-75-recursion.html", "t": "Tutorial 75: Recursion", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-75-recursion.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 75: Recursion", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what recursion is and the two parts every recursive function needs, how the calls pile up (depth), classic problems that become short with recursion, walking a tree (folders), the danger of recursion without an end."}, {"u": "tut-75-recursion.html#the-idea", "t": "Tutorial 75: Recursion", "h": "The idea", "x": "The idea # Russian dolls: to open a doll you open the outer doll, and then you do the same job for the doll inside, until you reach the smallest doll that cannot be opened. A recursive function has the same two parts: the stop (the base case ): a case that is answered directly \u2014 factorial(1) is 1; the step : a call of itself with a smaller problem \u2014 factorial(n) is n * factorial(n \u2212 1) . If the step does not get closer to the stop, the function calls itself until the program runs out of memory."}, {"u": "tut-75-recursion.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 75: Recursion", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 75: recursion - a function that calls itself: factorial, the Hanoi towers, permutations, a folder tree and a flood fill load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" } nMoves = 0 func main # 1. Recursion = a function solves a big problem by solving a smaller one with itself. It needs a STOP: a case that is answered directly. ? \"1. factorial(5) = \" + factorial(5) + \" (5 * factorial(4), ..., and factorial(1) = 1 stops it)\" ? \" sum of the numbers 1..100 = \" + sumTo(100) # 2. The danger: a recursion without a stop (or too deep) never ends. Count the depth to see how the "}, {"u": "tut-75-recursion.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 75: Recursion", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # 1\u20132. Factorial and the depth # factorial(5) waits for factorial(4) , which waits for factorial(3) \u2026 until factorial(1) returns 1; then every call finishes in reverse order. Step 2 prints one line for each call, indented by its depth: the calls pile up like a stack of plates ( tutorial 58 ) and are removed in the opposite order. 3. Towers of Hanoi # To move n discs from A to C: move n \u2212 1 discs from A to B (using C), move the biggest disc to C, then move the n \u2212 1 discs from B to C. That is the whole solution; you never have to think about the individual moves. The number of move"}, {"u": "tut-75-recursion.html#when-not-to-use-recursion", "t": "Tutorial 75: Recursion", "h": "When not to use recursion", "x": "When not to use recursion # Ring (like most languages) keeps each call in memory; a depth of hundreds of thousands can exhaust it. A loop uses no extra memory. A recursion that repeats the same work (the naive Fibonacci in tutorial 53 ) needs a cache. Use recursion when the problem has the shape of a tree or \"the same problem, but smaller\" ; use a loop for simple repetition."}, {"u": "tut-75-recursion.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 75: Recursion", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # stop and step, depth and the call stack, Hanoi, permutations, folder trees, flood fill, when a loop is better."}, {"u": "tut-75-recursion.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 75: Recursion", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write power(base, exponent) recursively. Print all subsets of [1,2,3] . Count the files of each extension below a folder with a recursive function. Write reverse(text) recursively (the last letter + the reverse of the rest)."}, {"u": "tut-75-recursion.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 75: Recursion", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 76: Other calendars"}, {"u": "tut-76-calendars.html", "t": "Tutorial 76: Other calendars", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-76-calendars.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 76: Other calendars", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the Calendar classes of System.Globalization , reading a DateTime in another calendar ( GetYear , GetMonth , GetDayOfMonth ), converting a date of another calendar to a DateTime ( ToDateTime ), month lengths and why Ramadan moves through the year, month and day names in the language of a culture ( ar-SA )."}, {"u": "tut-76-calendars.html#one-moment-many-calendars", "t": "Tutorial 76: Other calendars", "h": "One moment, many calendars", "x": "One moment, many calendars # A .NET DateTime always stores a Gregorian date. A calendar object is a translator: you give it the DateTime and ask for the year, month and day as that calendar counts them . The classes you need are UmAlQuraCalendar (the Hijri calendar used in Saudi Arabia), HijriCalendar , PersianCalendar , HebrewCalendar , JapaneseCalendar , ThaiBuddhistCalendar and GregorianCalendar . All are in the namespace System.Globalization , so the program starts with using { \"System\", \"System.Globalization\" } ."}, {"u": "tut-76-calendars.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 76: Other calendars", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 76: other calendars - Hijri (Umm al-Qura), Persian, Hebrew, Japanese and Thai Buddhist dates with System.Globalization load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Globalization\" } func main oDate = new DateTime(2024,3,11) # a DateTime always holds a Gregorian date; a Calendar object reads it in its own way # 1. The same day in several calendars. Each calendar class has GetYear, GetMonth and GetDayOfMonth. ? \"1. the day 2024-03-11 in:\" ? \" Gregorian : \" + dateText(new GregorianCalendar(),oDate) ? \" Hijri (Umm al-Qura): \" + dateText(new UmAlQuraCalendar(),oDate) ? \" Pers"}, {"u": "tut-76-calendars.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 76: Other calendars", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Reading a date # Every calendar has the same three methods, so one helper ( dateText ) works for all of them. 11 March 2024 is 1 Ramadan 1445 in the Umm al-Qura calendar, 21 Esfand 1402 in the Persian calendar, and the year 2567 in the Thai Buddhist calendar. The Japanese calendar counts years within an era : 2024 is year 6 of Reiwa. Going back # calendar.ToDateTime(year, month, day, hour, minute, second, millisecond) builds the DateTime from a date in that calendar: oHijri.ToDateTime(1445,9,1,0,0,0,0) is 2024-03-11. Step 3 uses it to print the start of each Hijri month: the len"}, {"u": "tut-76-calendars.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 76: Other calendars", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # calendars are translators of a DateTime , GetYear , GetMonth , GetDayOfMonth , GetDaysInMonth , ToDateTime , Arabic names and calendars with CultureInfo(\"ar-SA\") , how a calendar year differs from the Gregorian one."}, {"u": "tut-76-calendars.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 76: Other calendars", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Print today's date in the Hijri and the Persian calendar. Find how many days are left until the next 1 Ramadan. Use PersianCalendar to list the months of the current Persian year with their lengths. Show a date with Format in fa-IR and he-IL ."}, {"u": "tut-76-calendars.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 76: Other calendars", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 77: XSLT"}, {"u": "tut-77-xslt.html", "t": "Tutorial 77: XSLT", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-77-xslt.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 77: XSLT", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a stylesheet is and how XslCompiledTransform runs it, templates, for-each , sort , sum and filters, three outputs from one input: text, CSV and HTML, how this class looks from Ring."}, {"u": "tut-77-xslt.html#the-idea", "t": "Tutorial 77: XSLT", "h": "The idea", "x": "The idea # You have data in XML ( tutorial 72 ) and you want a report. You can walk the tree in Ring, or you can write a stylesheet : a small XML document of templates (\"when you see this part of the input, write this\"). The advantage is that the stylesheet is data : change it, and the report changes, without touching the program. <\"t\">class=\"k\">xsl:template \"t\">match=\"/library\"> <\"t\">class=\"k\">xsl:for-each \"t\">select=\"book\"> <\"t\">class=\"k\">xsl:sort \"t\">select=\"@year\" \"t\">data-type=\"number\" \"t\">order=\"descending\"/> <\"t\">class=\"k\">xsl:value-of \"t\">select=\"title\"/> (<\"t\">class=\"k\">xsl:value-of \""}, {"u": "tut-77-xslt.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 77: XSLT", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 77: XSLT - turn XML into text, CSV and HTML with a stylesheet (System.Xml.Xsl) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Xml.Xsl\" } C_XML = '<library><book year=\"1937\" genre=\"fantasy\"><title>The Hobbit</title><price>9.5</price></book><book year=\"1965\" genre=\"scifi\"><title>Dune</title><price>12</price></book><book year=\"1984\" genre=\"scifi\"><title>Neuromancer</title><price>8.25</price></book></library>' # A stylesheet is XML too: a template says what to write for a part of the input. xsl:output chooses the kind of result. C_TEXT = '<xsl:stylesheet version=\""}, {"u": "tut-77-xslt.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 77: XSLT", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The Ring side # Everything happens in transform : it writes the stylesheet text to a file, then oTransform = new XslCompiledTransform() oTransform.Load_(cSheet) # compile the stylesheet (Load is a Ring keyword, so the method is Load_) oTransform.Transform([ :inputUri = cInput, :resultsFile = cOutput ]) # input file -> output file Load_ and Transform take file names in this form; the output file is read back with File.ReadAllText . Compiling is the slow part, so a program that transforms many documents compiles the stylesheet once and calls Transform many times. Three stylesheets"}, {"u": "tut-77-xslt.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 77: XSLT", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # XslCompiledTransform , Load_ and Transform , templates, for-each , sort , sum , filters, text, CSV and HTML output, attribute value templates {@name} ."}, {"u": "tut-77-xslt.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 77: XSLT", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write a stylesheet that outputs only the titles of the science fiction books. Add a <xsl:if test=\"price > 10\"> that marks expensive books with * . Make the HTML a complete page ( <html><body>\u2026 ) with a heading and the total price. Transform an XML file that you create with XDocument ( tutorial 72 )."}, {"u": "tut-77-xslt.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 77: XSLT", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 78: Protecting and signing messages"}, {"u": "tut-78-crypto.html", "t": "Tutorial 78: Protecting and signing messages", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-78-crypto.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 78: Protecting and signing messages", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # turning a password into keys with PBKDF2 ( Rfc2898DeriveBytes ), AES encryption with a random IV, an HMAC that proves that the message was not changed, why you check the HMAC before you decrypt, digital signatures with RSA , how these classes are called from Ring (byte arrays are Ring texts). This builds on tutorial 8 (hashes and basic encryption)."}, {"u": "tut-78-crypto.html#two-different-problems", "t": "Tutorial 78: Protecting and signing messages", "h": "Two different problems", "x": "Two different problems # Problem Tool Key Nobody should read the message encryption (AES) one secret key , shared by sender and receiver Nobody should change it, and I want to know it is genuine HMAC (shared key) or a signature (RSA) HMAC: the same shared key. Signature: a private key signs, the public key verifies. Use both for a protected message: encrypt first, then compute the HMAC over the encrypted bytes ( encrypt-then-MAC ). The receiver checks the HMAC first, so a forged or damaged package is refused before any decryption work happens."}, {"u": "tut-78-crypto.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 78: Protecting and signing messages", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 78: protecting and signing messages - AES, HMAC (encrypt-then-MAC), a password-derived key and RSA signatures load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text\" \"System.Security.Cryptography\" } func main oRandom = new RandomNumberGenerator() # 1. A key from a password: PBKDF2 stretches it (many rounds, a random salt) into as many bytes as we ask for. # 64 bytes: the first 32 are the AES key, the last 32 the HMAC key (never use one key for two jobs). cSalt = oRandom.GetBytes(16) cKeys = deriveKeys(\"correct horse battery staple\",cSalt) cEncryptionKey = left(cKeys,32) cMac"}, {"u": "tut-78-crypto.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 78: Protecting and signing messages", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Keys from a password # A password is short and predictable; a key must be 32 random-looking bytes. Rfc2898DeriveBytes (PBKDF2) repeats a hash 100,000 times with a random salt and returns as many bytes as you ask for. 64 bytes are split in two: one key for encryption and a different one for the HMAC \u2014 never use one key for two jobs. The salt is not secret: store it next to the package. The constructor has several forms, so it is made with named arguments: dnNew(\"Rfc2898DeriveBytes\",[ :password = ..., :salt = ..., :iterations = ..., :hashAlgorithm = ... ]) . Encrypting # Aes (made"}, {"u": "tut-78-crypto.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 78: Protecting and signing messages", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # PBKDF2 keys and salts, AES with a fresh IV, encrypt-then-MAC and verifying first, a comparison that takes the same time, RSA signatures with a public key, byte arrays as Ring texts."}, {"u": "tut-78-crypto.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 78: Protecting and signing messages", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Put the salt in front of the package so that it can be opened with only the password. Save the public key XML in a file and verify a signature in a second program. Sign a file's SHA-256 hash instead of the whole file with SignHash . Time deriveKeys and change the number of rounds; how many are comfortable for your computer?"}, {"u": "tut-78-crypto.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 78: Protecting and signing messages", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 79: Two programs talking"}, {"u": "tut-79-processes-talk.html", "t": "Tutorial 79: Two programs talking", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-79-processes-talk.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 79: Two programs talking", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # starting another program with ProcessStartInfo and Process , and reading what it prints, named pipes : NamedPipeServerStream and NamedPipeClientStream , a line-based protocol on top of a stream ( StreamReader / StreamWriter ), a named mutex ( Mutex ) that the whole computer shares: only one copy of a program, the same program as parent and child ( --child )."}, {"u": "tut-79-processes-talk.html#pipes-and-mutexes", "t": "Tutorial 79: Two programs talking", "h": "Pipes and mutexes", "x": "Pipes and mutexes # A named pipe is a channel between two processes on one computer. One side is the server : it creates the pipe with a name and waits. The other side is the client : it connects to the name. Both sides get a stream and use it like a file or a socket ( tutorial 56 , tutorial 25 ). Pipes are fast and need no network and no port number. A named mutex is a lock with a name. The first program that gets it holds it; another program that asks for the same name does not get it. Typical use: a program that must not run twice (a tray application, a database service, an installer)."}, {"u": "tut-79-processes-talk.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 79: Two programs talking", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 79: two programs talking - a named pipe between processes, and a mutex that allows only one copy # ring t79_processes_talk.ring the parent: it starts a second copy of this program (the child) and talks to it # ring t79_processes_talk.ring --child the child (the parent starts it, you do not) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.IO.Pipes\" \"System.Diagnostics\" \"System.Threading\" } C_PIPE = \"ringdotnet_tutorial79\" C_MUTEX = \"ringdotnet_tutorial79_single_copy\" func main if find(sysargv,\"--child\") > 0 runChild() return ok runParent() # --------------------"}, {"u": "tut-79-processes-talk.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 79: Two programs talking", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # One file, two roles # The program starts itself a second time with the argument --child ( exefilename() is the Ring executable, filename() this file). main looks at sysargv : with --child it is the child, otherwise the parent. This keeps the tutorial in one file; in real life the two programs would be different files. The parent # The lock. dnNew(\"Mutex\",[ :initiallyOwned = False, :name = ... ]) opens (or creates) the mutex; WaitOne(0) means try to take it, do not wait . The parent gets it. The pipe. NamedPipeServerStream creates the pipe. The constructor has many forms, so it i"}, {"u": "tut-79-processes-talk.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 79: Two programs talking", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Process.Start with redirected output, named pipes between processes, a named mutex for a single-instance program, a line protocol over a stream."}, {"u": "tut-79-processes-talk.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 79: Two programs talking", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make the child answer time with the current time. Let the parent start two children one after the other, each using the pipe in turn. Make a program that exits with a message when it cannot get the mutex, and start it twice from two terminals. Send a list as a line of JSON ( tutorial 80 ) and parse it on the other side."}, {"u": "tut-79-processes-talk.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 79: Two programs talking", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 80: JSON as a tree"}, {"u": "tut-80-json-nodes.html", "t": "Tutorial 80: JSON as a tree", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-80-json-nodes.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 80: JSON as a tree", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # parsing JSON into a tree you can edit ( JsonNode , JsonObject , JsonArray ), reading with Item(\"key\") and Item(index) , SetItem , Add , Remove on objects and Add on arrays, building a document from Ring values, merging two documents, DeepClone and DeepEquals . These classes are in System.Text.Json.Nodes . Tutorial 6 read JSON with JsonDocument ; that class is fast but read-only. Use the nodes when you need to change JSON: configuration files, request bodies, data you assemble."}, {"u": "tut-80-json-nodes.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 80: JSON as a tree", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 80: JSON as a tree you can change - JsonNode, JsonObject and JsonArray (System.Text.Json.Nodes) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text.Json\" \"System.Text.Json.Nodes\" } C_CONFIG = '{\"name\":\"shop\",\"port\":8080,\"debug\":false,\"tags\":[\"web\",\"sql\"],\"database\":{\"host\":\"localhost\",\"pool\":5}}' func main # 1. Parse text into a tree. A JsonNode is an object, an array or a value; AsObject() and AsArray() give the specific class. oRoot = new JsonNode().Parse(C_CONFIG).AsObject() ? \"1. name = \" + oRoot.Item(\"name\").ToString() + \", port + 1 = \" + (number(oRoot.Item(\"port\").T"}, {"u": "tut-80-json-nodes.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 80: JSON as a tree", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Reading # new JsonNode().Parse(text) gives a node; AsObject() and AsArray() give the specific class. oRoot.Item(\"name\") is the child with that key and oTags.Item(0) the first array item (indexes start at 0, as in .NET). Every node has ToString() for simple values \u2014 numbers arrive as text, so number(oRoot.Item(\"port\").ToString()) \u2014 and GetPath() , which names where the node lives ( $.tags[0] ). GetValueKind() tells Object , Array , String , Number , True , False or Null . Changing # What Call set an existing value oRoot.SetItem(\"port\",9090) add a key oRoot.Add([ :propertyName = \""}, {"u": "tut-80-json-nodes.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 80: JSON as a tree", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # JsonNode , JsonObject , JsonArray , Item , SetItem , Add , Remove , ContainsKey , the Ring True is a number: parse true for JSON, merging with a small recursive function, DeepClone and DeepEquals ."}, {"u": "tut-80-json-nodes.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 80: JSON as a tree", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Read a configuration file ( tutorial 32 ) into a node, change one value and save it again. Write getPath(oRoot, \"database.host\") that follows a dotted path. Write removeNulls(oNode) that deletes all keys with null values, at every depth. Send the JSON of step 5 through the pipe of tutorial 79 ."}, {"u": "tut-80-json-nodes.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 80: JSON as a tree", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 81: Network tools"}, {"u": "tut-81-network-tools.html", "t": "Tutorial 81: Network tools", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-81-network-tools.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 81: Network tools", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # IPAddress : parsing, families (IPv4, IPv6), loopback, TryParse and other methods with out parameters, IPEndPoint : an address and a port, Dns.GetHostEntry , Ping , NetworkInterface , checking whether a port accepts connections, with a time limit."}, {"u": "tut-81-network-tools.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 81: Network tools", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 81: network tools - IP addresses, DNS, ping, network interfaces and checking whether a port is open (all on this computer) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Net\" \"System.Net.Sockets\" \"System.Net.NetworkInformation\" } func main # 1. IPAddress parses an address and knows its family. A method with an \"out\" parameter returns a list: [success, the address]. oParser = new IPAddress() oAddress = oParser.Parse(\"192.168.1.20\") cBytes = oAddress.GetAddressBytes() # the 4 bytes of an IPv4 address (a byte[] is a Ring text) ? \"1. \" + oAddress.ToString() + \" is \" + oAddres"}, {"u": "tut-81-network-tools.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 81: Network tools", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Addresses and out parameters # IPAddress.Parse(\"192.168.1.20\") gives an object. GetAddressBytes() returns the four bytes as a Ring text (a byte[] is a text in this package), so ascii(cBytes[1]) is 192. AddressFamily is InterNetwork for IPv4 and InterNetworkV6 for IPv6, and IsLoopback tests for 127.0.0.1 and ::1 . IPAddress.TryParse(text, out address) answers yes or no without raising an error. In .NET the address comes back through an out parameter; in Ring **a method with out parameters returns a list**: [ success, the address ] . So aGood = oParser.TryParse(\"10.0.0.7\") gives a"}, {"u": "tut-81-network-tools.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 81: Network tools", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # IPAddress , IPEndPoint , out parameters come back as a list, Dns , Ping , NetworkInterface , a connection test with a time limit."}, {"u": "tut-81-network-tools.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 81: Network tools", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write waitForPort(address, port, seconds) that tries every 200 ms and returns when the port opens. List the open ports of 127.0.0.1 between 8000 and 8010 after starting two listeners. Ping 127.0.0.1 five times and print the average round trip. Resolve a real host name with Dns.GetHostEntry and handle the error when there is no network."}, {"u": "tut-81-network-tools.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 81: Network tools", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 82: Shared memory"}, {"u": "tut-82-shared-memory.html", "t": "Tutorial 82: Shared memory", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-82-shared-memory.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 82: Shared memory", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a memory-mapped file is, MemoryMappedFile.CreateNew and OpenExisting (a named block that two programs share), a view accessor for numbers ( Write , ReadInt32 , ReadDouble ) and a view stream for text, why a text read from the memory ends at the first zero byte, mapping a real file ( CreateFromFile ) and changing it in place."}, {"u": "tut-82-shared-memory.html#the-idea", "t": "Tutorial 82: Shared memory", "h": "The idea", "x": "The idea # A normal file is read and written through calls. A memory-mapped file is a file (or just a block of memory with a name) that the system shows to your program as ordinary memory: what one program writes into it, another program that mapped the same name sees at once. It is the fastest way for programs on one computer to share data, and it is also a comfortable way to work with a very big file without loading it. Compare the tools of the last tutorials: a named pipe ( tutorial 79 ) sends messages one after another; shared memory is a place both programs can read and write at any time."}, {"u": "tut-82-shared-memory.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 82: Shared memory", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 82: shared memory - a memory-mapped file seen by two programs, numbers and text in it, and a file that is edited in place # ring t82_shared_memory.ring the parent: it makes the memory and starts the child # ring t82_shared_memory.ring --child the child (the parent starts it) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Diagnostics\" \"System.IO.MemoryMappedFiles\" \"System.Threading\" } C_NAME = \"ringdotnet_tutorial82\" func main if find(sysargv,\"--child\") > 0 runChild() return ok runParent() editFileInPlace() # ----------------------------------------------------"}, {"u": "tut-82-shared-memory.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 82: Shared memory", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # One name, two programs # The parent calls MemoryMappedFile.CreateNew([ :mapName = \"...\", :capacity = 4096 ]) : a block of 4,096 bytes with a name. The child (a second copy of the same program, started with --child as in tutorial 79) calls OpenExisting(name) and gets the same block . Nothing is copied. Numbers # CreateViewAccessor(NULL) gives a view with methods that work at a position (a byte number): Write(position, value) , ReadInt32(position) , ReadDouble(position) , ReadBoolean(position) . The two programs must agree on the layout \u2014 here: position 0 holds an integer, positio"}, {"u": "tut-82-shared-memory.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 82: Shared memory", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # named shared memory and OpenExisting , CreateViewAccessor for numbers and CreateViewStream for text, zero-filled memory and cutting a text, CreateFromFile for editing a file in place, the need for a layout and for locking."}, {"u": "tut-82-shared-memory.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 82: Shared memory", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Put a \"ready\" flag at position 200 and let the child wait ( Thread.Sleep in a loop) until the parent sets it. Keep a counter at position 0 that both programs increase, protected by a Mutex . Map a text file and replace one character of it in place. Store a text as length first : four bytes for the length, then the text."}, {"u": "tut-82-shared-memory.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 82: Shared memory", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 83: Tasks that wait"}, {"u": "tut-83-tasks-waiting.html", "t": "Tutorial 83: Tasks that wait", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-83-tasks-waiting.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 83: Tasks that wait", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a Task is and what Wait does, Task.Delay : a wait that does not block a thread, WhenAll and WhenAny , a time limit with Wait(milliseconds) , cancelling with CancellationTokenSource and a token, why many waits together take the time of the longest, not the sum."}, {"u": "tut-83-tasks-waiting.html#tasks-and-ring", "t": "Tutorial 83: Tasks that wait", "h": "Tasks and Ring", "x": "Tasks and Ring # In .NET a task is a promise: a piece of work that is running or waiting and will be finished , failed or cancelled . HttpClient.GetAsync , File.ReadAllTextAsync and Process.WaitForExitAsync all return tasks. Ring runs your code on one thread, and Ring code cannot run on other threads ( Core concepts ), so you do not write the work of a task in Ring. You use the tasks that .NET provides, start several, and wait for them in Ring. Task.Delay is the simplest such task: it finishes after a time and does nothing else, which makes the rules visible."}, {"u": "tut-83-tasks-waiting.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 83: Tasks that wait", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 83: tasks that wait - Task.Delay, WhenAll, WhenAny, time limits and cancellation (System.Threading.Tasks) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Diagnostics\" \"System.Threading\" \"System.Threading.Tasks\" } func main oTasks = new Task() # 1. A Task is \"something that finishes later\". Task.Delay(ms) is a task that finishes after that time, without using a thread to sleep. oWatch = new Stopwatch() oWatch.Start() oOne = oTasks.Delay(300) ? \"1. the task was made at once (under 100 ms): \" + yesNo(oWatch.ElapsedMilliseconds < 100) + \", finished already? \" + yesNo(oOne.IsCo"}, {"u": "tut-83-tasks-waiting.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 83: Tasks that wait", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Delay and Wait # Task.Delay(300) returns at once with a task that is not finished yet ( IsCompleted is false). Wait(NULL) blocks the Ring program until the task is finished; its Status is then RanToCompletion . Making the task and waiting for it are two separate steps \u2014 that separation is what lets you start many tasks first. WhenAll and WhenAny # Task.WhenAll([t1, t2, t3]) is a new task that finishes when all of them did. Three delays of 300 ms started together are over after about 300 ms, not 900. The same idea turns a hundred slow downloads into one wait. Task.WhenAny([\u2026]) fi"}, {"u": "tut-83-tasks-waiting.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 83: Tasks that wait", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Task.Delay , Wait , Status , IsCompleted , WhenAll and WhenAny with a Ring list of tasks, Wait(ms) as a time limit, CancellationTokenSource , CancelAfter and the token, tasks wait together."}, {"u": "tut-83-tasks-waiting.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 83: Tasks that wait", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Start three HttpClient requests ( tutorial 84 ) and wait for all with WhenAll ; compare the time with doing them one after another. Print the elapsed milliseconds for each step and see how close they are to the delays. Use WhenAny with a delay as a timeout : whichever of a request and a 2-second delay ends first wins. Cancel a Process.WaitForExitAsync of a program that runs for a minute."}, {"u": "tut-83-tasks-waiting.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 83: Tasks that wait", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 84: HttpClient in depth"}, {"u": "tut-84-http-client.html", "t": "Tutorial 84: HttpClient in depth", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-84-http-client.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 84: HttpClient in depth", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # one HttpClient for the whole program, and DefaultRequestHeaders , reading response headers and the content type, status codes: an answer of 404 is not an error, EnsureSuccessStatusCode when \"not OK\" must stop the program, cancelling a request that takes too long, saving a download with CopyToAsync . Tutorial 39 made a first REST client; tutorial 9 explained HTTP. This lesson adds what you need for real services."}, {"u": "tut-84-http-client.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 84: HttpClient in depth", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # The service is a small WebServer that runs in the same program (the client code is the same for a real service; only the address differs). # Tutorial 84: HttpClient in depth - headers both ways, status codes, errors, time limits, cancelling and saving a download # (a small server runs inside the program, so no internet is needed; the client code is what you write for a real service) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Net.Http\" \"System.Threading\" \"RingDotNet.Web\" } oServer = \"\" oClient = \"\" func main oServer = new WebServer(\"http://127.0.0.1:0\") oServer.onGet("}, {"u": "tut-84-http-client.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 84: HttpClient in depth", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # One client # Create one HttpClient and use it for all requests: it keeps connections open and is meant to be shared. Creating a client for each request wastes connections. DefaultRequestHeaders.Add(\"X-Client\", \u2026) adds headers that go with every request (a user agent, an API key, Accept ). The timeout of ten seconds protects the program from a server that never answers. Headers # Step 1 shows both directions. The request carried X-Client ; the page read it with oRequest.header(\"X-Client\") . The page set its own header with oRequest.setHeader(\"X-Server\",\"ring\") , and the client re"}, {"u": "tut-84-http-client.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 84: HttpClient in depth", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # one shared HttpClient , default headers, request and response headers, ContentType , StatusCode , ReasonPhrase , IsSuccessStatusCode , EnsureSuccessStatusCode , cancelling with a token, CopyToAsync to a file."}, {"u": "tut-84-http-client.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 84: HttpClient in depth", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a header Authorization with a fake token and let the server answer 401 when it is missing. Download the file with ResponseHeadersRead and print the ContentLength from the headers before copying. Send three requests together and wait with WhenAll ( tutorial 83 ). Retry a request three times with a pause when the status is 503 ."}, {"u": "tut-84-http-client.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 84: HttpClient in depth", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 85: Working with other programs"}, {"u": "tut-85-feed-programs.html", "t": "Tutorial 85: Working with other programs", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-85-feed-programs.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 85: Working with other programs", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the three streams of a program: standard input , standard output and standard error , sending lines to a program and closing its input, chaining programs: the output of one is the input of another, environment variables and the working folder of the new program, exit codes: 0 means success, WaitForExit(ms) as a time limit and Kill . Tutorial 10 started programs and tutorial 37 showed their output in a window. This lesson adds the input side and the control of the new program. Note The examples use the Windows commands sort , find and cmd . On Linux and macOS use sort , gr"}, {"u": "tut-85-feed-programs.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 85: Working with other programs", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 85: working with other programs - feeding standard input, environment variables, errors and exit codes, a time limit and Kill # (the examples use the Windows commands sort, find and cmd; on Linux and macOS use sort, grep and sh) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Diagnostics\" } func main # 1. Standard input: send lines to a program that reads its input, close the input, and read what it answers. # The program \"sort\" reads lines and prints them sorted. oSort = startProgram(\"sort\",\"\",True) oSort.StandardInput.WriteLine(\"pear\") oSort.StandardInput.Wri"}, {"u": "tut-85-feed-programs.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 85: Working with other programs", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Starting a program with redirected streams # startProgram makes a ProcessStartInfo (the file, the arguments) and sets three things: UseShellExecute = False (needed to redirect), CreateNoWindow (no console window) and, when asked, RedirectStandardInput , RedirectStandardOutput and RedirectStandardError . Process.Start(info) starts it and gives a Process . After that oProcess.StandardInput , StandardOutput and StandardError are stream writers and readers: your program is now the keyboard and the screen of the other program. Input # sort reads lines until its input ends, then print"}, {"u": "tut-85-feed-programs.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 85: Working with other programs", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # redirecting the three streams, the write, close, read, wait pattern, environment variables and WorkingDirectory , exit codes and standard error, WaitForExit(ms) and Kill ."}, {"u": "tut-85-feed-programs.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 85: Working with other programs", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write a function runAndCapture(cFile, cArguments) that returns [exit code, output, error] . Run ping with a time limit of two seconds and stop it if it is slower. Write a little program that reads lines from its input and prints them in upper case (the child of tutorial 79 ) and feed it from the parent. Chain three programs: sort, remove duplicates ( uniq or sort /unique ), count lines."}, {"u": "tut-85-feed-programs.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 85: Working with other programs", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 86: Escaping"}, {"u": "tut-86-escaping.html", "t": "Tutorial 86: Escaping", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-86-escaping.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 86: Escaping", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # why text must be encoded for the place where it goes, HTML: WebUtility.HtmlEncode and HtmlEncoder , URLs: UrlEncoder , JavaScript: JavaScriptEncoder , JSON: JsonEncodedText , XML: XElement escapes for you, regular expressions: Regex.Escape , SQL: parameters, never joined text."}, {"u": "tut-86-escaping.html#the-rule", "t": "Tutorial 86: Escaping", "h": "The rule", "x": "The rule # A text has no meaning by itself; the place gives it one. <b> is just letters in a text file and a command in an HTML page. If the text of a visitor reaches a place without being encoded, the visitor can write commands for that place: a script that runs in other people's browsers ( cross-site scripting ), SQL that reads your whole database ( SQL injection ). The defence is always the same: encode for the place where the text goes , with a tool that knows that place. Place Danger Tool HTML < , > , & , quotes WebUtility.HtmlEncode , HtmlEncoder URL & , = , space, non-ASCII UrlEncoder J"}, {"u": "tut-86-escaping.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 86: Escaping", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 86: escaping - the right encoding for the right place (HTML, URL, JavaScript, JSON, XML, regular expressions and SQL) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Net\" \"System.Text.Encodings.Web\" \"System.Text.Json\" \"System.Text.RegularExpressions\" \"System.Xml.Linq\" \"RingDotNet.Data\" } func main # A text from a user can contain characters that mean something in the place where you put it. The rule: ENCODE FOR THE PLACE. cText = \"<script>alert('x & y')</script>\" # 1. HTML: < > & and quotes become entities, so the browser shows the text and does not run it. WebUtility is t"}, {"u": "tut-86-escaping.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 86: Escaping", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # HTML # HtmlEncode replaces < , > , & and quotes by entities ( &lt; , &amp; , \u2026). The browser then shows <script> instead of running it. Step 2 puts the same name into a page twice: without encoding, the onerror handler would run in the visitor's browser. HtmlEncoder.Default.Encode (namespace System.Text.Encodings.Web ) is the stricter encoder that ASP.NET uses; the two differ in details such as &#39; against &#x27; . HtmlDecode reverses the encoding. URL, JavaScript, JSON # UrlEncoder turns space, & , / and non-ASCII letters into %XX ; encode each value , not the whole address ("}, {"u": "tut-86-escaping.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 86: Escaping", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # text is encoded for the place, WebUtility , HtmlEncoder , UrlEncoder , JavaScriptEncoder , JsonEncodedText , Regex.Escape , XElement escapes by itself, SQL parameters stop injection."}, {"u": "tut-86-escaping.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 86: Escaping", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make the tiny web site of tutorial 26 safe: where does it show a visitor's text? Write a function link(cText, cAddress) that builds <a href=\"\u2026\">\u2026</a> with the right encoding for the address and for the text. Search a text file for a phrase that the user types, using Regex.Escape . Try the attack text '; DROP TABLE People; -- as a parameter and check that the table is still there."}, {"u": "tut-86-escaping.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 86: Escaping", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 87: Expression trees"}, {"u": "tut-87-expressions.html", "t": "Tutorial 87: Expression trees", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-87-expressions.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 87: Expression trees", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # code as data: a tree of objects ( System.Linq.Expressions ), Parameter , Add , Multiply , Constant , Condition , Call_ , Lambda , ToString() of a tree and NodeType , compiling a tree ( Compile ) and calling the function, building a tree from something the program receives at run time. Entity Framework builds its queries the same way ( tutorial 27 ): a condition is first a tree , which can be turned into SQL, and only a compiled tree is a function you can run."}, {"u": "tut-87-expressions.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 87: Expression trees", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 87: building code while the program runs - expression trees (System.Linq.Expressions): build, show, compile and call load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Linq.Expressions\" } func main # The expression class of this namespace is called ExpressionsExpression in Ring (the name Expression is used by another namespace). oExpression = new ExpressionsExpression() oDouble = dnType(\"System.Double\") # 1. An expression tree is code as DATA: a tree of objects. Two parameters a and b, and a node that adds them. oA = oExpression.Parameter([ :type = oDouble, :name = \"a\" ]) oB "}, {"u": "tut-87-expressions.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 87: Expression trees", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The class name # The static factory class of the namespace is Expression ; another namespace already has a class with that name, so in Ring it is ** ExpressionsExpression ** (see Gotchas ). new ExpressionsExpression() is only a handle to reach the factory methods ( Add , Lambda , \u2026). Nodes # Every part of the code is a node made by a factory method: Node Made by Looks like a parameter Parameter([ :type = ..., :name = \"a\" ]) a a number Constant(10.0) 10 an operation Add , Subtract , Multiply , Divide with :left and :right (a + b) a comparison GreaterThan([ :left = ..., :right = ."}, {"u": "tut-87-expressions.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 87: Expression trees", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # code as a tree of objects, the factory methods and their named values, ToString , NodeType , Lambda , Compile , DynamicInvoke , building a function from run-time data."}, {"u": "tut-87-expressions.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 87: Expression trees", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Build a * a - b and compile it. Build a function that returns a when a is positive and 0 - a otherwise (the absolute value). Extend the helper binaryFunction with % ( Modulo ) and > ( GreaterThan ). Turn the parser of tutorial 54 into a function that returns a tree instead of a number."}, {"u": "tut-87-expressions.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 87: Expression trees", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 88: Compression"}, {"u": "tut-88-compression.html", "t": "Tutorial 88: Compression", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-88-compression.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 88: Compression", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # how a compression stream wraps another stream, GZipStream , DeflateStream and BrotliStream , compressing and decompressing bytes in a MemoryStream , why Dispose is needed to finish the data, why random data does not shrink, writing and reading a .gz file with StreamWriter and StreamReader . Tutorial 5 made a ZIP archive of files. This lesson compresses data (one stream of bytes). GZip is the format of .gz files and of Content-Encoding: gzip on the web."}, {"u": "tut-88-compression.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 88: Compression", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 88: compression - GZip, Deflate and Brotli on streams, sizes, a round trip and a compressed file load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.IO.Compression\" \"System.Text\" } func main # Compression looks for repetition. A text with many repeated parts becomes small; random data does not. cText = \"\" for i = 1 to 200 cText += \"The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog. \" next oUtf8 = new UTF8Encoding() aBytes = oUtf8.GetBytes(cText) # a byte[] is a Ring text of bytes in this package ? \"1. original text: \" + len(aBytes) + \" bytes\" # 2. The three formats. Each"}, {"u": "tut-88-compression.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 88: Compression", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Streams that wrap streams # A compression stream does not hold data itself: it is built on top of another stream and a mode ( Compress or Decompress ). Bytes written to a compressor are packed and written to the stream below it; bytes read from a decompressor come from the stream below it, unpacked. The stream below can be a MemoryStream (bytes in memory) or a FileStream (a file). The three classes have the same members, so the function packer is the only place that knows the format. The constructor has several forms, so it is called with names: dnNew(\"GZipStream\",[ :stream = oS"}, {"u": "tut-88-compression.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 88: Compression", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # a compression stream wraps another stream, Dispose finishes the compressed data, the three formats have the same members, repeated text shrinks, random or already compressed data does not, a compressed file is a StreamWriter on a GZipStream on a FileStream ."}, {"u": "tut-88-compression.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 88: Compression", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Compress the text of a real file (a log from tutorial 21 ) and print the ratio for each format. Add CompressionLevel and compare \"Fastest\" with \"SmallestSize\" with a Stopwatch . Write a function gzipFile(source, target) that uses CopyTo between two file streams. Compress the same bytes twice and look at the size of the second result."}, {"u": "tut-88-compression.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 88: Compression", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 89: Collections for many threads"}, {"u": "tut-89-concurrent-collections.html", "t": "Tutorial 89: Collections for many threads", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-89-concurrent-collections.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 89: Collections for many threads", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # why an ordinary list is not safe for several threads, ConcurrentQueue and ConcurrentStack , BlockingCollection : Add , Take , CompleteAdding , a bounded size, ConcurrentDictionary : TryAdd , TryUpdate , TryRemove , the Try... methods that return a list ( [ success, value ] ), generic types made with dnNewGeneric and used with callMethod and getProp ."}, {"u": "tut-89-concurrent-collections.html#why", "t": "Tutorial 89: Collections for many threads", "h": "Why", "x": "Why # If two threads add to one List at the same moment, the list can lose an item or break. The concurrent collections do each step (add, remove, \"add if missing\") as one indivisible step . They are the tool for a producer that adds work and a consumer that takes it. The program in this lesson uses one thread, so that its output is always the same; the calls are the same as in a program with many threads. ( Tutorial 83 shows tasks.)"}, {"u": "tut-89-concurrent-collections.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 89: Collections for many threads", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 89: collections for many threads - ConcurrentQueue, ConcurrentStack, BlockingCollection and ConcurrentDictionary load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Collections.Concurrent\" } func main # The collections of System.Collections.Concurrent are safe when several threads use them at the same time: the 'is it # there? then change it' steps are done as ONE step. They are generic, so they are made with dnNewGeneric (a type name, # the type arguments, the constructor values). cInt = \"System.Int32\" cText = \"System.String\" # 1. ConcurrentQueue: first in, first out. TryDequ"}, {"u": "tut-89-concurrent-collections.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 89: Collections for many threads", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Generic types # These classes are generic ( ConcurrentQueue<T> ), so a Ring program creates a closed type with dnNewGeneric(type, [type arguments], [constructor values]) . Some closed types have a Ring class and work like any object (the queue of names in step 1); for the others the object is a plain DotNetObject and you use callMethod(\"Name\", [arguments]) and getProp(\"Name\") ( Core concepts ). The result is the same, only the way of writing differs. A dictionary item is read with callMethod(\"get_Item\", [key]) . Try methods # TryDequeue , TryPop , TryPeek , TryRemove end with an"}, {"u": "tut-89-concurrent-collections.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 89: Collections for many threads", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # concurrent collections do each step as one step, the Try methods return [ success, value ] , BlockingCollection and CompleteAdding , bounded collections, dnNewGeneric with callMethod and getProp ."}, {"u": "tut-89-concurrent-collections.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 89: Collections for many threads", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Count the letters of a text in a ConcurrentDictionary with TryAdd and TryUpdate . Write a function drain(oQueue) that returns all the items of a ConcurrentQueue as a Ring list. Fill a bounded collection (size 3) with TryAdd and print what was refused. Use ConcurrentBag (an unordered collection) and compare it with the queue."}, {"u": "tut-89-concurrent-collections.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 89: Collections for many threads", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 90: Big numbers"}, {"u": "tut-90-big-numbers.html", "t": "Tutorial 90: Big numbers", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-90-big-numbers.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 90: Big numbers", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the limit of a Ring number and why it exists, BigInteger : Parse , Add , Multiply , Pow , Remainder , ModPow , GreatestCommonDivisor , Compare , exact factorials and Fibonacci numbers, Complex : the real and imaginary parts, Magnitude , Multiply . ( Tutorial 57 used Decimal for exact decimals; this lesson is about exact large whole numbers.)"}, {"u": "tut-90-big-numbers.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 90: Big numbers", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 90: numbers that do not fit - BigInteger for exact big whole numbers, Complex numbers and the limits of Ring numbers load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Numerics\" } func main # 1. A Ring number is a double: whole numbers are exact up to 9007199254740992 (2^53). After that, the last digits are lost. ? \"1. Ring: 9007199254740992 + 1 = \" + (9007199254740992 + 1) + \" (the +1 is lost)\" # 2. BigInteger keeps every digit. It is made from a text, and its result is shown as a text. oBig = new BigInteger() nA = oBig.Parse(\"9007199254740992\") nB = oBig.Add(nA,oBig.One) ? "}, {"u": "tut-90-big-numbers.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 90: Big numbers", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The limit of Ring numbers # A double stores 53 bits of precision. Whole numbers are exact up to 2^53 = 9007199254740992; above that, + 1 can be lost, as step 1 shows (it prints the same number). BigInteger # A BigInteger is a struct from System.Numerics . In Ring you create it from a text ( Parse(\"9007199254740992\") ), calculate with the static methods ( Add , Multiply , Pow , ...) and show it with ToString() . The operators + and * of Ring do not work on these objects, so every step is a method call. The first object is a handle to reach the static methods ( new BigInteger() );"}, {"u": "tut-90-big-numbers.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 90: Big numbers", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Ring numbers are exact up to 2^53, BigInteger is created from a text and used through static methods, Pow , ModPow , GreatestCommonDivisor , Compare , Complex with Real , Imaginary and Magnitude ."}, {"u": "tut-90-big-numbers.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 90: Big numbers", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Print 100! and count its digits. Find the first Fibonacci number with 100 digits. Write isEven(oBig) with Remainder , and test it with 2^100 and 2^100 + 1. Multiply two complex numbers by hand and check the result with Multiply ."}, {"u": "tut-90-big-numbers.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 90: Big numbers", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 91: UDP"}, {"u": "tut-91-udp.html", "t": "Tutorial 91: UDP", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-91-udp.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 91: UDP", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the difference between UDP (messages) and TCP (a stream), UdpClient : Send , Receive , ReceiveAsync , Available , binding to a port, and reading the port the system chose, answering the sender, a time limit with Task.Wait(ms) , why a program that uses UDP must expect lost messages. Tutorial 25 used TCP: a connection, then a stream of bytes. UDP has no connection. Everything in this lesson runs on 127.0.0.1 , so it works without a network."}, {"u": "tut-91-udp.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 91: UDP", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 91: UDP - datagrams between two sockets of this computer, Receive with a time limit, broadcast-style replies and lost data load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Net\" \"System.Net.Sockets\" \"System.Text\" } func main oUtf8 = new UTF8Encoding() oLoop = new IPAddress().Loopback # 1. UDP sends separate messages (datagrams) without a connection: no Accept, no stream. A socket that listens binds to a port # (0 = the system chooses a free one); LocalEndPoint tells which. oServer = dnNew(\"UdpClient\",[ :port = 0 ]) nPort = oServer.Client.LocalEndPoint.Port ? \"1. the server s"}, {"u": "tut-91-udp.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 91: UDP", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Datagrams # A UDP message is called a datagram . It is sent in one piece and received in one piece: three Send calls give three Receive results of 3, 12 and 5 bytes, never a joined or a half message (a TCP stream has no such borders). There is no Accept and no stream; a socket that wants to receive only needs a port. new UdpClient([ :port = 0 ]) binds to a port that the system chooses, and Client.LocalEndPoint.Port tells which one. Texts are sent as bytes ( UTF8Encoding.GetBytes ) and read with GetString . Sending # Send has several forms, so it takes names: the bytes ( :dgram )"}, {"u": "tut-91-udp.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 91: UDP", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # UDP sends messages; TCP sends a stream, UdpClient.Send , Receive , ReceiveAsync , Available , answering to the sender's endpoint, a time limit for a receive, UDP gives no promise of delivery."}, {"u": "tut-91-udp.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 91: UDP", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write a small time server : it answers every datagram time with the current time. Send 100 numbered datagrams quickly to a socket that is slow to read and count how many arrive (on one computer the answer is usually all of them: why is that no proof for UDP in general?). Add a resend: the client sends, waits 200 ms for an answer and tries three times. Send a datagram larger than 1500 bytes and look at what Receive returns."}, {"u": "tut-91-udp.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 91: UDP", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 92: More data structures"}, {"u": "tut-92-data-structures.html", "t": "Tutorial 92: More data structures", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-92-data-structures.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 92: More data structures", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # PriorityQueue : always the most urgent item first, SortedSet : no duplicates and always sorted, SortedDictionary : keys in order, LinkedList : cheap changes at both ends and next to a node, BitArray : a million yes/no values in 125 KB, with logic operations, the Ring words that need callMethod ( Get , And , Or ). Tutorial 4 used List and Dictionary , and tutorial 89 the collections for many threads."}, {"u": "tut-92-data-structures.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 92: More data structures", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 92: more data structures - PriorityQueue, SortedSet, SortedDictionary, LinkedList and BitArray load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Collections\" \"System.Collections.Generic\" } func main cInt = \"System.Int32\" cText = \"System.String\" # 1. PriorityQueue<element, priority>: Dequeue always gives the element with the SMALLEST priority, whatever the order of Enqueue. oTasks = dnNewGeneric(\"System.Collections.Generic.PriorityQueue\",[cText,cInt],[]) oTasks.callMethod(\"Enqueue\",[\"write the report\",3]) oTasks.callMethod(\"Enqueue\",[\"fix the server\",1]) oTasks.callMethod(\"En"}, {"u": "tut-92-data-structures.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 92: More data structures", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Closed generic types # All of them but BitArray are generic: dnNewGeneric(type, [type arguments], [constructor values]) makes a closed type such as PriorityQueue<string, int> . They have no Ring class, so the objects are used in the plain style: callMethod(\"Add\", [value]) , getProp(\"Count\") , and toList() to copy a collection into a Ring list ( Core concepts ). PriorityQueue # Enqueue(element, priority) adds; Dequeue() returns the element with the smallest priority number , whatever the order of adding. It is the right tool for a to-do list by urgency, for events by time, and fo"}, {"u": "tut-92-data-structures.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 92: More data structures", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # five collections and what each one is for, the plain style ( callMethod , getProp , toList ) for closed generic types, BitArray operations change their own array, the few Ring words that must be called with callMethod ."}, {"u": "tut-92-data-structures.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 92: More data structures", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make a task list with PriorityQueue where two tasks have the same priority: in which order do they come out? (Do not rely on it.) Use a SortedSet of words to print the words of a text without duplicates in alphabetical order. Find all primes below 1000 with a BitArray (the sieve of Eratosthenes). Build an undo list of the last 5 actions with a LinkedList ."}, {"u": "tut-92-data-structures.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 92: More data structures", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 93: Identifiers and versions"}, {"u": "tut-93-identifiers.html", "t": "Tutorial 93: Identifiers and versions", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-93-identifiers.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 93: Identifiers and versions", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # Guid : a new identifier, its text formats, parsing, Empty , why Guids can be made anywhere without a clash, Version : its parts and its comparison, sorting a list of versions correctly, the version of the running .NET."}, {"u": "tut-93-identifiers.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 93: Identifiers and versions", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 93: identifiers and versions - Guid, Version, and sorting version numbers by number and not as text load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Collections.Generic\" } func main oGuid = new Guid() # 1. A Guid is a 128-bit number that is unique for practical purposes: two programs on two computers can make one each and # never meet the same value. It is shown as 32 hex digits in 5 groups. NewGuid is random: only its shape is the same. cNew = oGuid.NewGuid().ToString() ? \"1. a new Guid has \" + len(cNew) + \" characters, with dashes at 9, 14, 19 and 24: \" + (cNew[9] = \"-\" a"}, {"u": "tut-93-identifiers.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 93: Identifiers and versions", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Guid # A Guid is a 128-bit number. A new one is random, with so many possible values that two programs on two computers never make the same one in practice, so no server or counter has to hand them out. That makes a Guid a good key for a record that is created offline, a name for a temporary file and an id in a message. NewGuid() gives a new one; the output of step 1 shows only its shape (36 characters with dashes in fixed places), because the value is different at every run. Parse reads the usual text; Format(\"N\") writes 32 digits without dashes, \"B\" writes with braces and \"D\" "}, {"u": "tut-93-identifiers.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 93: Identifiers and versions", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Guid : new, parse, formats, Empty , TryParse , a Guid is unique but not secret, Version compares numbers, not text, sorting versions with a List<Version> ."}, {"u": "tut-93-identifiers.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 93: Identifiers and versions", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make 100000 Guids and check that all are different (use a HashSet ). Write isNewer(cA, cB) for two version texts. Read the version of the bridge assembly ( RingDotNet.Bridge ) with reflection. Name a file with a Guid and write a function that finds such files again by their name pattern."}, {"u": "tut-93-identifiers.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 93: Identifiers and versions", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 94: E-mail messages"}, {"u": "tut-94-mail-messages.html", "t": "Tutorial 94: E-mail messages", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-94-mail-messages.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 94: E-mail messages", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # MailAddress : reading Name <user@host> and using it as a check, MailMessage : sender, recipients ( To , CC ), subject, body, properties named like Ring words ( From , To ), AlternateView : the same message as text and as HTML, where sending would happen ( SmtpClient ) and why it is not in the lesson."}, {"u": "tut-94-mail-messages.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 94: E-mail messages", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 94: e-mail messages - addresses, a MailMessage with several recipients, an attachment, a body in HTML and in text (nothing is sent) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Net.Mail\" \"System.Net.Mime\" \"System.Text\" } func main # 1. MailAddress reads \"Name <user@host>\" and splits it. A wrong address raises an error, so it is a check too. oAddress = new MailAddress(\"Ann Lee <ann.lee@example.com>\") ? \"1. display name: \" + oAddress.DisplayName ? \" user: \" + oAddress.User + \", host: \" + oAddress.Host + \", address: \" + oAddress.Address for cText in [\"bob@examp"}, {"u": "tut-94-mail-messages.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 94: E-mail messages", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Addresses # new MailAddress(\"Ann Lee <ann.lee@example.com>\") splits the text into DisplayName , User , Host and Address . A text that is not an address raises an error, so the function isAddress uses try / catch ( tutorial 7 ) to turn the error into a yes or no. The check is about the form ( user@host ); it cannot know whether the mailbox exists. bob@ and @example.com are refused, bob@example.com is accepted. Note .NET is generous with display names: bob example@x.com is read as the name bob and the address example@x.com . If your program needs exactly one address in a text box,"}, {"u": "tut-94-mail-messages.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 94: E-mail messages", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # MailAddress splits and checks an address, MailMessage has sender, recipients, subject and body, From and To work as normal properties, alternate views give text and HTML in one mail, sending is a separate step that needs a server."}, {"u": "tut-94-mail-messages.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 94: E-mail messages", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write addressList(cText) that splits a@x.com; b@y.com into a list of MailAddress objects and skips the wrong ones. Add a Bcc recipient and print all the recipients of the mail. Add an attachment ( Attachments.Add ) made from a file of tutorial 5 and print its name. Read about MailMessage.Headers and add the header X-Mailer ."}, {"u": "tut-94-mail-messages.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 94: E-mail messages", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 95: Identities and claims"}, {"u": "tut-95-claims.html", "t": "Tutorial 95: Identities and claims", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-95-claims.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 95: Identities and claims", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # a claim : a type and a value, ClaimsIdentity (claims from one source) and ClaimsPrincipal (the user), FindFirst , FindAll and HasClaim , roles and IsInRole , a permission check where the rules are data. ASP.NET Core uses exactly these classes for every login, so what you learn here is what a web program ( tutorial 16 ) receives after the user signed in. Tutorial 49 made a login with a password hash; this lesson is about what happens after the login."}, {"u": "tut-95-claims.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 95: Identities and claims", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 95: identities and claims - ClaimsIdentity, ClaimsPrincipal, roles, claim types and a simple permission check load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Security.Claims\" } func main # A claim is a statement about a user: a type and a value (\"Role\" = \"Admin\", \"Email\" = \"ann@example.com\"). # An identity is a list of claims from one source; a principal holds identities. ASP.NET uses these classes for every login. oIdentity = dnNew(\"ClaimsIdentity\",[ :authenticationType = \"Password\" ]) oIdentity.AddClaim(dnNew(\"Claim\",[ :type = \"Name\", :value = \"Ann\" ])) oIdentity.AddClai"}, {"u": "tut-95-claims.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 95: Identities and claims", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Claims, identities, principals # A claim is one statement about a user: Name = Ann , Email = ann@example.com , Role = Editor . The type is just a text; .NET has standard names ( ClaimTypes.Email is a long address-like text) but your own short names work in your own program. A user can have several claims of the same type, like the two roles here. An identity is a list of claims that came from one source (a password login, a certificate, a token) together with the name of that source ( AuthenticationType ). A principal is the user as the program sees them, and can hold several id"}, {"u": "tut-95-claims.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 95: Identities and claims", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # claims, identities and principals, FindFirst , FindAll , HasClaim and IsInRole , roleType and nameType , a permission check driven by a list of rules."}, {"u": "tut-95-claims.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 95: Identities and claims", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add the claim Department = Sales and write a rule that allows delete only for the Sales department. Make hasAllRoles(oPrincipal, aRoles) . Serialize the claims of a user to JSON ( tutorial 80 ) and build the identity again from the JSON. Make an identity with no authentication type and look at IsAuthenticated ."}, {"u": "tut-95-claims.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 95: Identities and claims", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 96: Tar archives"}, {"u": "tut-96-tar.html", "t": "Tutorial 96: Tar archives", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-96-tar.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 96: Tar archives", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a tar file is and how it differs from ZIP and GZip, making a tar file from a folder ( TarFile.CreateFromDirectory ), listing the entries with TarReader , reading one file without unpacking the rest, unpacking ( TarFile.ExtractToDirectory )."}, {"u": "tut-96-tar.html#zip-gzip-and-tar", "t": "Tutorial 96: Tar archives", "h": "ZIP, GZip and tar", "x": "ZIP, GZip and tar # Format What it does ZIP ( tutorial 5 ) collects files and compresses each one GZip ( tutorial 88 ) compresses one stream of data tar collects files and folders into one file, without compressing Linux programs combine the last two: a .tar.gz (or .tgz ) file is a tar archive compressed with GZip. Tar also keeps things that ZIP does not always keep, such as Unix permissions and links."}, {"u": "tut-96-tar.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 96: Tar archives", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 96: tar archives - make a .tar from a folder, list it, read one file from it and unpack it (System.Formats.Tar) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Formats.Tar\" } func main oPath = new Path() cBase = oPath.Combine([oPath.GetTempPath(),\"ringdotnet_tutorial96\"]) cSource = oPath.Combine([cBase,\"source\"]) cTar = oPath.Combine([cBase,\"backup.tar\"]) cTarget = oPath.Combine([cBase,\"unpacked\"]) oDirectory = new Directory() oFile = new File() if oDirectory.Exists(cBase) oDirectory.Delete([ :path = cBase, :recursive = True ]) ok oDirectory.CreateDirectory(oPa"}, {"u": "tut-96-tar.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 96: Tar archives", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Making the archive # TarFile.CreateFromDirectory(source folder, tar file, include the folder itself) packs a whole folder. It is a static method of a static class, so the program calls it with dnStatic(type, method, [arguments]) with the arguments in order. Here the folder itself is not included, so the entries are readme.txt and notes/a.txt , not source/readme.txt . A tar file is built from blocks of 512 bytes, which step 1 checks: the size is a multiple of 512. Reading the entries # A TarReader is made on a stream ( dnNew(\"TarReader\",[ :archiveStream = oStream ]) ). GetNextEnt"}, {"u": "tut-96-tar.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 96: Tar archives", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # tar collects files without compressing them, CreateFromDirectory and ExtractToDirectory with dnStatic , TarReader , GetNextEntry , EntryType , DataStream , reading is sequential, the danger of names that leave the target folder."}, {"u": "tut-96-tar.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 96: Tar archives", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make a .tar.gz : create the tar file, then compress it with GZipStream ( tutorial 88 ). Write listTar(cFile) that returns the names of the entries as a Ring list. Write a function that adds one more file to an archive with TarWriter . Make an archive with an empty folder and look at how it is listed."}, {"u": "tut-96-tar.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 96: Tar archives", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 97: Comparing text"}, {"u": "tut-97-comparers.html", "t": "Tutorial 97: Comparing text", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-97-comparers.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 97: Comparing text", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # ordinal comparison (the code of each character) and culture-aware comparison (the rules of a language), ignoring case, String.Compare and what its result means, StringComparer : a comparer that you give to Sort , a dictionary or a set, why \"TITLE\".ToLower() can be wrong in Turkish. Tutorial 33 formatted numbers and dates for a culture, and tutorial 52 looked at characters. This lesson is about comparing text, which every search box, sort order and dictionary of names needs."}, {"u": "tut-97-comparers.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 97: Comparing text", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 97: comparing text - ordinal and culture comparison, ignoring case, StringComparer and a sorted set that ignores case load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Globalization\" \"System.Collections.Generic\" } func main oString = new String() # 1. Two texts can be compared in different ways. Ordinal compares the code of every character: fast, exact, the same on every # computer. Culture-aware comparison follows the rules of a language (in German \"\u00e4\" is close to \"a\"). Both can ignore case. ? \"1. 'apple' = 'APPLE'\" ? \" ordinal: \" + (oString.Compare([ :strA = \"apple\", :strB"}, {"u": "tut-97-comparers.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 97: Comparing text", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Ordinal and culture # Ordinal comparison looks at the number (code) of every character. It is exact and fast and gives the same answer on every computer, so it is the right choice for technical text: file names, identifiers, keys, protocols. Culture comparison follows the rules of a language: letters with accents are close to their base letters and a and A are neighbours. Use it for text that people read, such as a sorted list of names on the screen. The comparison type is an enum, so it is a text: \"Ordinal\" , \"OrdinalIgnoreCase\" , \"InvariantCulture\" , \"InvariantCultureIgnoreCas"}, {"u": "tut-97-comparers.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 97: Comparing text", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # ordinal for technical text, culture for text that people read, the sign of the result of Compare , StringComparer for sorting, dictionaries and sets, case-insensitive lookups with OrdinalIgnoreCase , the Turkish i and why the invariant culture is safer."}, {"u": "tut-97-comparers.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 97: Comparing text", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Sort the file names of a folder ( tutorial 5 ) in ordinal order and in culture order and look at the difference. Build a case-insensitive Dictionary of settings ( tutorial 32 ). Compare \"Stra\u00dfe\" and \"STRASSE\" with the four comparison types. Write sameName(a, b) for user names that must ignore case and accents (look at CompareOptions )."}, {"u": "tut-97-comparers.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 97: Comparing text", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 98: Memory and the garbage collector"}, {"u": "tut-98-memory.html", "t": "Tutorial 98: Memory and the garbage collector", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-98-memory.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 98: Memory and the garbage collector", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what the garbage collector (GC) does and why you rarely call it, GC.GetTotalMemory , GC.Collect , GC.CollectionCount , WeakReference : a pointer that does not keep an object alive, generations 0, 1 and 2, handles: how Ring frees .NET objects for you ( dnFlush() and release() )."}, {"u": "tut-98-memory.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 98: Memory and the garbage collector", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 98: memory and the garbage collector - how much memory .NET uses, GC.Collect, WeakReference, generations and a big array load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Text\" } func main oGC = new GC() # 1. .NET frees the memory of objects that nothing uses any more: the garbage collector (GC). The numbers depend on the computer and # the moment, so the program prints only what is always true. nBefore = oGC.GetTotalMemory(True) ? \"1. the program uses memory: \" + (nBefore > 0) ? \" the number of generations: \" + (oGC.MaxGeneration + 1) + \" (0 = new objects, 1 = i"}, {"u": "tut-98-memory.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 98: Memory and the garbage collector", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The garbage collector # In .NET you never free an object by hand. The GC finds the objects that no variable can reach any more and frees them. You do not choose the moment: it runs when memory gets short, or when the runtime decides. GC.Collect() forces a collection, and GC.GetTotalMemory(True) collects first and then returns the number of bytes in use. Both are useful for learning and testing ; in a real program calling Collect is almost always wrong, because the GC knows better than you when to run (and a forced collection can slow the program). The numbers (bytes in use, how "}, {"u": "tut-98-memory.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 98: Memory and the garbage collector", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # the GC frees unreachable objects; you do not choose when, GetTotalMemory , Collect and CollectionCount for learning and tests, Ring frees the handle of a dropped object for you; Dispose() frees resources, callgc() with dnFlush() , or release() , make it immediate, WeakReference for caches, generations: young objects are collected often."}, {"u": "tut-98-memory.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 98: Memory and the garbage collector", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make 100 000 StringBuilder objects with a capacity of 4000 in a loop and look at GC.GetTotalMemory after the loop, with and without callgc() and dnFlush() . Build a small cache of results with WeakReference objects in a Ring list. Find out how many collections of generation 0, 1 and 2 happened in your program ( CollectionCount ). Read about GCSettings.IsServerGC and GCSettings.LatencyMode and print them."}, {"u": "tut-98-memory.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 98: Memory and the garbage collector", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 99: Vectors and matrices"}, {"u": "tut-99-vectors.html", "t": "Tutorial 99: Vectors and matrices", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-99-vectors.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 99: Vectors and matrices", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # Vector2 , Vector3 : a point or a direction, Length , Distance , Normalize , Add , Subtract , Multiply , Negate and Lerp , the dot product and the cross product and what they tell you, Matrix3x2 : a move, a rotation, and both together. Tutorial 14 moved shapes with Ring numbers. With vectors you describe a position as one value, and a transformation as one matrix, so the same code works for one point or for a thousand."}, {"u": "tut-99-vectors.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 99: Vectors and matrices", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 99: vectors and matrices - Vector3 (length, dot and cross product, distance), Vector2 and a rotation with Matrix3x2 load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Numerics\" } func main # 1. A Vector3 holds three numbers (X, Y, Z): a point or a direction in space (Vector2 has two numbers, Vector4 four). # The numbers are single-precision floats, so results are shown rounded. oA = dnNew(\"Vector3\",[ :x = 3, :y = 4, :z = 0 ]) oB = dnNew(\"Vector3\",[ :x = 1, :y = 2, :z = 3 ]) ? \"1. a = \" + show3(oA) + \", length of a = \" + oA.Length() + \", squared length = \" + oA.LengthSquared()"}, {"u": "tut-99-vectors.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 99: Vectors and matrices", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Vectors # A Vector3 holds three numbers X , Y , Z . It can be a position (a point in space) or a direction with a length (a speed, a force). Vector2 has two numbers and Vector4 four. They are value types (structs): dnNew(\"Vector3\",[ :x = 3, :y = 4, :z = 0 ]) makes one by names, and oV.X reads a component. The numbers are single-precision floats (about 7 digits), so a result such as 0.6 can really be 0.6000000238 ; the program rounds before printing ( roundNumber ), and so should yours when you compare. Operations # The operations are static methods : Add(a, b) , Subtract(a, b) ,"}, {"u": "tut-99-vectors.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 99: Vectors and matrices", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # vectors as points and directions, length, distance, unit vectors and Lerp , the dot product (a number) and the cross product (a vector), matrices that move and rotate, and combining them, the order of transformations matters."}, {"u": "tut-99-vectors.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 99: Vectors and matrices", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write angleBetween(a, b) with Dot , Length and Acos ( tutorial 3 ). Move a point along a straight line from A to B in 10 steps with Lerp and print the points. Rotate the four corners of a square by 45 degrees and print them. Try move first, then turn and compare the result with step 6."}, {"u": "tut-99-vectors.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 99: Vectors and matrices", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 100: Writing JSON as a stream"}, {"u": "tut-100-json-writer.html", "t": "Tutorial 100: Writing JSON as a stream", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-100-json-writer.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 100: Writing JSON as a stream", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # Utf8JsonWriter : start and end of objects and arrays, properties and values, indented and compact output ( JsonWriterOptions ), Flush and why the writer needs it, the escaping that is done for you, and the errors for invalid structure, JsonSerializer and JsonSerializerOptions for objects and collections. Tutorial 6 read and wrote JSON as a tree and tutorial 80 built it from nodes. A tree is easy to change but it keeps the whole document in memory. A writer is the other style: you say what comes next, and it goes straight to a stream (a file, a network connection, a Memory"}, {"u": "tut-100-json-writer.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 100: Writing JSON as a stream", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 100: writing JSON as a stream - Utf8JsonWriter, indentation, nesting, escaping, and JsonSerializer options load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Text\" \"System.Text.Json\" } func main # 1. Utf8JsonWriter writes a JSON text piece by piece into a stream, without building a tree in memory first. # It is the right tool for big documents and for output that must be exactly formatted. oStream = new MemoryStream oOptions = dnNew(\"JsonWriterOptions\",[]) oOptions.Indented = True oWriter = dnNew(\"Utf8JsonWriter\",[ :utf8Json = oStream, :options = oOptions ]) oWrit"}, {"u": "tut-100-json-writer.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 100: Writing JSON as a stream", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Writing the document # The writer is made on a stream ( dnNew(\"Utf8JsonWriter\",[ :utf8Json = oStream, :options = oOptions ]) ). Then you call, in order, what the document contains: Call Writes WriteStartObject(NULL) / WriteEndObject() { and } (with a name, WriteStartObject(\"address\") , it is a property of the object around it) WriteStartArray(\"skills\") / WriteEndArray() [ and ] WriteString(\"name\",\"Ann\") a property with a text value WriteNumber(\"age\",31) , WriteBoolean(\"active\",True) , WriteNull(\"manager\") properties with other values WriteStringValue(\"ring\") , WriteNumberValue(4"}, {"u": "tut-100-json-writer.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 100: Writing JSON as a stream", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Utf8JsonWriter writes JSON into a stream, token by token, indented and compact forms, Flush and Dispose , escaping and structure checks are done for you, JsonSerializer for whole objects."}, {"u": "tut-100-json-writer.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 100: Writing JSON as a stream", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write an array of 1000 objects with id and square into a file ( tutorial 5 ), and read it back with JsonDocument ( tutorial 6 ). Write the nested folder structure of a directory as JSON with a recursive function. Make JsonSerializerOptions with PropertyNamingPolicy = JsonNamingPolicy.CamelCase and look at the names. Write a number with many decimals with WriteNumber and look at how it is written."}, {"u": "tut-100-json-writer.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 100: Writing JSON as a stream", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 101: Number types"}, {"u": "tut-101-number-types.html", "t": "Tutorial 101: Number types", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-101-number-types.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 101: Number types", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the integer types and their limits, MinValue and MaxValue , checked conversion with Convert (an error, not a wrong number), TryParse for text that a user typed, integer division and the remainder ( Math.DivRem ), Int128 and UInt128 , Single and Double , and comparing numbers with decimals. Tutorial 3 used numbers and dates, tutorial 57 used Decimal for money and tutorial 90 used BigInteger . This lesson is about the fixed-size types that .NET methods ask for."}, {"u": "tut-101-number-types.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 101: Number types", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 101: number types - the limits of Byte, Int16, Int32, Int64 and Int128, overflow, checked conversion, Half and Single load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" } func main # A Ring number is always a double. .NET has many number types with different sizes and limits. When a number goes to .NET, # the bridge uses the type the method asks for, and a value that does not fit raises an error: it does not wrap around. oByte = new Byte() oShort = new Int16() oInt = new Int32() oLong = new Int64() # 1. The limits of each type (MinValue and MaxValue are static fields) ? \"1. the limit"}, {"u": "tut-101-number-types.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 101: Number types", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Many types, one Ring number # In Ring there is one number type, a double (exact for whole numbers up to 9007199254740992). .NET has Byte (0 to 255), Int16 (about plus or minus 32 thousand), Int32 (about plus or minus 2 thousand million), Int64 , Int128 and others, signed and unsigned, and Single , Double and Decimal for fractions. When Ring calls a method, the bridge converts the Ring number into the type the method asks for. A number that does not fit raises an error instead of wrapping around, so a wrong value is found at the call. Byte.MaxValue and the other limits are static"}, {"u": "tut-101-number-types.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 101: Number types", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # one Ring number, many .NET types, limits with MinValue and MaxValue , Convert.ToByte and the others raise an error when the number does not fit, TryParse for user input, DivRem , Int128 , Single against Double , compare fractions with a small difference."}, {"u": "tut-101-number-types.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 101: Number types", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write readPort(cText) that returns a port number (1 to 65535) or -1, with UInt16.TryParse . Find the first number that a Single can not increase by 1 (16777216) with a loop of Convert.ToSingle . Print the limits of SByte , UInt16 , UInt32 and UInt64 . Add the amounts 0.1 ten times in a loop and compare the sum with 1 in both ways."}, {"u": "tut-101-number-types.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 101: Number types", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 102: IP networks"}, {"u": "tut-102-ip-networks.html", "t": "Tutorial 102: IP networks", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-102-ip-networks.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 102: IP networks", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the CIDR notation ( 192.168.1.0/24 ), IPNetwork : TryParse , BaseAddress , PrefixLength , Contains , how many addresses a network has, what a wrong network looks like, an allow list for private addresses (RFC 1918), IPv6. Tutorial 81 used IPAddress and IPEndPoint . This lesson goes one level up: a range of addresses. A web program ( tutorial 16 ) uses it to allow or refuse a client by its address."}, {"u": "tut-102-ip-networks.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 102: IP networks", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 102: IP networks - CIDR notation, the addresses of a subnet, \"is this address in that network?\" and a small allow list load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Net\" } func main # A network is written in CIDR form: an address and the number of bits that are the same for the whole network. # 192.168.1.0/24 means: the first 24 bits (192.168.1) are fixed, the last 8 bits (0 to 255) are free. oNetworkClass = new IPNetwork() aParsed = oNetworkClass.TryParse(\"192.168.1.0/24\") oNetwork = aParsed[2] ? \"1. network: \" + oNetwork.ToString() ? \" base address: \" + oNetwork.BaseAd"}, {"u": "tut-102-ip-networks.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 102: IP networks", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # CIDR notation # An IPv4 address has 32 bits. A network is written as an address and a prefix length : the number of bits at the start that are the same for every address of the network. In 192.168.1.0/24 the first 24 bits ( 192.168.1 ) are fixed and the last 8 bits are free, so the network holds the addresses 192.168.1.0 to 192.168.1.255 . IPNetwork.TryParse(text) answers [ success, network ] ( tutorial 81 ); the network has BaseAddress , PrefixLength and ToString() . Contains # oNetwork.Contains(address) tells whether an IPAddress is inside the range. The first and the last add"}, {"u": "tut-102-ip-networks.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 102: IP networks", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # CIDR: an address and a prefix length, IPNetwork.TryParse , BaseAddress , PrefixLength , Contains , the size of a network, 2 to the power of (bits \u2212 prefix), the base address rule, an allow list in a Ring list."}, {"u": "tut-102-ip-networks.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 102: IP networks", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write networkOf(cAddress, nPrefix) that returns the network that contains an address. Write an allow list that reads its networks from a settings file ( tutorial 32 ). Use the list in tutorial 55 so that only addresses of 127.0.0.0/8 can see the page. Print the first and last address of 192.168.1.64/26 ."}, {"u": "tut-102-ip-networks.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 102: IP networks", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 103: Dates and times"}, {"u": "tut-103-dates-and-times.html", "t": "Tutorial 103: Dates and times", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-103-dates-and-times.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 103: Dates and times", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # DateOnly and TimeOnly : say in the type that you have only a date, or only a time, TimeSpan : the length between two moments, DateTimeOffset : a moment together with its distance from UTC, what AddMonths and AddYears do at the end of a month, counting days and computing an age, reading a date with an exact format. Tutorial 3 made DateTime values and tutorial 68 converted between time zones. This lesson adds the types that make a program clearer and safer."}, {"u": "tut-103-dates-and-times.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 103: Dates and times", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 103: dates and times - DateOnly, TimeOnly, DateTimeOffset, TimeSpan and calculations that do not break load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Globalization\" } func main # 1. DateTime mixes a date and a time. When you only need ONE of them, DateOnly and TimeOnly say it in the type: a birthday has no # time, an opening hour has no date. oDate = makeDate(2024,2,28) ? \"1. a date: \" + oDate.Format(\"yyyy-MM-dd\") + \", \" + oDate.DayOfWeek + \", day \" + oDate.DayOfYear + \" of the year\" oNextDay = oDate.AddDays(2) ? \" 2 days later: \" + oNextDay.Format(\"yyyy-MM-dd\") + \" (2024"}, {"u": "tut-103-dates-and-times.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 103: Dates and times", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Date and time apart # A DateTime always has both a date and a time, so a birthday is midnight on that day and an opening hour is that hour on 1 January of year 1 . Neither is true. DateOnly is a day (year, month, day) and TimeOnly a time of day, so the program cannot mix them up. DateOnly knows the DayOfWeek and the DayOfYear (the 28th of February is day 59) and AddDays goes over the end of the month and over a leap day. TimeOnly.AddHours(3) after 22:30 gives 01:30: a time of day goes round the clock and has no date to move to. These structs have several constructors, so they ar"}, {"u": "tut-103-dates-and-times.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 103: Dates and times", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # DateOnly and TimeOnly make the meaning clear, TimeSpan parts against totals, DateTimeOffset is a real moment, AddMonths goes to the last day that exists, how to count an age, TryParseExact for dates that people type."}, {"u": "tut-103-dates-and-times.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 103: Dates and times", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write lastDayOfMonth(year, month) with DateOnly and AddMonths . Print the next 6 due dates of a bill that is due on the 31st (add months to the first date). Write daysUntil(date) that counts the days to the next birthday. Show the same moment as a DateTimeOffset in three offsets."}, {"u": "tut-103-dates-and-times.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 103: Dates and times", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 104: Text readers and writers"}, {"u": "tut-104-text-readers-writers.html", "t": "Tutorial 104: Text readers and writers", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-104-text-readers-writers.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 104: Text readers and writers", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # StringWriter and StringReader , the base classes TextWriter and TextReader , and why a function should take one, reading line by line with ReadLine and Peek , Console.SetOut to capture output, composite formatting with String.Format ( {0} , alignment, formats). Tutorial 5 wrote and read files and tutorial 56 explained streams. A string can be a stream too, and that is very useful."}, {"u": "tut-104-text-readers-writers.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 104: Text readers and writers", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 104: text readers and writers - StringWriter and StringReader, TextWriter in functions, and capturing Console output load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Text\" } func main # 1. A StringWriter is a TextWriter that writes into a string in memory. Code that writes to \"a TextWriter\" does not care whether it # is a file, the console or a string: that makes it easy to test and to reuse. oWriter = new StringWriter oWriter.WriteLine(\"first line\") oWriter.Write(\"second \") oWriter.Write(\"line\") oWriter.WriteLine(NULL) oWriter.WriteLine(\"third line\") cText = su"}, {"u": "tut-104-text-readers-writers.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 104: Text readers and writers", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # StringWriter # A StringWriter has the same methods as a StreamWriter ( Write , WriteLine ), but it collects the text in memory; ToString() gives it back. Use it to build a longer text piece by piece, and above all as a test double for a file or the console. WriteLine(NULL) ends the current line without adding text. (Windows ends a line with two characters, CR LF , and Linux with one, LF ; the program removes char(13) so the output is the same everywhere.) StringReader # StringReader reads a text like a file: ReadLine gives one line, ReadToEnd the rest. It has no EndOfStream ; Pe"}, {"u": "tut-104-text-readers-writers.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 104: Text readers and writers", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # StringWriter and StringReader for text in memory, Peek and ReadLine to read all lines, functions that take a TextWriter can write anywhere, Console.SetOut captures output (and must be undone), String.Format with places, alignment and formats."}, {"u": "tut-104-text-readers-writers.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 104: Text readers and writers", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write numberedLines(oReader, oWriter) that copies a text and puts a line number before each line. Write a test that captures the output of Console.WriteLine and checks it. Make a table with String.Format and aligned columns for a list of products and prices. Read a CSV text with StringReader and Split ( tutorial 2 )."}, {"u": "tut-104-text-readers-writers.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 104: Text readers and writers", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 105: Digital signatures"}, {"u": "tut-105-signatures.html", "t": "Tutorial 105: Digital signatures", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-105-signatures.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 105: Digital signatures", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a signature proves, and what it does not, private and public keys, ECDsa : making a key, SignData , VerifyData , why a changed message or a different key fails, sharing the public key and nothing else. Tutorial 8 used hashes and encryption and tutorial 78 used RSA and more. A signature adds the answer to \"who?\" ."}, {"u": "tut-105-signatures.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 105: Digital signatures", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 105: digital signatures - ECDsa keys, signing a message, verifying it, and what happens when the message or the key changes load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Security.Cryptography\" \"System.Text\" } func main oUtf8 = new UTF8Encoding() # A digital signature proves two things about a message: who sent it (only the owner of the PRIVATE key can make the signature) and # that it was not changed (the signature fits only this exact message). Everybody with the PUBLIC key can check it. # ECDsa uses elliptic curves: small keys and fast signatures. (tutorial 78 used RSA"}, {"u": "tut-105-signatures.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 105: Digital signatures", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # What a signature proves # A key pair has two parts that fit each other. The private key stays with its owner and makes signatures. The public key can be given to everybody and only checks signatures. A valid signature proves two things: the message was signed by the owner of the private key , and the message is exactly the signed one \u2014 changing a single character makes the check fail. It does not hide the message (that is encryption) and it does not prove that the owner is the person you think; for that you need a certificate from somebody you trust. ECDsa # ECDsa uses elliptic "}, {"u": "tut-105-signatures.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 105: Digital signatures", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # a signature proves the owner and the integrity, not secrecy, the private key signs and the public key verifies, ECCurve.CreateFromFriendlyName , ECDsa.Create , SignData , VerifyData , a changed message or another key fails, export the public parameters and keep the private key."}, {"u": "tut-105-signatures.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 105: Digital signatures", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Sign a file ( tutorial 5 ) and write the signature to another file; verify both later. Write signedMessage(text) that returns the text and its signature as Base64 in a JSON object ( tutorial 100 ). Change one byte of the signature and check that verification fails. Try the curve nistP384 and look at the size of the signature."}, {"u": "tut-105-signatures.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 105: Digital signatures", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 106: Arrays"}, {"u": "tut-106-arrays.html", "t": "Tutorial 106: Arrays", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-106-arrays.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 106: Arrays", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # how a .NET array differs from a Ring list, making a typed array ( List<T>.ToArray() ), toList() to read an array in Ring, the Array methods: Sort , Reverse , IndexOf , BinarySearch , Copy , Clear , zero-based positions, an array with two dimensions. Tutorial 4 used lists and dictionaries, tutorial 45 used arrays of bytes and tutorial 92 other collections. Arrays are the base of all of them."}, {"u": "tut-106-arrays.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 106: Arrays", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 106: arrays - Ring lists and .NET arrays, Array.Sort, Reverse, IndexOf, BinarySearch, Copy and Clear, and arrays with two dimensions load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" } func main oArray = new Array() # 1. A .NET array has a fixed size and one type. A Ring list becomes an array when a method asks for one: the bridge makes an int[], # string[] or double[] from the values of the list. An array that comes back is a DotNetObject: toList() makes a Ring list of it. aNumbers = [42,7,19,3,25] oSorted = dnStatic(\"System.Array\",\"Sort\",[aNumbers]) ? \"1. a list given to Array.Sor"}, {"u": "tut-106-arrays.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 106: Arrays", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Arrays and lists # A .NET array has a fixed length and one type ( int[] , string[] , double[] ), so it is compact and fast and an array of numbers never holds a text. A Ring list can change size and hold any mix. When a method wants an array and you pass a list, the bridge makes an array from the values (an int[] for whole numbers, a string[] for texts) for that call only . The array that the method sorts or changes is a copy : in step 1 the Ring list is unchanged after Array.Sort . To work on an array you must have the array, as a .NET object. Making an array # The easy way is "}, {"u": "tut-106-arrays.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 106: Arrays", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # an array has a fixed size and one type; a list given to .NET becomes an array copy, typed arrays from List<T>.ToArray() and back with toList() , Sort , Reverse , IndexOf , BinarySearch , Copy , Clear , positions start at 0, BinarySearch needs a sorted array and answers a negative number for \"not found\", two dimensions with CreateInstance and GetLength_ ."}, {"u": "tut-106-arrays.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 106: Arrays", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write contains(oArray, value) with IndexOf . Fill an array with 100 000 random numbers ( tutorial 69 ), sort it and compare IndexOf and BinarySearch with a Stopwatch . Use BinarySearch to insert a value into a sorted list at the right place. Fill a 9 x 9 Double array with the multiplication table and print it."}, {"u": "tut-106-arrays.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 106: Arrays", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 107: Sets"}, {"u": "tut-107-sets.html", "t": "Tutorial 107: Sets", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-107-sets.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 107: Sets", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a set is and why it is fast, HashSet : Add , Remove , Count , UnionWith , IntersectWith , ExceptWith , SymmetricExceptWith , IsSubsetOf , IsSupersetOf , Overlaps , SetEquals , a set with a comparer (case ignored), removing duplicates from a list. Tutorial 92 showed SortedSet , a set that keeps its values in order. HashSet does not keep an order, and it is faster."}, {"u": "tut-107-sets.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 107: Sets", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 107: sets - HashSet, union, intersection, difference, subsets, and how a set removes duplicates and finds common items fast load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" } func main cText = \"System.String\" # A set holds each value ONCE and has no order. Adding a value that is already there changes nothing. Looking for a value is # very fast, even with a million values, because a set uses a hash table (a list has to look at every item). oSetA = makeSet([\"ring\",\"dotnet\",\"linq\",\"json\",\"web\"]) oSetB = makeSet([\"web\",\"json\",\"tcp\",\"udp\",\"ring\",\"ring\"]) ? \"1. A has \" + oSetA.getProp(\"C"}, {"u": "tut-107-sets.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 107: Sets", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # One copy of each value # A set holds a value once . The list for set B has six words, ring twice, and the set has five items. Add answers whether the value was new ( true ) or already there ( false ); Remove takes a value away. There is no order : the items come out in an order that you must not depend on, so the program sorts them before printing. The set is stored in a hash table : a value is turned into a number that points at the place where it lives, so Contains takes the same short time for ten values and for ten million, while a list has to look at every item. When a prog"}, {"u": "tut-107-sets.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 107: Sets", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # a set keeps one copy of each value and finds values fast, Add tells whether the value was new, union, intersection, difference and symmetric difference change the set, so copy first, IsSubsetOf , IsSupersetOf , Overlaps and SetEquals , comparers and removing duplicates."}, {"u": "tut-107-sets.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 107: Sets", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Find the words that two text files have in common ( tutorial 5 ). Check that a user has all the permissions of a list with IsSupersetOf ( tutorial 95 ). Count the different words of a text ( tutorial 2 ). Compare Contains in a HashSet and in a Ring list with 100 000 items and a Stopwatch ."}, {"u": "tut-107-sets.html#next", "t": "Tutorial 107: Sets", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tutorial 108: ZIP archives in memory"}, {"u": "tut-108-zip-in-memory.html", "t": "Tutorial 108: ZIP archives in memory", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-108-zip-in-memory.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tutorial 108: ZIP archives in memory", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # ZipArchive on a MemoryStream (modes Create , Read , Update ), CreateEntry , Entries , GetEntry , Delete , why Dispose is needed, compression levels, checking the names of entries. Tutorial 5 made a ZIP file from a folder, tutorial 88 compressed streams and tutorial 96 made tar archives. This lesson works with the entries of an archive one by one."}, {"u": "tut-108-zip-in-memory.html#the-program", "t": "Tutorial 108: ZIP archives in memory", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Tutorial 108: ZIP archives in memory - ZipArchive on a MemoryStream, entries, reading one file, compression levels, and updating an archive load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.IO.Compression\" \"System.Text\" } func main oUtf8 = new UTF8Encoding() # 1. A ZIP archive does not have to be a file. ZipArchive works on any stream: a MemoryStream keeps the whole archive in memory, which is # what a web program does when it sends a download, or a test that does not want to leave files on the disk. oMemory = new MemoryStream oZip = dnNew(\"ZipArchive\",[ :stream = oMemory"}, {"u": "tut-108-zip-in-memory.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tutorial 108: ZIP archives in memory", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The stream # ZipArchive is made on a stream and a mode: dnNew(\"ZipArchive\",[ :stream = oMemory, :mode = \"Create\", :leaveOpen = True ]) . leaveOpen = True keeps the MemoryStream open when the archive is closed, so the program can read the bytes afterwards ( ToArray() ), send them or open the archive again. Without it, closing the archive also closes the stream. The mode is an enum, so it is a text. Creating entries # CreateEntry(\"data/names.txt\") makes an entry (a file inside the archive; folders are just a / in the name) and Open() gives a stream to write its content, here a Str"}, {"u": "tut-108-zip-in-memory.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tutorial 108: ZIP archives in memory", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # ZipArchive on a MemoryStream with leaveOpen , CreateEntry , Entries , GetEntry , Open , Delete , the archive must be disposed to be complete, compression levels, and small files that grow, check entry names before you unpack."}, {"u": "tut-108-zip-in-memory.html#exercises", "t": "Tutorial 108: ZIP archives in memory", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write zipFiles(aNames) that returns the bytes of an archive and send them in the web server of tutorial 55 . Write listArchive(oStream) that returns the entry names as a Ring list. Compare the five levels for a real text file and a JPEG picture. Add a comment to an entry ( Comment ) and read it back."}, {"u": "tut-108-zip-in-memory.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "Tutorial 108: ZIP archives in memory", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # You have reached the end of the tutorials. Keep Core concepts , the class reference and Gotchas & limits at hand, and read the applications for bigger programs."}, {"u": "concepts.html", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "concepts.html#classes-with-the-same-names-as-net", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Classes with the same names as .NET", "x": "Classes with the same names as .NET # After using { \"Namespace\" } every public type of that namespace is a Ring class with the same short name and the same members: using { \"System.Text\" } oSB = new StringBuilder(\"Hello\") # with arguments: parentheses (Ring calls init() only with them) oSB.Append(\", Ring\").Append(\"!\") # methods that return a wrapped class return that class ? oSB.ToString() ? oSB.Length # properties and fields are attributes new Console().WriteLine(\"Hi\") # static members are called on an instance ? new Math().Max(3,9) Hello from .NET Max(3,9) = 9 Sqrt(144) = 12 PI = 3.14 Hello,"}, {"u": "concepts.html#short-names-for-static-classes", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Short names for static classes", "x": "Short names for static classes # A class such as Math , Path or File has only static members, so new Math() just makes an object that gives access to them. If you prefer the .NET style, make that object once at the top of the program and give it the name of the class: load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" } Math = new Math() Path = new Path() func main ? Math.Max(3,9) # 9 ? Path.Combine([\"a\",\"b\"]) # a\\b ? new Math().Min(3,9) # the class is still there: 3 The names of Ring variables are not case sensitive, so a variable path is the same name as Path , and a local variable with that na"}, {"u": "concepts.html#naming-rules", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Naming rules", "x": "Naming rules # Ring has no method overloading, so a .NET method name that has several overloads becomes one Ring method with one parameter ; you choose the overload with the value you pass (see Named arguments below). A name with a single form keeps the .NET parameters. .NET Ring Rule Append() , Append(string) , Append(char, int) Append(NULL) , Append(\"x\") , Append([ :value = \"x\", :repeatCount = 3 ]) One method: NULL = no parameters, a value = one parameter, a **list of :name = value ** = the overload with those parameter names. Math.Max(a, b) Max(3,9) A name with only one number of parameters"}, {"u": "concepts.html#named-arguments", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Named arguments", "x": "Named arguments # Ring has no optional or overloaded parameters, but it has a simple replacement: a **list of :name = value pairs . When a method has overloads with different numbers of parameters, its plain name takes one parameter** and looks at what you pass: You pass What happens NULL (an empty string) the parameterless overload is called: oProcess.Start(NULL) a list of :name = value pairs the overload is chosen by the parameter names of the .NET method; the order does not matter and optional parameters can be left out anything else the overload with one parameter is called: oMath.Round(2."}, {"u": "concepts.html#properties-and-fields-are-attributes", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Properties and fields are attributes", "x": "Properties and fields are attributes # .NET properties are Ring attributes. Ring calls the generated getName() / setName(value) for you, so reading and writing is simple, also inside { } : oForm = new Form() { Text = \"Hello\" Width = 400 } ? oForm.Controls.Count # chained: Controls returns a ControlCollection oForm { Height = 300 Text += \"!\" } Assigning a read-only property raises an error. Properties whose names are Ring keywords ( From , To , Step , On ...) are not attributes: use getFrom() / setFrom(value) . Length = 5 Truncated = Hel Math.PI = 3.14 Path sep = \\ Count = 2 Capacity = 50 Caugh"}, {"u": "concepts.html#braces-instead-of-the-dot-operator", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Braces instead of the dot operator", "x": "Braces instead of the dot operator # Ring lets you write { } after an object, and everything inside is evaluated on that object . It is a simple way to configure a .NET object, and all the samples in this documentation use it: oInfo = new ProcessStartInfo() { FileName = \"cmd\" Arguments = \"/c ver\" RedirectStandardOutput = True UseShellExecute = False CreateNoWindow = True } This is exactly the same as writing oInfo.FileName = \"cmd\" line by line. Inside the braces you can assign properties, call methods ( setClickEvent(\"...\") , Controls.Add(oButton) ) and use += . Use it on any object: oForm { H"}, {"u": "concepts.html#enums-are-strings", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Enums are strings", "x": "Enums are strings # Enum values are strings in both directions : oForm { WindowState = \"Maximized\" FormBorderStyle = \"FixedDialog\" } oDate = new DateTime(2024,12,25) ? oDate.DayOfWeek # \"Wednesday\" oLabel.TextAlign = \"MiddleCenter\" Flag combinations use the .NET text form: \"Top, Left\" ."}, {"u": "concepts.html#value-types-and-dnnull", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Value types and dnNull()", "x": "Value types and dnNull() # Structs ( Point , Size , Color , DateTime ) are objects like any other. Their constructors take the numbers: new Point(10,20) , new DateTime(2024,12,25) : oButton { Location = new Point(10,20) Size = new Size(100,30) } oPictureBox.Image = dnNull() # the .NET null dnNull() is the .NET null . A .NET method that returns null gives an object whose isNull() is true."}, {"u": "concepts.html#out-and-ref-parameters", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "out and ref parameters", "x": "out and ref parameters # A method with out or ref parameters returns a list : [result, out values...] : aResult = oDict.TryGetValue(\"key\") # [1, value] or [0, \"\"] if aResult[1] ? aResult[2] ok aParsed = new Int32().TryParse(\"123\") # [1, 123]"}, {"u": "concepts.html#generics", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Generics", "x": "Generics # The most useful closed generic types have classes: StringList , NumberList , ObjectList ( List<T> ), StringDictionary , NumberDictionary , ObjectDictionary ( Dictionary<string,T> ), StringSet , StringQueue , StringStack . See Collections & LINQ . Any other closed generic type can be created without a class: oList = dnNewGeneric(\"System.Collections.Generic.List\",[\"System.Int32\"],[]) oList.callMethod(\"Add\",[5]) Generic methods work too (the bridge infers the type arguments from the values), which is what makes LINQ possible."}, {"u": "concepts.html#return-values", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Return values", "x": "Return values # .NET returns Ring gets string string numbers ( int , long , double , decimal ...) number bool 1 / 0 enum string byte[] string (binary safe) arrays and collections of simple values list (use toList() on a collection object) any other object a wrapped object (generated class, or a plain DotNetObject ) null an object whose isNull() is true"}, {"u": "concepts.html#errors", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Errors", "x": "Errors # A .NET exception becomes a Ring error and can be caught with try / catch : try new Int32().Parse(\"abc\") catch ? cCatchError done DotNet Error: System.FormatException: The input string 'abc' was not in a correct format. Set the environment variable RINGDOTNET_TRACE=1 to get the .NET stack trace in the message."}, {"u": "concepts.html#plain-objects", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Plain objects", "x": "Plain objects # When no class exists for a type, or when you want the generic style, every wrapped object is also a DotNetObject and has: Method What it does callMethod(name, [args]) call a method getProp(name) / setProp(name, value) read or write a property or field toString() ToString() of the object typeName() full .NET type name toList() copy an array or collection into a Ring list isNull() true for the .NET null release() release the handle at once (Ring does it for you when the object is dropped; the object can not be used after this)"}, {"u": "core-api.html", "t": "Core functions", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "core-api.html#runtime", "t": "Core functions", "h": "Runtime", "x": "Runtime # dnVersion() # Returns the version of the .NET runtime hosted in the process. load \"dotnet.ring\" ? dnVersion() # .NET 9.0.15 dnUseDesktop(), dnUseWeb(), dnUseAll(), dnUseData() # Select the runtime mode before the first .NET call. You normally do not call them: the using { } block calls the right one when it sees the namespaces (see Getting started )."}, {"u": "core-api.html#importing-classes", "t": "Core functions", "h": "Importing classes", "x": "Importing classes # Function What it does using { \"System\" \"System.Text\" } Loads the generated wrapper classes of each namespace (once), like C#'s using . A value that is not a text raises an error. dnImport(\"System.IO\") The function behind using , handy when the name is computed. Returns true. Raises an error when no classes were generated for the name. dnImportFrom(\"myclasses/\",\"MyCompany.Tools\") Loads classes generated for your own assembly with wrapassembly.ring . See Your own assemblies . dnRegisterClass(type, class, nInit) Used by the generated files: registers a Ring class for a .NET ty"}, {"u": "core-api.html#loading-assemblies", "t": "Core functions", "h": "Loading assemblies", "x": "Loading assemblies # Function What it does dnLoad(\"path/to/Library.dll\") Loads an assembly from a path and returns its name. Its dependencies are found next to it. dnLoadName(\"System.Xml\") Loads an assembly by name."}, {"u": "core-api.html#without-wrapper-classes", "t": "Core functions", "h": "Without wrapper classes", "x": "Without wrapper classes # Any type is usable without generated classes: # a type, for static members oConsole = dnType(\"System.Console\") oConsole.callMethod(\"WriteLine\",[\"Hello from .NET\"]) # a static call in one line ? dnStatic(\"System.Math\",\"Max\",[3,9]) # 9 # a new object oSB = dnNew(\"System.Text.StringBuilder\",[\"Hello\"]) oSB.callMethod(\"Append\",[\", Ring!\"]) ? oSB.toString() # Hello, Ring! ? oSB.getProp(\"Length\") # 12 oSB.setProp(\"Capacity\",64) Function Returns dnType(\"Namespace.Type\") a type object: use callMethod / getProp for static members dnNew(\"Namespace.Type\", [args]) a new object (th"}, {"u": "core-api.html#dotnetobject", "t": "Core functions", "h": "DotNetObject", "x": "DotNetObject # Every .NET object that Ring holds is a DotNetObject (the generated classes derive from it): oObject.callMethod(\"Method\",[arg1,arg2]) # call a method oObject.getProp(\"Name\") # read a property or field oObject.setProp(\"Name\",value) # write it oObject.toString() # ToString() oObject.typeName() # \"System.Text.StringBuilder\" oObject.toList() # an array or a collection as a Ring list oObject.isNull() # true for the .NET null oObject.release() # release the handle oObject.nHandle is the handle number."}, {"u": "core-api.html#dnrequest", "t": "Core functions", "h": "dnRequest", "x": "dnRequest # dnRequest(operation, params) sends one request to the bridge; everything above is built on it. The operations are type new call get set tostring typename tolist describe load loadname release version . You normally never need it."}, {"u": "core-api.html#callbacks", "t": "Core functions", "h": "Callbacks", "x": "Callbacks # dnCallback(code) passes Ring code where a delegate is expected. See Callbacks & events ."}, {"u": "core-api.html#test-helpers", "t": "Core functions", "h": "Test helpers", "x": "Test helpers # dnAssert(\"name\", actual, expected) # counts a pass or prints FAIL: name expected <..> got <..> dnTestSummary() # prints \"TESTS: n passed, m failed\" nDotNetPass and nDotNetFail hold the counters. See Testing ."}, {"u": "collections-linq.html", "t": "Collections & LINQ", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "collections-linq.html#typed-collections", "t": "Collections & LINQ", "h": "Typed collections", "x": "Typed collections # The common closed generic collections have ready-made classes ( using { \"System.Collections.Generic\" } ): Class .NET type StringList , NumberList , ObjectList List<string> , List<double> , List<object> StringDictionary , NumberDictionary , ObjectDictionary Dictionary<string, T> StringSet HashSet<string> StringQueue , StringStack Queue<string> , Stack<string> using { \"System.Collections.Generic\" } oList = new StringList() oList.Add(\"banana\") oList.Add(\"apple\") oList.Add(\"cherry\") oList.Sort(NULL) ? oList.Count for cItem in oList.toList() # toList() copies the collection to a"}, {"u": "collections-linq.html#other-collection-classes", "t": "Collections & LINQ", "h": "Other collection classes", "x": "Other collection classes # System.Collections , System.Collections.Specialized , System.Collections.Concurrent and System.Collections.ObjectModel are wrapped completely: ArrayList , Hashtable , SortedList , Stack , Queue , BitArray , NameValueCollection , ObservableCollection and so on. samples/UsingDotNet/introduction/test10_collections.ring load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Collections\" \"System.Collections.Specialized\" \"System.Collections.Generic\" } # Word frequency with Dictionary<string,double> (entries are wrapped KeyValuePair objects) oCount = new NumberDictionary() for cWord in split(\""}, {"u": "collections-linq.html#sorting-and-searching-with-ring-code", "t": "Collections & LINQ", "h": "Sorting and searching with Ring code", "x": "Sorting and searching with Ring code # Methods that take a delegate ( Predicate , Comparison , Action ) accept dnCallback(...) . The Ring code reads aDotNetArgs and sets xDotNetResult : oList.FindAll(dnCallback(\"xDotNetResult = left(aDotNetArgs[1],1) = 'a'\")) oList.Sort([ :comparison = dnCallback(\"xDotNetResult = len(aDotNetArgs[1]) - len(aDotNetArgs[2])\") ]) oList.ForEach_(dnCallback(\"? aDotNetArgs[1]\")) See Callbacks & events ."}, {"u": "collections-linq.html#linq", "t": "Collections & LINQ", "h": "LINQ", "x": "LINQ # using { \"System.Linq\" } loads Enumerable and a fluent wrapper, dnQuery(...) , that works on a Ring list or any .NET collection . The lambda is Ring code: it reads aDotNetArgs[1] and writes xDotNetResult . using { \"System.Linq\" } aSales = [120, 45, 300, 15, 210, 99, 81] aTop = dnQuery(aSales).Where(\"xDotNetResult = aDotNetArgs[1] >= 100\").OrderByDescending(\"xDotNetResult = aDotNetArgs[1]\").Take(3).ToList() ? dnQuery(aSales).Count() ? dnQuery(aSales).Sum(\"\") ? dnQuery(aSales).Average(\"\") ? dnQuery(aSales).Max(\"\") samples/UsingDotNet/introduction/test11_linq.ring load \"dotnet.ring\" using {"}, {"u": "callbacks-events.html", "t": "Callbacks & events", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "callbacks-events.html#callbacks-with-dncallback", "t": "Callbacks & events", "h": "Callbacks with dnCallback()", "x": "Callbacks with dnCallback() # Pass dnCallback(code) wherever a .NET delegate is expected: Action , Func , Predicate , Comparison , EventHandler , Task continuations... The bridge builds a delegate of the exact type . When .NET invokes it, the Ring code runs, as with events in RingQt. It can read aDotNetArgs (the list of the delegate arguments) and set xDotNetResult (converted to the delegate's return type). nTotal = 0 oList.ForEach_(dnCallback(\"nTotal += len(aDotNetArgs[1])\")) # ForEach is a Ring keyword: ForEach_ oList.Sort([ :comparison = dnCallback(\"xDotNetResult = len(aDotNetArgs[1]) - len"}, {"u": "callbacks-events.html#events", "t": "Callbacks & events", "h": "Events", "x": "Events # Every public .NET event gets two methods, as in RingQt: setXXXEvent(code) and clearXXXEvent() . The code runs when the event is raised and can read aDotNetArgs , which is [sender, eventArgs] (both .NET objects). Each event has one handler; setting it again replaces it. using { \"System.Collections.ObjectModel\" } oList = new ObservableStringList() { setCollectionChangedEvent(\"? 'changed: ' + aDotNetArgs[2].getProp('Action')\") Add(\"one\") } CollectionChanged event #1 CollectionChanged event #2 CollectionChanged event #3 Events received : 3 Items : 1 (second handler) Items : 3 action = Add"}, {"u": "callbacks-events.html#calling-ring-from-net-code-you-wrote", "t": "Callbacks & events", "h": "Calling Ring from .NET code you wrote", "x": "Calling Ring from .NET code you wrote # Your own assembly can call Ring as well: when a parameter is typed object , the bridge passes the callback unchanged and your code can call RingDotNet.Bridge.CallRing(callback, returnType, args) . This is how the Entity Framework hooks ( onSaving , onLog ) and value converters are built \u2014 see Entity Framework reference ."}, {"u": "foundation.html", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "foundation.html#what-is-wrapped", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "What is wrapped", "x": "What is wrapped # Every namespace below has generated classes, loaded on demand with using { \"Name\" } . Area Namespaces Core System , System.Globalization , System.Numerics , System.Reflection , System.Runtime.InteropServices , System.Runtime.Loader , System.ComponentModel , System.Resources Text System.Text , System.Text.RegularExpressions , System.Text.Json , System.Text.Json.Nodes , System.Text.Encodings.Web , System.Text.Unicode Files and streams System.IO , System.IO.Compression , System.IO.Pipes , System.IO.MemoryMappedFiles , System.Formats.Tar Collections System.Collections , .Generic "}, {"u": "foundation.html#dates-strings-conversions-math", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "Dates, strings, conversions, math", "x": "Dates, strings, conversions, math # DateTime , TimeSpan , Convert , Math , String helpers, Guid , Random , Environment : samples/UsingDotNet/introduction/test08_system.ring load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" } # Dates and times oNow = new DateTime(2024,12,25).AddHours(18.5) ? \"Christmas 2024 : \" + oNow.Format(\"dddd, dd MMMM yyyy HH:mm\") ? \"Plus 10 days : \" + oNow.AddDays(10).Format(\"yyyy-MM-dd\") ? \"Day of week : \" + oNow.DayOfWeek oSpan = new TimeSpan().FromMinutes(135) ? \"135 minutes : \" + oSpan.ToString() + \" (\" + oSpan.TotalHours + \" hours)\" # Strings, conversions, math oStr = new String()"}, {"u": "foundation.html#files-regular-expressions-and-compression", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "Files, regular expressions and compression", "x": "Files, regular expressions and compression # File , Directory , Path , StreamReader / StreamWriter , MemoryStream , Regex , GZipStream : samples/UsingDotNet/introduction/test09_io_regex.ring load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.IO.Compression\" \"System.Text.RegularExpressions\" } oPath = new Path() oFile = new File() cDir = oPath.Combine([oPath.GetTempPath(),\"ringdotnet_demo\"]) new Directory().CreateDirectory(cDir) # Text files cLog = oPath.Combine([cDir,\"log.txt\"]) oWriter = new StreamWriter(cLog) oWriter.WriteLine(\"2024-05-17 ERROR disk full\") oWriter.WriteLine(\"2024-05-18 I"}, {"u": "foundation.html#networking", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "Networking", "x": "Networking # Uri , IPAddress , Dns , HttpClient , WebUtility , Encoding \u2014 a program that does not need the internet to run: samples/UsingDotNet/introduction/test12_network.ring load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text\" \"System.Net\" \"System.Net.Http\" \"System.Threading.Tasks\" } # A tiny web server and client in one Ring program: .NET runs the requests on its thread pool, # Ring waits for the tasks and answers cPrefix = \"http://localhost:\" + (20000 + random(20000)) + \"/\" oServer = new HttpListener() oServer.Prefixes.Add(cPrefix) oServer.Start() ? \"Listening on \" + cPrefix oWaiting = oServ"}, {"u": "foundation.html#cryptography-and-globalization", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "Cryptography and globalization", "x": "Cryptography and globalization # Hashes ( SHA256 , MD5 ), HMAC, random bytes, AES, culture-aware formatting: samples/UsingDotNet/introduction/test13_security.ring load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Globalization\" \"System.Security.Cryptography\" \"System.Diagnostics\" } # Hashes and HMAC oConvert = new Convert() ? \"SHA256(abc) : \" + lower(oConvert.ToHexString(new SHA256().HashData(\"abc\"))) ? \"MD5(abc) : \" + lower(oConvert.ToHexString(new MD5().HashData(\"abc\"))) oHmac = dnNew(\"HMACSHA256\",[ :key = \"secret key\" ]) ? \"HMAC : \" + left(lower(oConvert.ToHexString(oHmac.ComputeHash(\"message\"))),"}, {"u": "foundation.html#xml-json-and-data-tables", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "XML, JSON and data tables", "x": "XML, JSON and data tables # XmlDocument , XPath , System.Text.Json , DataTable and DataView \u2014 no database needed: samples/UsingDotNet/introduction/test14_xml_json_data.ring load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Xml\" \"System.Text.Json\" \"System.Data\" } # XML with XPath oDoc = new XmlDocument() oDoc.LoadXml(\"<shop><item price='3.5'>apple</item><item price='1.2'>fig</item><item price='9'>melon</item></shop>\") oItems = oDoc.SelectNodes(\"//item[@price > 2]\") ? \"Items over 2 : \" + oItems.Count for nLoop = 0 to oItems.Count - 1 oItem = oItems.Item(nLoop) ? \" \" + oItem.InnerText + \" costs \" + oItem.GetAtt"}, {"u": "foundation.html#reflection-and-big-numbers", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "Reflection and big numbers", "x": "Reflection and big numbers # Type , Assembly , MethodInfo , BigInteger , Complex : samples/UsingDotNet/introduction/test15_reflection_numbers.ring load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Reflection\" \"System.Numerics\" \"System.Text.Encodings.Web\" \"System.Collections.Concurrent\" \"System.Runtime.InteropServices\" } # Big integers: factorial of 40 oBig = new BigInteger() nFactorial = oBig.Parse(\"1\") for nLoop = 2 to 40 nFactorial = oBig.Multiply(nFactorial,nLoop) next ? \"40! : \" + nFactorial.ToString() ? \"2^200 : \" + oBig.Pow(2,200).ToString() oZ = dnNew(\"Complex\",[ :real = 3, :imaginary = 4 ]) "}, {"u": "foundation.html#processes-and-threads", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "Processes and threads", "x": "Processes and threads # using { \"System.Diagnostics\" \"System.Threading.Tasks\" } oInfo = new ProcessStartInfo() { FileName = \"cmd\" Arguments = \"/c ver\" RedirectStandardOutput = True UseShellExecute = False CreateNoWindow = True } oProcess = new Process().Start([ :startInfo = oInfo ]) ? trim(oProcess.StandardOutput.ReadToEnd()) oProcess.WaitForExit(NULL) oTask = new Task().Delay(30) # a Task: wait for it on the Ring thread oTask.Wait(NULL) ? oTask.IsCompleted oResult = new Task().FromResult(\"done\") ? oResult.getProp(\"Result\") # the Result of a Task<T> Warning .NET threads can not run Ring code. "}, {"u": "foundation.html#a-bigger-example", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "A bigger example", "x": "A bigger example # samples/UsingDotNet/introduction/test16_forms_app.ring and demo_todo.ring combine collections, files, JSON and a form. For complete programs see Applications ."}, {"u": "winforms.html", "t": "Windows Forms", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "winforms.html#the-shape-of-a-program", "t": "Windows Forms", "h": "The shape of a program", "x": "The shape of a program # load \"dotnet.ring\" using { # the names start the Windows Desktop runtime and make the Ring thread an STA thread \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" # also loads the GUI helpers } oForm = \"\" # globals used by event code oCount = \"\" nClicks = 0 func main oForm = uiForm(\"Click counter\",260,130) oCount = uiLabel(oForm,\"0 clicks\",20,15,220,24) uiButton(oForm,\"Click me\",20,50,100,32,\"clicked()\") uiRun(oForm) func clicked nClicks++ oCount.Text = \"\" + nClicks + \" clicks\" A label and a button: the whole program is about 15 lines The using block first (it starts the desktop "}, {"u": "winforms.html#the-helpers", "t": "Windows Forms", "h": "The helpers", "x": "The helpers # Everything the helpers do can also be written with the plain classes (see the next section); they exist to keep small programs short. They are loaded together with System.Windows.Forms . Helper What it creates uiForm(title, w, h) a form with a client area of w \u00d7 h, centered uiLabel(parent, text, x, y, w, h) a label uiButton(parent, text, x, y, w, h, clickCode) a button; clickCode is Ring code or a function call uiTextBox(parent, x, y, w, h) a text box uiMemo(parent, x, y, w, h) a multi-line text box with scroll bars uiCheckBox(parent, text, x, y, w, h, clickCode) a check box uiLi"}, {"u": "winforms.html#every-control-is-available", "t": "Windows Forms", "h": "Every control is available", "x": "Every control is available # The helpers are optional. The whole of System.Windows.Forms (414 classes), System.Drawing and its sub-namespaces are wrapped, so you can use any control directly: oTree = new TreeView() oTree.Dock = \"Fill\" oRoot = oTree.Nodes.Add(\"Fruit\") oRoot { Nodes.Add(\"Apple\") Nodes.Add(\"Pear\") } oTree.ExpandAll() oForm.Controls.Add(oTree) Group Controls Text and input Label , TextBox , RichTextBox , MaskedTextBox , NumericUpDown , DateTimePicker , MonthCalendar Buttons and choices Button , CheckBox , RadioButton , ComboBox , ListBox , CheckedListBox , TrackBar , ProgressBar L"}, {"u": "winforms.html#events", "t": "Windows Forms", "h": "Events", "x": "Events # oButton.setClickEvent(\"clicked()\") oText.setTextChangedEvent(\"textChanged()\") oForm.setFormClosingEvent(\"closing()\") oPicture.setMouseDownEvent(\"? aDotNetArgs[2].X\") The code of an event runs on the Ring thread when the message loop dispatches it. Details and warnings: Callbacks & events ."}, {"u": "winforms.html#keyboard-in-games", "t": "Windows Forms", "h": "Keyboard in games", "x": "Keyboard in games # A button (or any control that takes the focus) steals the arrow keys, Space and Enter from the form, so the form's KeyDown never sees them. uiGameKeys fixes that: oForm.setKeyDownEvent(\"keyPressed(aDotNetArgs[2].KeyCode)\") uiGameKeys(oForm,\"keyPressed(aDotNetArgs[2].KeyCode)\") # after the controls were added Every control then reports the arrows as normal keys and the form handles them. Tetris, 2048, Sokoban, Snake and Pong use it. samples/UsingDotNet/applications/Tetris \u2014 keys are handled by the form"}, {"u": "winforms.html#self-testing-programs", "t": "Windows Forms", "h": "Self-testing programs", "x": "Self-testing programs # Every sample and application can drive itself and check its results \u2014 the same program, no separate test harness: uiTest(oForm,\"selfTest()\") # when started with --selftest: run selfTest() once the form is shown uiRun(oForm) uiEnd() # prints SELFTEST OK, or the failures func selfTest oForm.Controls.Item(1).PerformClick() dnAssert(\"one click\",oCount.Text,\"1 clicks\") uiSendKeys(oForm,\"12{+}30{ENTER}\") types into the focused control through the real key processing by posting window messages to your own form \u2014 it can never type into another program. See Testing ."}, {"u": "winforms.html#more", "t": "Windows Forms", "h": "More", "x": "More # Forms samples \u2014 32 short samples. Applications \u2014 43 complete programs. WPF \u2014 the other desktop UI framework."}, {"u": "gallery-forms.html", "t": "Windows Forms samples", "h": "", "x": "Run one with ring 06_listbox.ring from samples/UsingDotNet/Forms ; with --selftest it checks itself and closes. Click Source code on a card to read the program, or click the picture to enlarge it. Hello form The smallest window: a form with a label ring samples/UsingDotNet/Forms/01_hello_form.ring Source code # The smallest window: a form with a label load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } oForm = uiForm(\"Hello\",300,120) oLabel = uiLabel(oForm,\"Hello from Ring and .NET!\",20,40,260,30) oLabel.Font = uiFont(\"Segoe UI\",14,true) uiTest(oForm,\"dnAssert('label text',oLa"}, {"u": "gallery-apps.html", "t": "Applications", "h": "", "x": "Run one: cd samples/UsingDotNet/applications/Calculator then ring main.ring . With ring main.ring --selftest the application drives its own form, checks the results with dnAssert() and prints SELFTEST OK . Ports of RingQt applications: Calculator, Stopwatch, TicTacToe, FifteenPuzzle, LightsOut, Game2048, MemoryGame, Snake, TowersOfHanoi, AnalogClock, RockPaperScissors, BMICalculator, GameOfLife, Minesweeper, EpochTime, AsciiTable, Calendar, EightQueens, Sudoku, Pong, Tetris, Sokoban, TypingQuiz, JulianDay. The others are new: Notepad, Paint, TodoList, ColorMixer, NumberGuess, Hangman, DiceRoll"}, {"u": "wpf.html", "t": "WPF", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "wpf.html#a-first-wpf-window", "t": "WPF", "h": "A first WPF window", "x": "A first WPF window # load \"dotnet.ring\" using { # System.Windows* starts the desktop runtime \"System.Windows\" \"System.Windows.Controls\" } oWindow = wpfWindow(\"Hello\",300,200) oPanel = new StackPanel() oWindow.Content = oPanel oPanel.Children.Add(wpfLabel(\"Hello from WPF\")) wpfRun(oWindow) The using block starts the desktop runtime (it calls dnUseDesktop() when it sees a System.Windows* namespace). Import the WPF namespaces you need: System.Windows , .Controls , .Controls.Primitives , .Media , .Shapes , .Data , .Input , .Documents , .Markup , .Threading , .Media.Imaging , .Media.Animation ..."}, {"u": "wpf.html#names-that-start-with-wpf", "t": "WPF", "h": "Names that start with Wpf", "x": "Names that start with Wpf # WPF has classes with the same names as Windows Forms and System.Drawing classes. To keep both usable in one program, the WPF version of a clashing name starts with Wpf : Windows Forms / System.Drawing WPF Button , TextBox , Label WpfButton , WpfTextBox , WpfLabel Point , Color , Brush WpfPoint , WpfColor , WpfBrush Binding , Rectangle WpfBinding , WpfRectangle The others keep their name: Window , Grid , StackPanel , Canvas , Slider , Ellipse , Thickness ... Warning A global variable named like an attribute ( x , width ) breaks obj.X in Ring. Keep program state in fu"}, {"u": "wpf.html#the-helpers", "t": "WPF", "h": "The helpers", "x": "The helpers # Helper Does wpfWindow(title, w, h) a window of the given size, centered wpfLabel(text) , wpfText(text) a Label / TextBlock wpfButton(text, clickCode) a button with a click handler wpfBrush(\"SteelBlue\") , wpfColor(name) a brush or color from a name or #RRGGBB wpfXaml(text) the objects of a XAML text wpfFind(root, name) an element by its x:Name wpfRun(window) shows the window and runs the message loop wpfTest(window, code) , wpfEnd() self test ( --selftest ) wpfPixel(element, w, h, x, y) the color of a pixel of a rendered element (tests)"}, {"u": "wpf.html#xaml", "t": "WPF", "h": "XAML", "x": "XAML # Describe the window in XAML and find the elements by name: cXaml = '<Window xmlns=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation\" xmlns:x=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml\" Title=\"From XAML\" Width=\"320\" Height=\"180\">' cXaml += '<StackPanel Margin=\"15\"><TextBlock x:Name=\"message\" FontSize=\"18\" Text=\"Press the button\"/>' cXaml += '<Button x:Name=\"press\" Content=\"Press me\" Margin=\"0,10,0,0\"/></StackPanel></Window>' oWindow = wpfXaml(cXaml) oMessage = wpfFind(oWindow,\"message\") oPress = wpfFind(oWindow,\"press\") oPress.setClickEvent(\"oMessage.Text = 'The XAML button w"}, {"u": "wpf.html#data-binding", "t": "WPF", "h": "Data binding", "x": "Data binding # Bind a property of one element to a property of another \u2014 without code: oSlider = new Slider() oSlider.Maximum = 100 oText = new WpfTextBox() oBinding = dnNew(\"WpfBinding\",[ :path = \"Value\" ]) oBinding.Source = oSlider oText.SetBinding(oText.TextProperty,oBinding) samples/UsingDotNet/Wpf/03_binding_slider.ring \u2014 the text follows the slider"}, {"u": "wpf.html#shapes-and-brushes", "t": "WPF", "h": "Shapes and brushes", "x": "Shapes and brushes # oCanvas = new Canvas() oRect = new WpfRectangle() { Width = 80 Height = 50 Fill = wpfBrush(\"SteelBlue\") } oCanvas.Children.Add(oRect) oCanvas.SetLeft(oRect,10) oCanvas.SetTop(oRect,10) samples/UsingDotNet/Wpf/07_shapes_pixels.ring"}, {"u": "wpf.html#layout", "t": "WPF", "h": "Layout", "x": "Layout # Grid , StackPanel , DockPanel , WrapPanel , Canvas , UniformGrid and the Thickness margin work as in XAML: samples/UsingDotNet/Wpf/08_grid_layout.ring"}, {"u": "wpf.html#events", "t": "WPF", "h": "Events", "x": "Events # Events work like Windows Forms events: setClickEvent , setValueChangedEvent , setTextChangedEvent ... Routed events can be raised from tests with RaiseEvent ."}, {"u": "wpf.html#applications", "t": "WPF", "h": "Applications", "x": "Applications # samples/UsingDotNet/applications/WpfCalculator \u2014 a calculator whose UI is XAML. samples/UsingDotNet/applications/WpfColorMixer \u2014 sliders that mix a color. samples/UsingDotNet/applications/WpfCalculator More: WPF samples \u2014 all 10 with screenshots and source."}, {"u": "gallery-wpf.html", "t": "WPF samples", "h": "", "x": "Run one with ring 06_xaml_window.ring from samples/UsingDotNet/Wpf ; with --selftest it checks itself and closes. See the WPF guide for the concepts. Hello window The smallest WPF window ring samples/UsingDotNet/Wpf/01_hello_window.ring Source code # The smallest WPF window load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Windows\" \"System.Windows.Controls\" \"System.Windows.Controls.Primitives\" \"System.Windows.Media\" \"System.Windows.Shapes\" \"System.Windows.Data\" \"System.Windows.Threading\" \"System\" } oWindow = wpfWindow(\"Hello WPF\",320,160) oPanel = new StackPanel() oWindow.Content = oPanel oText = new TextBlo"}, {"u": "aspnet.html", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "aspnet.html#a-web-server-in-a-few-lines", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "A web server in a few lines", "x": "A web server in a few lines # load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"RingDotNet.Web\" } # starts the ASP.NET Core mode and loads the web server oServer = new WebServer(\"http://localhost:5080\") oServer.onGet(\"/\",\"home\") oServer.onPost(\"/api/echo\",\"echo\") oServer.run() # serves until stop() func home oRequest return \"<h1>Hello</h1>\" + oRequest.path() func echo oRequest return [201,\"text/plain\",\"you sent \" + oRequest.body()] using { \"RingDotNet.Web\" } starts the ASP.NET Core runtime and loads the web server of RingDotNet (it calls dnUseWeb() for you). A handler is a Ring function that receives the request an"}, {"u": "aspnet.html#handlers-and-responses", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "Handlers and responses", "x": "Handlers and responses # A handler returns one of: Return value Response a text status 200, text/html [status, contentType, text] exactly that webJson(value) status 200, application/json webJson turns a Ring list into a JSON array and a list of [name, value] pairs into a JSON object (nested values too): func listTodos oRequest aList = [] for aTodo in aTodos aList + [[\"id\",aTodo[1]],[\"text\",aTodo[2]]] next return webJson(aList) GET /api/todos"}, {"u": "aspnet.html#the-request", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "The request", "x": "The request # Method Returns oRequest.method() \"GET\" , \"POST\" ... oRequest.path() /api/todos oRequest.queryString() ?id=1 oRequest.query(\"id\") one query value oRequest.body() the body as text oRequest.header(\"Accept\") a request header oRequest.form(\"name\") a form field of a POST oRequest.setHeader(name, value) add a response header oRequest.context() the ASP.NET HttpContext , for everything else"}, {"u": "aspnet.html#the-webserver", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "The WebServer", "x": "The WebServer # Method Does new WebServer(url) starts Kestrel; port 0 picks a free port onGet(path, handler) , onPost , onPut , onDelete routes ( route(method, path, handler) for any method) notFound(handler) the handler for unknown paths (default: Not found: GET /x ) url() the real address (when the port was 0) run() serve until stop() serveOne(milliseconds) handle one waiting request; true if one was handled stop() , close() stop serving, stop the server An error raised by a handler becomes a 500 response with the message. nRequests counts the requests served."}, {"u": "aspnet.html#one-ring-thread", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "One Ring thread", "x": "One Ring thread # Kestrel runs on .NET threads and Ring code may only run on the Ring thread. So the requests are queued and serveOne() / run() take them off the queue on the Ring thread. This is the reason you can use any Ring code and global state in a handler without locks \u2014 and also why handlers do not run in parallel. You can mix serving with your own loop (for example next to a GUI: name \"System.Windows.Forms\" and \"RingDotNet.Web\" in the same using block, or call dnUseAll() ): while true oServer.serveOne(100) # handle a request if one arrives within 100 ms doOtherWork() end"}, {"u": "aspnet.html#a-complete-application", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "A complete application", "x": "A complete application # samples/UsingDotNet/applications/WebApi is a to-do list with a web page and a JSON API. It tests itself by calling its own server with HttpClient : samples/UsingDotNet/applications/WebApi/main.ring # A small web application written in Ring on ASP.NET Core: a to-do list with a web page and a JSON API # ring main.ring then open http://localhost:5080/ # GET /api/todos POST /api/todos (the text is the body) DELETE /api/todos?id=1 load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Net.Http\" \"System.Threading.Tasks\" \"RingDotNet.Web\" } aTodos = [] # [id, text] nNextId = 1 oServer = "}, {"u": "aspnet.html#all-of-aspnet-core", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "All of ASP.NET Core", "x": "All of ASP.NET Core # The ASP.NET Core classes are wrapped too: Microsoft.AspNetCore.* (Http, Routing, Authentication, Authorization, Mvc, Builder, Hosting, StaticFiles...), Microsoft.Extensions.* (DependencyInjection, Configuration, Logging, Hosting, Caching...) and Microsoft.Net.Http.Headers . Class names that clash with other namespaces start with Asp . using { \"Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http\" } oPath = dnNew(\"PathString\",[ :value = \"/api/items\" ]) ? oPath.Value # /api/items ? oPath.StartsWithSegments(dnNew(\"PathString\",[ :value = \"/api\" ])) oQuery = new QueryString().Create([ :name = \"name\", :v"}, {"u": "aspnet.html#what-is-not-supported", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "What is not supported", "x": "What is not supported # MVC controllers and Razor pages written in C# \u2014 the web server is the Ring one. Handlers that run in parallel (there is one Ring thread). Calling Ring from middleware you wrote in .NET."}, {"u": "ef.html", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "ef.html#the-idea", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "The idea", "x": "The idea # Ring cannot declare .NET classes, so a Ring program describes the model with data : a list of tables, each with a list of columns. EF Core works on that model in two ways: Class Rows are Best for RingDatabase lists of [name, value] pairs scripts, tools, small apps \u2014 the simplest API RingEntityDb real .NET objects (classes generated from the schema) with navigation properties change tracking, Include , an object-oriented style Both support SQLite (bundled), an in-memory database, and SQL Server, PostgreSQL and MySQL when their EF Core package is available. load \"dotnet.ring\" using { "}, {"u": "ef.html#a-table-and-its-rows", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "A table and its rows", "x": "A table and its rows # The schema is a list of tables: [tableName, [ [column, type, isKey, options], ... ]] . Column types: int long double decimal string bool datetime bytes . An int or long key is generated by the database. docs/examples/ef_basic.ring # Entity Framework Core: a table, rows, SQL conditions load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"RingDotNet.Data\" } func main oDb = new RingDatabase(\"Data Source=:memory:\",[ [\"Customers\",[ [\"Id\",\"int\",1], [\"Name\",\"string\",0,[[\"required\",1],[\"maxlength\",40]]], [\"Age\",\"int\",0,[[\"nullable\",1]]], [\"City\",\"string\",0,[[\"index\",1]]] ]] ]) nAnn = oDb.insert(\"Custome"}, {"u": "ef.html#column-options", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "Column options", "x": "Column options # The optional fourth item of a column is a list of [name, value] options: Option Meaning [\"required\",1] NOT NULL for text columns [\"nullable\",1] the column accepts null (a number can then be empty) [\"unique\",1] a unique index \u2014 a duplicate raises an error [\"index\",1] a plain index [\"maxlength\",40] maximum length of a text [\"default\",5] default value (numbers or text) [\"references\",\"Authors\"] foreign key to the key of another table [\"ondelete\",\"cascade\"] cascade , restrict , setnull , noaction [\"convert\",\"upper\"] a value converter (see hooks ) Several columns with isKey = 1 make"}, {"u": "ef.html#relationships", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "Relationships", "x": "Relationships # A column with [\"references\",\"Authors\"] becomes a foreign key. children , parent and include read the related rows: docs/examples/ef_relations.ring # Entity Framework Core: foreign keys, children, parent, include and delete behaviors load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"RingDotNet.Data\" } func main oDb = new RingDatabase(\"Data Source=:memory:\",[ [\"Authors\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Name\",\"string\",0]]], [\"Books\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Title\",\"string\",0],[\"AuthorId\",\"int\",0,[[\"references\",\"Authors\"],[\"ondelete\",\"cascade\"]]]]] ]) nTolkien = oDb.insert(\"Authors\",[[\"Name\",\"Tolkien\"]]) nLewis = oDb.insert("}, {"u": "ef.html#queries-as-data", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "Queries as data", "x": "Queries as data # For anything beyond a SQL condition, describe the query as data. EF Core builds a real LINQ expression tree from it and translates it to SQL, so it works with every provider: docs/examples/ef_queries.ring # Entity Framework Core: queries as LINQ expression trees, aggregates and SQL of any shape load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"RingDotNet.Data\" } func main oDb = new RingDatabase(\"Data Source=:memory:\",[ [\"People\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Name\",\"string\",0],[\"Age\",\"int\",0],[\"City\",\"string\",0],[\"Score\",\"double\",0]]] ]) oDb.insert(\"People\",[[\"Name\",\"Ann\"],[\"Age\",31],[\"City\",\"Cairo\"],[\"Score\","}, {"u": "ef.html#transactions-and-manual-saving", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "Transactions and manual saving", "x": "Transactions and manual saving # oDb.begin() oDb.update(\"Accounts\",nA,[[\"Balance\",70]]) oDb.update(\"Accounts\",nB,[[\"Balance\",80]]) oDb.commit() # or oDb.rollback() oDb.autoSave(false) # changes are written together by save() oDb.insert(\"Accounts\",[[\"Owner\",\"D\"],[\"Balance\",5]]) oDb.insert(\"Accounts\",[[\"Owner\",\"E\"],[\"Balance\",6]]) oDb.save() oDb.autoSave(true) The async versions return a .NET Task ; dbWait waits for it and returns the result: oTask = oDb.insertAsync(\"Accounts\",[[\"Owner\",\"F\"],[\"Balance\",7]]) ? dbWait(oTask) # the key ? len(dbWait(oDb.allAsync(\"Accounts\")))"}, {"u": "ef.html#migrations", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "Migrations", "x": "Migrations # migrate() brings an existing database in line with the model: it creates the missing tables, columns and indexes and returns the list of changes. apply(name, sql) runs a named SQL change once and records it in the table RingMigrations . docs/examples/ef_migrate.ring # Entity Framework Core: migrations of an existing database file load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.IO\" \"RingDotNet.Data\" } func main cFile = new Path().Combine([new Path().GetTempPath(),\"ringdotnet_docs_migrate.db\"]) if new File().Exists(cFile) new File().Delete(cFile) ok cConnection = \"Data Source=\" + cFile # version"}, {"u": "ef.html#hooks-filters-and-converters", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "Hooks, filters and converters", "x": "Hooks, filters and converters # Ring functions can run when changes are saved or SQL is executed; a table can have a global query filter (soft delete); a column can have a value converter : docs/examples/ef_hooks.ring # Entity Framework Core: hooks, query filters, converters, transactions load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"RingDotNet.Data\" } aSql = [] func main oDb = new RingDatabase(\"Data Source=:memory:\",[ [\"Notes\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Text\",\"string\",0,[[\"convert\",\"upper\"]]],[\"Deleted\",\"int\",0]],[[\"filter\",[\"Deleted\",\"=\",0]]]] ]) oDb.onSaving(dnCallback(\"beforeSave\")) oDb.onLog(dnCallback(\"logSql\")) o"}, {"u": "ef.html#real-entity-classes", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "Real entity classes", "x": "Real entity classes # RingEntityDb uses the same schema, but each table becomes a real .NET class with one property per column, a navigation property to the parent for each foreign key ( AuthorId \u2192 Author ) and a collection on the parent ( Books ). EF tracks the objects like it tracks classes written in C#: docs/examples/ef_entities.ring # Entity Framework Core with real entity classes: navigation properties, tracking, include load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"RingDotNet.Data\" } func main oDb = new RingEntityDb(\"Data Source=:memory:\",[ [\"Authors\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Name\",\"string\",0]]], [\"Books\",[[\"Id"}, {"u": "ef.html#providers", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "Providers", "x": "Providers # The connection text selects the provider: Connection Provider \"Data Source=people.db\" SQLite (bundled). \"Data Source=:memory:\" for an in-memory SQLite database \"inmemory:name\" the EF in-memory provider (no SQL, no migrations; same name = same database) \"sqlserver:Server=.;Database=x;...\" SQL Server \u2014 needs Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer.dll next to the other assemblies \"postgres:Host=...;...\" PostgreSQL (Npgsql provider) \"mysql:Server=...;...\" MySQL (Pomelo provider) Warning The SQL Server, PostgreSQL and MySQL providers are loaded by name from their NuGet packages and hav"}, {"u": "ef.html#the-full-ef-api", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "The full EF API", "x": "The full EF API # Everything in EF Core is also wrapped as classes ( using { \"Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore\" } , .ChangeTracking , .Infrastructure , .Metadata , .Metadata.Builders , .Storage , .Query , .Migrations , .Diagnostics , Microsoft.Data.Sqlite ; clashing names start with Ef ). oDb.context() returns the real DbContext : oContext = oDb.context() ? oContext.Database.ProviderName # Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite The complete list of methods is in the Entity Framework reference . A complete program: samples/UsingDotNet/applications/Contacts \u2014 a contacts book with a console menu."}, {"u": "ef-reference.html", "t": "Entity Framework reference", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "ef-reference.html#schema-format", "t": "Entity Framework reference", "h": "Schema format", "x": "Schema format # aSchema = [ [ \"TableName\", [ [ \"Column\", \"type\", isKey, [ [\"option\", value], ... ] ], ... ], [ [\"tableOption\", value], ... ] # optional ], ... ] Part Values column type int , long , double , decimal , string (or text ), bool , datetime , bytes isKey 1 for a key column; several give a composite key. A single int / long key is generated by the database column options required , nullable , unique , index , maxlength , default , references , ondelete , convert table options [\"filter\", condition] \u2014 a global query filter"}, {"u": "ef-reference.html#creating-a-database", "t": "Entity Framework reference", "h": "Creating a database", "x": "Creating a database # oDb = new RingDatabase(connection, aSchema) # rows as [name, value] pairs oDb = new RingEntityDb(connection, aSchema) # real entity objects The connection starts with the provider name ( inmemory: , sqlserver: , postgres: , mysql: ) or is a SQLite connection string."}, {"u": "ef-reference.html#ringdatabase", "t": "Entity Framework reference", "h": "RingDatabase", "x": "RingDatabase # Rows # Method Returns insert(table, values) the key (a list for a composite key). values is [[\"Name\",\"Ann\"],[\"Age\",31]] byKey(table, key) the row, or \"\" . The row is tracked : after raw SQL, call discard() to read the new values all(table) all rows where(table, condition, params) rows matching a SQL condition with {0} parameters (SQLite) update(table, key, values) 1 when the row exists, else 0 remove(table, key) 1 when the row existed, else 0 count(table) the number of rows dbValue(row, column) a column of a row ( \"\" for null) Queries # Method Does query(table, spec) rows matchi"}, {"u": "ef-reference.html#ringentitydb", "t": "Entity Framework reference", "h": "RingEntityDb", "x": "RingEntityDb # Method Does newEntity(table) a new object of the generated class entityType(table) the .NET Type properties(table) the property names: columns first, then navigations ( Author , Books ) add(entity) adds the entity (and the entities it refers to); returns the key remove(entity) removes it find(table, key) the tracked entity, or \"\" query(table, spec) tracked entities; the specification also accepts [\"include\",[\"Author\",\"Other.Nested\"]] all(table) , count(table, cond) all entities, number of entities getValue(entity, property) , setValue(entity, property, value) read or write a pro"}, {"u": "ef-reference.html#query-specification", "t": "Entity Framework reference", "h": "Query specification", "x": "Query specification # A query is a list of [item, value] pairs: Item Value where a condition orderby [[column, \"asc\"/\"desc\"], ...] skip , take numbers select [column, ...] \u2014 keeps only those columns (RingDatabase) include [navigation, ...] (RingEntityDb) ignorefilters 1 to ignore the table's global filter A condition is [column, op, value] or a combination: Form Meaning [\"Age\", \">\", 30] comparison: = != < <= > >= [\"Name\", \"contains\", \"an\"] contains , startswith , endswith (text) [\"City\", \"in\", [\"Cairo\",\"Giza\"]] membership [\"Boss\", \"isnull\"] , [\"Boss\", \"notnull\"] null checks [\"and\", c1, c2, ..."}, {"u": "ef-reference.html#providers-and-capabilities", "t": "Entity Framework reference", "h": "Providers and capabilities", "x": "Providers and capabilities # Capability SQLite In-memory SQL Server / PostgreSQL / MySQL tables, rows, relations, queries (expression trees), aggregates \u2714 \u2714 untested where / execute / scalar / sqlRows with SQL \u2714 \u2014 untested migrate / apply \u2714 \u2014 \u2014 transactions \u2714 \u2714 untested typed entities ( RingEntityDb ) \u2714 \u2714 untested"}, {"u": "ef-reference.html#what-is-not-supported", "t": "Entity Framework reference", "h": "What is not supported", "x": "What is not supported # Classes written in C# source \u2014 entity classes are generated from the schema. Lambdas written by hand \u2014 queries are data. Migrations that alter or drop columns (use execute or apply ). Hooks, converters and migrate() in RingEntityDb . The SQL Server, PostgreSQL and MySQL providers have not been tested."}, {"u": "own-assemblies.html", "t": "Your own assemblies", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "own-assemblies.html#two-ways-to-use-an-assembly", "t": "Your own assemblies", "h": "Two ways to use an assembly", "x": "Two ways to use an assembly # Way When Generic calls \u2014 dnLoad , dnType , dnNew , callMethod a quick call, nothing to generate Generated classes \u2014 wrapassembly.ring the simple oObj.Method() syntax, properties, events, enums, inheritance"}, {"u": "own-assemblies.html#generic-calls", "t": "Your own assemblies", "h": "Generic calls", "x": "Generic calls # load \"dotnet.ring\" dnLoad(\"path/to/MyLibrary.dll\") # dependencies are found next to the dll oCalc = dnNew(\"MyCompany.Tools.Calculator\",[10]) oCalc.callMethod(\"Add\",[5]) ? oCalc.getProp(\"Total\") # 15 ? dnStatic(\"MyCompany.Tools.Calculator\",\"Square\",[7]) # 49"}, {"u": "own-assemblies.html#generated-classes", "t": "Your own assemblies", "h": "Generated classes", "x": "Generated classes # Generate Ring classes for every public type of the DLL: cd tools ring wrapassembly.ring path/to/MyLibrary.dll ../myclasses This writes one loader per namespace and one file per class into myclasses/ . Use them like any other wrapped namespace: load \"dotnet.ring\" dnLoad(\"path/to/MyLibrary.dll\") dnImportFrom(\"myclasses/\",\"MyCompany.Tools\") oCalc = new Calculator() oCalc.Add(10).Multiply(3) # fluent: methods that return a wrapped class return that class ? oCalc.Total # 30 oCalc.Name = \"mine\" # a property ? new Calculator().Square(7) # a static method ? oCalc.Describe([ :level "}, {"u": "own-assemblies.html#nuget-packages", "t": "Your own assemblies", "h": "NuGet packages", "x": "NuGet packages # A NuGet package is a set of DLL files. Copy the DLLs of the package (for example from ~/.nuget/packages/<name>/<version>/lib/net8.0/ ) and the DLLs it depends on next to each other, then wrap and load as above: ring wrapassembly.ring Newtonsoft.Json.dll ../json dnLoad(\"Newtonsoft.Json.dll\") dnImportFrom(\"json/\",\"Newtonsoft.Json\") Entity Framework Core is wrapped the same way \u2014 that is how its classes were generated."}, {"u": "own-assemblies.html#which-namespaces-to-wrap", "t": "Your own assemblies", "h": "Which namespaces to wrap", "x": "Which namespaces to wrap # wrapassembly.ring <dll> <folder> [namespace ...] wraps only the namespaces you list. Without names, every namespace of the assembly is wrapped."}, {"u": "own-assemblies.html#rules", "t": "Your own assemblies", "h": "Rules", "x": "Rules # Generated loaders use the names of the .NET classes. When a class name is also a name Ring or another namespace already uses, the generator can add a prefix \u2014 see Class generator . Keep the DLL and its dependencies in one folder: assemblies are resolved next to the DLL you load. The runtime mode matters: a DLL that needs Windows Forms requires the desktop mode first (a using block that names System.Windows.Forms , or dnUseDesktop() )."}, {"u": "generator.html", "t": "Class generator", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "generator.html#what-the-generator-does", "t": "Class generator", "h": "What the generator does", "x": "What the generator does # tools/gendotnet.ring reads the manifest ( tools/dotnetmanifest.ring ), loads the assemblies, and uses reflection to write Ring source: libraries/dotnetlib/generated/ System.ring # the namespace loader: imports, registrations System.classes.ring # all the classes of the namespace (one file: loading and installing thousands of small files is slow) System.IO.ring System.IO.classes.ring ... classindex.ring # [type, namespace] of every class: automatic imports The class files are plain Ring: a Class X from Base with a method per .NET method, getX / setX for properties, set"}, {"u": "generator.html#regenerate", "t": "Class generator", "h": "Regenerate", "x": "Regenerate # cd tools ring gendotnet.ring # every namespace of the manifest ring gendotnet.ring System.Text System.IO # only these"}, {"u": "generator.html#the-manifest", "t": "Class generator", "h": "The manifest", "x": "The manifest # Each entry describes one namespace: Key Meaning :namespace the .NET namespace, also the name used with using { \"...\" } :assemblies assemblies that contain the types (default System.Private.CoreLib ) :all true wraps every public class and struct of the namespace :exclude type names that :all must skip :types explicit or extra types: [.NET name, Ring class name, init() parameter count] \u2014 closed generic types and nested types go here :prefer [\"Namespace.Type.Method=arity\"] \u2014 the overload that gets the plain name :uses namespaces imported together with this one (their classes are re"}, {"u": "generator.html#closed-generic-types", "t": "Class generator", "h": "Closed generic types", "x": "Closed generic types # The generic collections have classes through :types . Add your own with a line like: [\"System.Collections.Generic.List`1[[System.Int32]]\",\"IntList\",0] The third item forces the number of parameters of init() (0 here). After regenerating, new IntList() is a List<int> ."}, {"u": "generator.html#name-clashes", "t": "Class generator", "h": "Name clashes", "x": "Name clashes # When two namespaces contain a class with the same short name, the later one gets a prefix: System.Timers.Timer becomes TimersTimer , WPF's Button becomes WpfButton , ASP.NET's MediaTypeHeaderValue becomes AspMediaTypeHeaderValue , EF's clashing names start with Ef ."}, {"u": "generator.html#overload-rules", "t": "Class generator", "h": "Overload rules", "x": "Overload rules # The generator names overloads so that the plain name is the most useful one: the parameterless form wins when the type has one ( Cancel() , Wait() , Reverse() ), except Write* methods; otherwise the shortest overload is the plain name; overloads whose parameters are arrays (except byte[] / char[] ), delegates, comparers or spans never decide the plain name; a name with overloads of several parameter counts is one method that takes one parameter ( NULL , a value, or a list of :name = value ); the constructors other than init are New_ ; ToString() keeps its parameterless form; a"}, {"u": "generator.html#how-a-wrapper-class-looks", "t": "Class generator", "h": "How a wrapper class looks", "x": "How a wrapper class looks # Class StringBuilder from Object Func Append P1 return dnRequest(\"call\",[self,\"Append\",P1]) Func getLength return dnRequest(\"get\",[self,\"Length\"]) Func setLength P1 dnRequest(\"set\",[self,\"Length\",P1]) Ring calls getLength() when you read oSB.Length , and setLength(value) when you assign to it."}, {"u": "generator.html#wrapping-your-own-assembly", "t": "Class generator", "h": "Wrapping your own assembly", "x": "Wrapping your own assembly # tools/wrapassembly.ring uses the same generator for a single DLL: Your own assemblies ."}, {"u": "testing.html", "t": "Testing", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "testing.html#the-test-suite", "t": "Testing", "h": "The test suite", "x": "The test suite # sh samples/UsingDotNet/tests/run.sh B:/ring/bin/ring.exe samples/UsingDotNet/tests/run.sh runs: every samples/UsingDotNet/tests/test_*.ring (language bridge, collections, LINQ, system, IO, net, services, web, data, entity framework, keyboard, WPF...), the console samples, every Windows Forms and WPF sample with --selftest , every application with --selftest , and prints 0 failed when everything passes. It needs the package bin folder on the PATH; the script adds it for you. Programs run four at a time, and the windows of the programs that run themselves are invisible and not i"}, {"u": "testing.html#dnassert", "t": "Testing", "h": "dnAssert", "x": "dnAssert # load \"dotnet.ring\" dnAssert(\"max\",new Math().Max(3,9),9) dnAssert(\"name\",oForm.Text,\"Hello\") dnTestSummary() TESTS: 2 passed, 0 failed A failing assertion prints FAIL: name expected <9> got <3> and counts a failure. dnTestSummary() returns the number of failures."}, {"u": "testing.html#programs-that-test-themselves", "t": "Testing", "h": "Programs that test themselves", "x": "Programs that test themselves # Add three lines to a Windows Forms program and it becomes its own test: with --selftest it shows the form, runs the test code, closes the form and prints the result. oForm = uiForm(\"Click counter\",260,130) ... uiTest(oForm,\"selfTest()\") # runs selfTest() once the form is shown, when --selftest is used uiRun(oForm) # shows the form; the form closes itself after the test uiEnd() # prints SELFTEST OK, or the failures func selfTest oForm.Controls.Item(1).PerformClick() dnAssert(\"one click\",oCount.Text,\"1 clicks\") ring main.ring # the normal program ring main.ring --"}, {"u": "testing.html#typing-keys-in-tests", "t": "Testing", "h": "Typing keys in tests", "x": "Typing keys in tests # uiSendKeys(oForm,\"12{+}30{ENTER}\") types into the focused control of your own form . It posts window messages through the real Windows Forms key processing, so it behaves like a user and can never type into another program (which SendKeys could). Keys: {LEFT} {RIGHT} {UP} {DOWN} {ENTER} {ESC} {BACKSPACE} {TAB} {DELETE} , {+} for a plus sign."}, {"u": "testing.html#testing-the-screenshots", "t": "Testing", "h": "Testing the screenshots", "x": "Testing the screenshots # The screenshots in this documentation are made by docs/tools/shoot_all.sh : it starts each sample, finds its window, captures it with PrintWindow and closes it."}, {"u": "gotchas.html", "t": "Gotchas & limits", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "gotchas.html#ring-gotchas", "t": "Gotchas & limits", "h": "Ring gotchas", "x": "Ring gotchas # Use parentheses with new when you pass arguments # new StringBuilder(\"x\") \u2014 with parentheses, because Ring calls init() only then. A class without arguments works both ways ( new MemoryStream , new MemoryStream() ). For StringBuilder , Random and Version , which take an optional first argument, write new StringBuilder or new StringBuilder(\"x\") , not new StringBuilder() . Strings and numbers # \"text\" + number works. number + \"text\" is an error. Write string(n) + \" items\" . Do not call a method on a new object on the right of an attribute assignment # oForm.Text = new Path().GetFi"}, {"u": "gotchas.html#net-gotchas", "t": "Gotchas & limits", "h": ".NET gotchas", "x": ".NET gotchas # Put the using block before the first .NET call # The runtime mode (desktop, web, both) is chosen by the using { } block (or by dnUseDesktop() , dnUseWeb() , dnUseAll() ) and must come before any other .NET call : the first call starts the runtime and the mode can not change afterwards. If a program needs both Windows Forms/WPF and ASP.NET Core, name both in the first using block. Callbacks run on the Ring thread only # Events from timers on other threads ( System.Threading.Timer , Task.Run(...) ) can not run Ring code; they print a message on stderr and do nothing. Use the Windo"}, {"u": "gotchas.html#limits", "t": "Gotchas & limits", "h": "Limits", "x": "Limits # Not supported Why / what to do Declaring .NET classes or interfaces in Ring Ring is not compiled to .NET. Write them in C#, build a DLL and wrap it . .NET members named like some Ring words ( Get , And , Or , Not , Next , End , If , For , New , Load , Call , Put , Give , See ) Ring does not accept these after a dot (others, such as From , To , Set , Step , In , On , Off , are fine). A method gets a _ at the end ( Next_ , Call_ ); a property is read with getProp(\"Name\") and setProp(\"Name\", value) ; a method that kept its name is called with callMethod . Ring code on .NET threads One Ri"}, {"u": "gotchas.html#troubleshooting", "t": "Gotchas & limits", "h": "Troubleshooting", "x": "Troubleshooting # Message Cause Runtime Error in loading the dynamic library ring_dotnet.dll is not on the PATH. Add the package bin folder, or install the package into the Ring folder. DotNet Error: No generated classes for the namespace X the name is wrong, or the namespace is not in the manifest \u2014 see Class generator . Error in property name, property not found: x a global variable named like the attribute, or an object that is a plain DotNetObject (use getProp(\"x\") ). Calling function with less number of parameters a constructor that needs arguments: new DateTime(2024,12,25) . Trying to de"}, {"u": "reference.html", "t": "Function index", "h": "", "x": "This page is generated from the comments in libraries/dotnetlib/*.ring . Search it with the box at the top of the page."}, {"u": "reference.html#core-functionsring", "t": "Function index", "h": "Core (functions.ring)", "x": "Core (functions.ring) # Function Description dnRequest(cOperation, aParameters) Send a request to the bridge: dnRequest(\"call\", [oTarget, \"Method\", arg1, ...]), returns one value. The common answers (a text, a number, a yes/no, one object) are read directly, without making a list of items. dnFlush() The handles of the objects that Ring freed are released in groups (the next .NET call does it when 32 are waiting). dnFlush() releases them now. Ring frees an object a little after the last variable that held it is gone; callgc() (called in the same function) makes it free the objects at once. So t"}, {"u": "reference.html#dotnetobject-and-friends-classesring", "t": "Function index", "h": "DotNetObject and friends (classes.ring)", "x": "DotNetObject and friends (classes.ring) # Function Description DotNetObject.init(pHandle) (the parameter has another name: \"this.nHandle = ...\" would run Ring's property setter machinery, which breaks an outer assignment like oForm.Text = new Path().GetFileName(...)) DotNetObject.dnDefaultObject(cTypeName) The default object of a class (used by the class body of the generated classes, for \"new X\" without parentheses) DotNetObject.isNull() True for the null reference DotNetObject.callMethod(cMethod, aParameters) Call a method: oObject.callMethod(\"Append\", [\"text\"]) DotNetObject.getProp(cMember)"}, {"u": "reference.html#linq-linqring", "t": "Function index", "h": "LINQ (linq.ring)", "x": "LINQ (linq.ring) # Function Description dnQuery(xSource) DotNetQuery.init(xSource) DotNetQuery.Where(cCode) DotNetQuery.Select(cCode) DotNetQuery.SelectMany(cCode) DotNetQuery.OrderBy(cCode) DotNetQuery.OrderByDescending(cCode) DotNetQuery.ThenBy(cCode) DotNetQuery.ThenByDescending(cCode) DotNetQuery.TakeWhile(cCode) DotNetQuery.SkipWhile(cCode) DotNetQuery.Take(nCount) DotNetQuery.Skip(nCount) DotNetQuery.Distinct() DotNetQuery.Reverse() DotNetQuery.Concat(oOther) DotNetQuery.Union(oOther) DotNetQuery.Intersect(oOther) DotNetQuery.Except(oOther) DotNetQuery.ToList() DotNetQuery.Count() DotNet"}, {"u": "reference.html#windows-forms-helpers-guiring", "t": "Function index", "h": "Windows Forms helpers (gui.ring)", "x": "Windows Forms helpers (gui.ring) # Function Description uiForm(cTitle, nWidth, nHeight) A form with a client area of nWidth x nHeight, centered on the screen uiLabel(oParent, cText, nX, nY, nWidth, nHeight) uiButton(oParent, cText, nX, nY, nWidth, nHeight, cClickCode) A button; cClickCode is Ring code run on click (\"\" for none) uiTextBox(oParent, nX, nY, nWidth, nHeight) uiMemo(oParent, nX, nY, nWidth, nHeight) uiCheckBox(oParent, cText, nX, nY, nWidth, nHeight, cClickCode) uiListBox(oParent, nX, nY, nWidth, nHeight) uiComboBox(oParent, nX, nY, nWidth, aItems) uiPanel(oParent, nX, nY, nWidth, "}, {"u": "reference.html#wpf-helpers-wpfring", "t": "Function index", "h": "WPF helpers (wpf.ring)", "x": "WPF helpers (wpf.ring) # Function Description wpfWindow(cTitle, nWidth, nHeight) A window of nWidth x nHeight, centered on the screen wpfXaml(cXaml) Build the objects of a XAML text: wpfXaml('<Button xmlns=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation\" Content=\"Hi\"/>') wpfFind(oRoot, cName) The element called cName (x:Name / Name in XAML) inside oRoot wpfBrush(cColor) A brush from a name or a code: wpfBrush(\"Red\"), wpfBrush(\"#336699\") wpfColor(cColor) A color from a name or a code wpfLabel(cText) wpfText(cText) wpfButton(cText, cClickCode) A button; cClickCode is Ring code run whe"}, {"u": "reference.html#web-server-webring", "t": "Function index", "h": "Web server (web.ring)", "x": "Web server (web.ring) # Function Description webJson(xValue) The response of a handler as JSON: a Ring list, or a list of [name, value] pairs for a JSON object RingRequest.init(pHandle) RingRequest.method() RingRequest.path() RingRequest.queryString() RingRequest.query(cName) a query string value: /search?q=ring --> query(\"q\") RingRequest.body() RingRequest.header(cName) RingRequest.form(cName) RingRequest.setHeader(cName, cValue) RingRequest.context() the ASP.NET HttpContext for everything else RingRequest.respond(nStatus, cType, cBody) WebServer.init(cUrl) WebServer.url() the real address (w"}, {"u": "reference.html#entity-framework-dataring", "t": "Function index", "h": "Entity Framework (data.ring)", "x": "Entity Framework (data.ring) # Function Description dbValue(aRow, cColumn) The value of a column in a row: dbValue(aRow,\"Name\") dbWait(oTask) wait for a Task of the async methods and return its result RingDatabase.init(cConnection, aSchema) cConnection is a SQLite connection string (\"Data Source=people.db\") or starts with the provider: \"inmemory:shop\", \"sqlserver:Server=.;Database=x;...\", \"postgres:Host=...\", \"mysql:Server=...\" (those need the EF Core package of the provider next to RingDotNet.Data.dll) RingDatabase.insert(cTable, aValues) add a row, returns the key RingDatabase.byKey(cTable, "}, {"u": "reference.html#generated-classes", "t": "Function index", "h": "Generated classes", "x": "Generated classes # The wrapper classes are generated; there is one file per class in libraries/dotnetlib/generated/<Namespace>/ . The members of a class are the members of the .NET class (see the .NET documentation ), named by the rules . To see the Ring name of a method, open its class file or call dnDescribe(\"Namespace.Type\") ."}, {"u": "ring-intro.html", "t": "Quick introduction to Ring", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "ring-intro.html#who-this-page-is-for", "t": "Quick introduction to Ring", "h": "Who this page is for", "x": "Who this page is for # This is an appendix: the rest of the documentation does not depend on reading it in order. If you already write Ring, go back to Getting started . If you come from C#, Python, JavaScript or another language, read this page once: Ring differs from some languages in a few ways, and knowing them helps when you read Ring code and the messages that come from .NET. The five programs are in docs/tutorials ( r01_ring_basics.ring \u2026 r05_ring_errors_files.ring ); run one with: ring r01_ring_basics.ring Note This is a quick tour, not the manual. The Ring language documentation (<htt"}, {"u": "ring-intro.html#1-values-text-and-numbers", "t": "Quick introduction to Ring", "h": "1. Values, text and numbers", "x": "1. Values, text and numbers # # Ring in 15 minutes, part 1: values, text, numbers and comparisons (no .NET here: plain Ring) # 1. A variable needs no declaration and no type; the type belongs to the value. \"?\" prints a line. nCount = 3 # a number cName = \"Ring\" # a text lReady = True # a yes/no value: True is 1, False is 0 ? nCount ? cName ? lReady ? type(nCount) + \" \" + type(cName) + \" \" + type(lReady) # 2. All numbers are doubles. Whole numbers print without decimals, others with at most a few. ? 7 / 2 ? 7 % 2 # the remainder ? floor(7 / 2) # the whole part ? 2 ** 10 # power (\"^\" is a bit op"}, {"u": "ring-intro.html#2-lists", "t": "Quick introduction to Ring", "h": "2. Lists", "x": "2. Lists # # Ring in 15 minutes, part 2: lists (the only collection), pairs, nested lists # 1. A list holds anything. Indexes start at 1. \"+\" adds an item at the end. aNames = [\"Ann\",\"Bob\",\"Cy\"] aNames + \"Dee\" ? len(aNames) ? aNames[1] + \" \" + aNames[len(aNames)] aNames[2] = \"Rob\" # change an item ? aNames # 2. Walk a list with for ... in. The item is a copy: change the list by index. for cName in aNames ? \"hello \" + cName next for nIndex = 1 to len(aNames) aNames[nIndex] = upper(aNames[nIndex]) next ? aNames # 3. Search, insert, delete, sort ? find(aNames,\"CY\") # the position (0: not found) i"}, {"u": "ring-intro.html#3-decisions-loops-functions", "t": "Quick introduction to Ring", "h": "3. Decisions, loops, functions", "x": "3. Decisions, loops, functions # # Ring in 15 minutes, part 3: decisions, loops and functions # Ring runs a file from the top. If the file has a function called main, Ring calls it by itself when it reaches the end of the file. # The code at the top level (before the first func) runs first. A func continues until the next func or class, so statements go BEFORE the functions. nTotal = 0 # a global variable: defined at the top, outside every function func main # 1. if / but (else if) / else, closed with ok nAge = 17 if nAge >= 18 ? \"adult\" but nAge >= 13 ? \"teenager\" else ? \"child\" ok # 2. for c"}, {"u": "ring-intro.html#4-classes-and-objects", "t": "Quick introduction to Ring", "h": "4. Classes and objects", "x": "4. Classes and objects # # Ring in 15 minutes, part 4: classes and objects (the code at the top comes first, the classes go LAST: a func or class continues until the next func or class) func main # 1. new makes an object. The parentheses call init(). oAnn = new Person(\"Ann\",31) ? oAnn.name oAnn.birthday() ? oAnn.describe() # 2. Attributes are read and written with a dot. An attribute that the class does not have is an error. oAnn.age = 40 ? oAnn.age # 3. Braces: set attributes (and call methods) of the object inside { }. This is the style of this documentation. oBob = new Person(\"Bob\",25) { ci"}, {"u": "ring-intro.html#5-errors-files-and-other-files", "t": "Quick introduction to Ring", "h": "5. Errors, files and other files", "x": "5. Errors, files and other files # # Ring in 15 minutes, part 5: errors, files, arguments and other files func main # 1. try / catch / done: the error text is in cCatchError. raise() makes an error of your own. try nResult = 10 / 0 catch ? \"caught: \" + substr(cCatchError,14) # a runtime error starts with \"Error (R1) : \", then the text done try checkAge(-5) catch ? \"caught: \" + cCatchError done checkAge(30) ? \"30 is fine\" # 2. Files: write() and read() work with the whole text of a file; fexists() asks if it exists cFile = \"ring_intro_demo.txt\" write(cFile,\"line one\" + nl + \"line two\" + nl) ? f"}, {"u": "ring-intro.html#ring-and-other-languages", "t": "Quick introduction to Ring", "h": "Ring and other languages", "x": "Ring and other languages # Some differences from other popular languages, as facts: Often in other languages In Ring declarations, types none: the value has the type == to compare = (the context decides); != for different arrays from 0 lists from 1 integers and decimals one number type, a double \"a\" + 1 and 1 + \"a\" the same the left side decides methods overloaded by parameters one function per name, with a fixed number of parameters { } blocks words in the first style ( ok , next , end , off , done ); braces are allowed too string is characters text is bytes (UTF-8) references to objects obje"}, {"u": "ring-intro.html#what-comes-next", "t": "Quick introduction to Ring", "h": "What comes next", "x": "What comes next # A Ring program uses .NET with two lines: load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text\" } oBuilder = new StringBuilder(\"Hello\") ? oBuilder.Append(\", Ring!\").ToString() Continue with Getting started , then Core concepts (how .NET names, overloads, properties and enums look in Ring) and the tutorials . The points of Ring that matter when you use .NET are collected in Gotchas & limits ."}];